  {
    "title": "Easton forfeits boys' game to Cambridge-SD",
    "date": "2024-01-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton forfeits boys' game to Cambridge-SD\n\n January 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 721\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Easton High's boys' basketball team has forfeited its game Thursday at Cambridge-South Dorchester.Thursday's girls' game between the two schools will be played as scheduled at Easton High at 4 p.m., but spectators will not be allowed to attend.\nThe decision to forfeit the boys' game and play the girls' game without spectators is largely connected to the Oct. 27 football game between the two schools at Easton High that was stopped with just under 7 minutes remaining in the third quarter because of altercations away from the playing field.\nAt that game, the Easton and Cambridge-SD coaching staffs led their teams off the field while fans were instructed to leave the stadium by school administrators and local law enforcement officers. Three teenagers were arrested following the altercations.\nThe pattern that is emerging — basketball games scheduled between the two schools at Easton High this season will be played without fans; basketball games slated for Cambridge-SD will be forfeited by Easton High. The issue came down to playing the game with limited spectators. Easton wanted to limit or play without spectators. Cambridge-SD wanted spectators allowed.\nThis past Sunday, Easton High announced on its Instagram page, \"Due to events that occurred during the EHS vs. CSD football game in the Fall, we have decided not to allow spectators at our upcoming girls and boys basketball game against CSD at Easton High School. As always, safety is a top priority. We apologize for any inconvenience and thank you for your support and understanding.\"\nOn Tuesday, the administrative offices of Cambridge-South Dorchester posted a statement on its Facebook page announcing Easton's decision to forfeit Thursday's boys' game at Cambridge-SD, and the Feb. 15 girls' game slated at Cambridge-SD.\n\"It is with great disappointment that we must convey this news: Easton High School has decided to forfeit the upcoming girls' and boys' basketball games scheduled to be held at Cambridge South Dorchester High School on Jan. 11 and Feb. 15,\" the statement said. \"This decision was made by Easton High School and was not influenced by Cambridge-South Dorchester High in any way.\"\nThe post went on to say, \"As a result of Easton High School's prearranged decision to forfeit these games, Cambridge South Dorchester High School will be credited with the wins for both of the boys' and girls' teams. We regret that parents, spectators and community members will not have the opportunity to witness these anticipated games.\n\"While we extend our best wishes to Easton High School, it is essential to emphasize that safety remains our top priority. We too understand the importance of maintaining a safe and secure environment for all involved.\nCambridge-SD High School was closed Wednesday due to weather conditions. Phone calls to the office of Cambridge-SD athletic director Casey O'Bier went unanswered.\nOn Wednesday, Talbot County Public Schools released a second statement in an email, saying that following the football game, administrators talked with coaches, athletes and parents about safety at future events.\n\"TCPS made the difficult decision not to allow spectators at upcoming home basketball events against CSD,\" the statement read.\nHome games against Cambridge-SD will be livestreamed.\nThe statement continued that Talbot County school administrators asked that Cambridge-SD limit spectators at their home games.\n\"They chose not to,\" according to the Talbot County schools statement.\n\"Because the safety of our students and staff is always our highest priority, we have decided not to travel to CSD for the boys' and girls' basketball games on Jan. 11 and Feb. 15, thereby forfeiting those games,\" the statement read. \"We value our relationship with our Dorchester County neighbors and believe this decision is in the best interests of all of the athletes involved.\"\nThe Easton and Cambridge-SD rivalry is one of the Bayside Conference's richest, especially in football and boys' basketball.\nEntering Thursday's boys contest, Cambridge-SD (7-0 overall) was sitting atop the North Bayside with a 6-0 record and bidding for its first division title since 2020. Following the Vikings are second-place Queen Anne's (6-1) and Easton (5-1). Thursday's forfeit moves the Vikings to 8-0 overall and 7-0 in the division, while Easton slips to 6-4 overall, 5-2 division.\nThe boys' game at Easton is scheduled for Feb. 15 and will not have spectators.\nCambridge-SD's girls enter today's game at 5-2 overall, 4-2 North, trailing division leaders Queen Anne's (7-0, 6-0) and Kent Island (6-1, 6-0). Easton's girls are currently 3-6, 3-3.\nStar Democrat Reporter Natalie Jones contributed to this article. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 1,
    "day": 10,
