  {
    "title": "Buccaneers fall to Decatur, bounce back against Tech",
    "date": "2024-02-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Buccaneers fall to Decatur, bounce back against Tech\n\n February 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1052\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — The streak — which unofficially reached 61 consecutive victories against Bayside Conference opponents Wednesday night — is not the overwhelming driving force.No, what really pushes Stephen Decatur High's wrestling team is the idea it won't be good enough after graduating its state champions and finishers from the previous year.\n\"The kids know we haven't lost a Bayside match in awhile,\" Seahawks head coach Josh August said. \"But I feel like each year, we have like really good guys, state champs that graduated and go off to college, and then the guys that are left feel like they have something to prove. Because when you have a state champ, or two state champs leave, (people) think that you are not going to be as good. I think our guys use that to motivate themselves, keep on carrying on with the wins and making states.\"\nDecatur kept carrying on Wednesday night, routing non-conference foe Harford Vo-Tech, 61-9, before defeating Kent Island, 44-23, to extend its Bayside dominance while remaining undefeated heading Friday's showdown with unbeaten Rising Sun.\n\"I thought we wrestled really good against Harford Tech,\" said August, after the Seahawks improved to 13-0 overall, 10-0 Bayside. \"Some of our guys stepped up big, went up a couple weights for the Kent Island match. Not a lot of guys do that. We got guys wanting to wrestle big matches. Overall, I think the team is clicking pretty good.\"\nOverall, Kent Island head coach Tommy Sitar was happy with how his team battled against Decatur, which routed the Buccaneers, 60-18 at the Iron Duals in early January.\n\"We won only three matches against them,\" Sitar said of the Iron Horse dual against Decatur. \"(Tonight) we won six matches, doubled our output, which is nice.\"\nPerhaps the nicest part of the Decatur match for Kent Island was after losing the first three weight classes to Juan Hinojosa (113), Liam Hugues (120) and Reid Caimi (126) by pin to fall behind 18-0, the Bucs (10-3, 8-1) won the next four weights to pull within 18-14.\n\"They definitely looked strong,\" Sitar said of Silas Brobst (132), Greg Couch (138), Cody Sandefer (144) and Ashton Main (150). \"I was impressed with the way they were able to dictate to the other wrestlers what was going to happen.\"\nBrobst start the mini-rally when he went into overtime tied 1-1 with Aaron August then scored a takedown with 26 seconds left in the 1-minute overtime for a 3-1 sudden victory.\n\"Silas wrestled his best match definitely on his feet,\" Sitar said. \"The way he was moving and setting him up was awesome.\"\nKent Island's next three wrestlers would each score the first takedown in their respective matches. Couch scored a late takedown for a 9-1 major decision over Jake Saullo. Sandefer built a 10-0 lead over two periods en route to a 12-3 major over Alex Riehl. Main capped the run with a 10-3 decision over Braydon Bell at 150.\nDespite having its lead trimmed to four, Coach August was pleased with how his team wrestled through the middleweights.\n\"The score looked closer than the last time we went against Kent Island,\" Josh August said. \"They're a solid team every year. We came away with the victory. It was a little close in the middle, but they are strong right there. One of our things was our guys weren't getting pinned. Our guys that were outmatched were hanging in there, wrestling the full six minutes.\"\nThe Seahawks seized full control from there, packaging three pinfalls and two major decisions over the next five bouts for a 44-23 lead.\nDecatur's Amarian Manuel earned a 16-8 major over Brandon Adler at 157. Parker Intrieri pinned Chris Kougoures in 47 seconds at 165. Gavin Solito — wrestling up from 157 — put together a 9-1 major over Giuseppe Mellinger at 175. Kole Kohut (190) pinned Brian Potter in 1:49, and Nate McDaniel pinned Nick Morey with 28 seconds remaining in the 215-pound match.\n\"I think it's an expectation by them,\" Josh August said of his team's mindset. \"It's an expectation that they wrestle to the level to get there, not that they are just going to get there.\"\nKent Island came back to win the last two bouts, Greg Coffey winning a 4-2 decision over Price Hill at 285, and Gavin Lindner pinning Jaxon Archer in 3:08 at 106.\nThe 21-point difference was the closest any Bayside team has come to Decatur this season.\nThe Bucs fell behind 7-0 to Harford Vo Tech, but then regrouped to win 10 of the remaining 12 bouts, which included pinfalls from Couch (138), Adler (157), Kougoures (166), Potter (190), Morey (215), Coffey (285) and Lindner (106).\n\"I talked to the guys after the Decatur match 'cause it appeared we started at a weight (120) where had kind of a letdown,\" Sitar said. \"I let them know, 'We're peaking. We haven't peaked. Decatur was not the peak. We don't stop now just because we wrestled a team tough. We keep on peaking.\"\nWedged in the Bucs' rally was Sitar's decision to insert Potter, a 175-pound backup, in at 190 and bump Morey from 190 to 215.\n\"At the end of the match we had a situation where we were going to be (potentially) down 15 with five bouts left, or we could be down potentially 15 with three bouts left,\" Sitar said. \"So I opted with the strategy of going down 15 with three bouts, and bumped our 190 (Morey) to 215, where I was 100 percent sure he could get a pinfall. And our backup 175-pounder, Brian Potter ended up getting a pin over Jack Ryder of Harford Tech, who I guess was ranked in the state.\"\nBrobst won a 13-3 major decision at 132 and Sandefer got a 7-3 decision at 144 to help the Bucs take a 13-7 lead. Tech's Ryan Sewell pinned Main at 150 to tie the match at 13-all before Kent Island won the next seven straight matches, which included a 6-2 decision by Mellinger at 175.\n\"It's nice to immediately erase it from the forefront,\" Sitar said of coming back and beating Harford Tech after the Decatur loss.\nNotes: Kent Island's three losses this season have come against Decatur, which is bidding for a fifth consecutive state dual-meet title — excluding the COVID-19 canceled 2021 season — two-time defending 1A state dual-meet champ and unbeaten South Carroll, and Class 4A Broadneck (9-3). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 1,
    "metadata_sport": "wrestling",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Decatur Seahawks\", \"Kent Island Buccaneers\", \"Harford Vo-Tech\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Josh August\", \"head coach Tommy Sitar\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Decatur 44, Kent Island 23; Kent Island 61, Harford Vo-Tech 9",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Griffin, Sabres stay in playoff hunt",
    "date": "2024-02-03",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Griffin, Sabres stay in playoff hunt\n\n February 3, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 848\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Brennen Griffin found an isolated, off-ice spot and went through his motions.Resting on his bulky leg pads, Saints Peter and Paul High's freshman goalie stared at the white board in front of him while extending his arms out to his sides. Moving like the second hand on a clock, Griffin steadily clicked his left arm upward, then brought his right arm up, up, and up.\n\"It's my pregame ritual basically,\" Griffin said. \"Every game, come into the rink, get dressed and find a quiet spot. And it's just for myself. It's time for me to focus. Time for me to lock myself in mentally.\"\nThe 6-foot-3 Griffin looked plenty locked in Friday, stopping 32 of the 33 shots he faced while helping Saints Peter and Paul to a huge 4-1 victory Loyola-Blakefield on senior night in a Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association B Conference game at the Talbot County Community Center.\n\"The door is still open,\" Sabres head coach Sam Gerdano said of Sts. Peter and Paul's chances at qualifying for the fourth and final spot in the MIAA B playoffs.\nThe Sabres (3-6 overall, 3-5 conference, 6 points) ended a four-game skid and remained two points behind St. Paul's (4-4, 8 points) for the fourth postseason spot. Boys Latin (8-1, 16), Loyola-Blakefield (6-2, 12) and Gilman (4-5, 8) currently hold down the top three spots.\n\"They really wanted it tonight,\" said Gerdano, whose team was shutout 5-0 by Loyola on Dec. 6. \"They knew it was going to be a hard game. But we talked about, 'This is our building. Nobody outworks us in this building.' And we didn't let that happen today.\"\nOutscored 22-4 in its previous four games, the Sabres took a 1-0 lead 1:40 into the game, when senior captain Robbie O'Connor took a pass from Griffin Fritzsche, got behind a pair of defenders, and tucked a shot past Dons goalie Tommy Bunte.\n\"I think that was really important because we scored like a minute or two in, and that really set the energy for the entire team,\" O'Connor said. \"We just kept that the whole way.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul also kept scoring.\nDaniel Schwaninger set up Fritzsche's goal with 3:44 remaining in the first period for a 2-0 lead. Loyola killed off a Sabres power play later in the first, but with 36 seconds left on the clock, Schwaninger scored unassisted for a three-goal cushion.\n\"We had a pretty decent first period when we played this team last, but we weren't able to score any goals on 'em,\" Gerdano said. \"Today we got a few past them and then it helped our confidence.\n\"But the second period is what I was more impressed with because while we didn't score, that period has really hurt us this season, especially against this team,\" Gerdano added. \"I think they scored four goals to last second period we played 'em. So I was really concerned about that, but they played really well.\"\nAgain, the Sabres struck quickly, as Jackson Steppe scored just 1:24 into the second for a 4-0 lead.\n\"Everyone just bought in,\" O'Connor said. \"Everyone put full effort the entire game. It was just great. Everyone was ready to play. Everyone was physical. They were hustling out there. We just made the plays at the right time.\"\nGriffin and defensemen Tyler Watson, Schwaninger, Gavin Corley and Zack Werner also made plays at the right time, as the Sabres' killed off all five Loyola power-play chances, including a two-man advantage midway through the third period.\n\"It was impressive,\" Gerdano said of his team's penalty kill. \"They picked their heads up. They found a lane to get it down the ice, blocked some shots. And Brennen played unbelievable. \"\nLoyola foiled Griffin's shutout bid with 1:09 remaining in the second period, when Ryder McClure scored off a feed from Josh Goloboski.\nBut Griffin and company blanked the Dons, who had scored 12 goals in their previous two games, the rest of the way.\n\"Goalie needs to be your best penalty killer, and Brennen was that, and even more than that today,\" Gerdano said. \"He's obviously a big goalie. Takes up a lot of the net. He's got a lot of skill to that as well. He's all over the place, making big stops for us. I think he was frustrating the other team for sure. That team is used to scoring goals and they had a really hard time because of our goalie, and just our defense in front of him, keeping the shots from the outside.\"\nAnd while Griffin impressed, the young goalie redirected praise toward those fronting him as if he were steering away one of Loyola's many shot on net.\n\"My defense was making sure I saw everything; weren't giving up too many good opportunities in front. That's how we got to 4-1,\" Griffin said. \"That's the best game I've seen them play. It was unreal what they were doing. Just when I thought we were going to let one up, somebody was sliding in blocking a shot. It was something special.\"\n\"Best experience I've ever been apart of,\" Griffin added. \"Especially on senior night, doing this for the seniors is unreal.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 3,
    "metadata_sport": "hockey",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Sts. Peter and Paul Sabres\", \"Loyola-Blakefield Dons\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Brennen Griffin\", \"head coach Sam Gerdano\", \"captain Robbie O'Connor\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Sts. Peter and Paul 4, Loyola-Blakefield 1",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "RISING TO THE OCCASION",
    "date": "2024-02-06",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "RISING TO THE OCCASION\n\n February 6, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1290\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n LANDOVER — Kent Island High's girls team was a strong contender to win Monday's Class 2A East Region indoor track and field championship.Bucs' longtime head coach Justin Holland felt even stronger about those title chances after Gabi Bernhard won the 500 meters and 55-meter hurdles less than 30 minutes apart. The top seed in the pole vault, Holland figured all Bernhard needed to do was clear her opening height of 9 feet, 6 inches and the team championship was a near lock.\n\"She's cleared 9-6 all year,\" Holland said. \"In fact, we were about to start at 10 feet from now on. But it was an easy meet, so she decided she was going to come in at 9-6, like she's done all year.\"\nLess than 5 minutes after winning the hurdles, Bernhard missed on the first of her three attempts at 9-6. Then she missed again.\n\"So I'm thinking, 'Oh my gosh. If she scratches this last one,\" Holland said.\nShe didn't.\nBernhard made her third and final attempt at 9-6, hugged her coach, then went on to clear 10-6 to win her third individual title while helping the Bucs seal the team crown at the Prince George's Sports and Learning Complex.\n\"I talked to her about how nervous (I was),\" Holland said of his post-victory chat with his three-time champion. \"That's the most (nervous) I've been in forever. I said, 'Don't do that to me ever again.' And I'm like, I'm just going to have to deal with a crying Gabi (if she missed on her third try). And she's like, 'Oh, I wasn't going to cry.' And I said, 'Yeah, but then I was going to let you know that probably cost us the regional championship as well. And she's like, 'Oh, yeah I would have cried as soon as you said that.' But I didn't say that was what was riding (on it).\"\nBernhard's victory, coupled with teammates' Siena Koch (7-6) and Nora Koch (6-6) finishing second and third, respectively, gave Kent Island a 24-point haul in the event, and more than sealed the title, as the Bucs totaled 69 points to outdistance runner-up Parkside (57), third-place Patuxent (52) and fourth-place North Caroline (43).\nBernhard's three titles highlighted an impressive day for North Bayside athletes, who won six other championships.\nQueen Anne's County junior Ben Marks routed the field to win the boys 800- and 1,600-meter titles. Easton senior Jed Smith teamed with Colin Smith, Jacob Camper and Sy Orndoff to win the boys 4x800 relay, then pulled away over the final laps to win the 3,200. Warrior teammate Sumayah Wilkins won the girls' high jump. And North Caroline's Morgan Knott, Linda Ridgell, Brooke Sullivan and Seanna Rupp took first in the girls' 4x800 relay.\n\"I think everybody stepped it up and did their part,\" Holland said of his girls team. \"We do our team chemistry and it works out. They worked hard and I'm proud of all of them. And obviously I think Gabi helped us out quite a bit.\"\nBut Bernhard didn't exactly have a whole lot of time between events.\nAfter qualifying in the preliminaries for the 55 hurdles, Bernhard had a little wait before doing a couple practice vaults. Then her schedule began to accelerate. She won the 500 in 1:22.94, as Easton's Emily Branic (2nd, 1:23.13) and Kent Island teammate Reese Heyliger (3rd, 1:23.28) chased her across the finish line.\nAfter a few more run-throughs in the vault, Bernhard was back on the track, outdueling Patuxent's Jordan Estrella in the 55 hurdles, clocking a first-place 9.08 to Estrella's 9.12.\n\"The goal for today was to come out, do my best,\" Bernhard said. \"Take it one event at a time. I did want to break 9 seconds in the hurdles, but I came really close. My (personal) record is 8.88. I'm going for that for states. And I'm happy to have cleared 10-6 in pole vault. I was a little worried there at 9-6.\"\nPart of that can be attributed to Bernhard just coming off her victory in the hurdles. With only four competitors in the pole vault, the height was raised quickly. Bernhard hit the bar on the way up on her first try, then brushed it off on the second. After conferencing with Holland, she easily cleared her opening height.\n\"I was like 'Don't worry about it,'\" Bernhard said. \"'Don't get stressed.' Settle yourself. Just know that you're going to do your best. If you don't worry about it you're guaranteed to do your best.\"\nBernhard also was a member of the Bucs' third-place 4x200 relay, joining Heyliger, Kendra Coffey and Siena Koch for a 1:51.91.\nMarks left little doubt who the winner of the 1,600 meters was going to be, as he broke from the field on the first backstretch and opened a commanding lead en route to a first-place 4:22.79 — 24 seconds ahead of runner-up Trevor Thompson of La Plata.\n\"I didn't want to get boxed because that would have totally messed up my race,\" said Marks, who finished 3 seconds off his personal-best. \"So I just wanted to get free from any barriers in front of me. I went out in 29-, 30-(second first lap). Honestly it felt really smooth. That was good.\n\"It was a solo effort today so it's a little different than what I've been used to,\" Marks said of his dominating performance in the 1,600. \"I knew there were some boys who maybe wanted to stay with me for a couple of laps. \"But I wanted to take it out (fast) and leave everybody in the dust.\"\nThough the pursuit was closer, and his margin of victory not as great, Marks wasn't really pushed in the 800, rolling to a first-place mark of 1:58.43, followed by La Plata's Kohl Girley (2:01.91), and Easton's Colin Smith (2:06.03) and Jed Smith (2:06.29).\n\"I thought there was going to be a little bit more of a push,\" Marks said of the 800. \"I took the lead right away so I really didn't see a single runner the entire race. When I got to the front I was able to keep running and (didn't) look behind me. I just stayed focused. Got to just run my race.\"\nJed Smith battled Huntingtown's Brendan Bischof through the majority of the 3,200. But the Easton senior steadily began a surge over the final laps that would not be answered, winning in 10:27.84 — 7 seconds ahead of Bishof.\n\"I felt strong throughout the race,\" Smith said. \"I was pacing my own game, kind of looking at the clock. I kind of felt like he went out a little too quick so I used that to my advantage, and was smart and stayed back. Once he started to slow down a bit I made my move on the straight, and then I think he got (close) again at the end. But I knew I had enough in me for that final push.\"\nSmith anchored Easton's winning 4x800 relay, which won in 8:30.24, well ahead of second-place Westlake (8:41.77).\nCaroline's Knott, Ridgell, Sullivan and Rupp opened the meet with one of the day's more decisive victories, rolling away with the 4x800 relay in 10:34.36, easily outdistancing runner-up La Plata (11:15.09).\nEaston head coach Beth Heinsohn, who was left in tears after the Warriors' convincing win in the boys 4x800 relay, was again in tears, when Wilkins angled over the bar at 5-3 to win the high jump.\n\"I was just thinking to myself, I've got so much confidence,\" Wilkins said. \"I was like, 'I'm going to clear it.' I was pushing for 5-4. But 5-3. I'll take it. I'm just so proud of myself I cried. And my coach cried as well.\"\nNotes: La Plata won the boys team title with 96 points, edging Westlake by one point. Huntingtown was third followed by Easton (44) and Kent Island (32). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 6,
    "metadata_sport": "track and field",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Landover, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island High School\", \"Parkside High School\", \"Patuxent High School\", \"Easton High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Justin Holland\", \"Gabi Bernhard\", \"Ben Marks\", \"Jed Smith\", \"Sumayah Wilkins\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "championship",
    "metadata_outcome": "Kent Island High School wins Class 2A East Region indoor track and field championship",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Moody's 24 points lead Cambridge-SD",
    "date": "2024-02-08",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Moody's 24 points lead Cambridge-SD\n\n February 8, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: News \n \n 34\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — LaShaun Moody scored 24 points Thursday to lead Cambridge-South Dorchester High's boys basketball team to an 86-27 victory over Washington.Sterling Perkins and Koby Ennals each had 23 points for the Vikings. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 8,
    "metadata_sport": "basketball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Cambridge, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Cambridge-South Dorchester High\", \"Washington\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Cambridge-SD 86, Washington 27",
    "metadata_importance": 6
  },
  {
    "title": "CHANGING THEIR TUNE",
    "date": "2024-02-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "CHANGING THEIR TUNE\n\n February 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 851\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n WYE MILLS — The talk surrounding St. Michaels High's boys basketball team used to be could it win just one game.That narrative has changed drastically this season. Now, as they enter the second week of February, the Saints are asking themselves can they earn a home-court playoff game?\nSt. Michaels kept that scenario alive Saturday afternoon, as senior Nakhi Miller scored a game-high 34 points and sophomore Johnny Mautz had 20, pacing the Saints to a 79-47 rout of Mardela in the boys opener of the Peake Challenge at Chesapeake College.\n\"I told them these last few games, 'No, we can't win the (North) Bayside, but you can set yourselves up to have a home game as far as the seeding goes for the playoffs,'\" St. Michaels second-year head coach Boo Ricketts said. \"We're not just playing to beat teams. We're playing to make progress and put ourselves in a good position as a team. This is all about the Saints.\"\nWhile Perryville (12-3) and Colonel Richardson (11-7) hold the top two seeds in Class 1A East Region I, St. Michaels (6-11 overall, 4-10 North Bayside) stayed within reach of No. 3 Bohemia Manor (8-9) and just ahead of Kent County (5-11) in the region field.\n\"We really didn't lose any pieces from last season,\" Mautz said. \"Just been working and it's starting to come together. I mean I think we should have more than six wins to be honest.\"\nMautz could certainly point toward a four-point loss to North Caroline on Dec. 12, or Thursday's loss on senior night against Gunston.\n\"As I tell my guys, 'We're not playing against teams this year,'\" Ricketts said. \"'We're playing against us. It's all about growing. It's all about progress.' They always ask me, 'Is this team good?' I said, 'We're not assessing teams. Every team is good. Let's show up and play our brand of basketball.'\"\nThe Saints did that against Mardela (1-16), as Miller and Mautz established an early trend of attacking the rim, while helping St. Michaels build a 21-6 lead after the first quarter.\n\"This game we definitely made up for it,\" said Miller, referring to the Gunston loss. \"We just have to become better. Go to practice and give 100 percent every day.\"\nMardela actually took a 2-0 lead before the game-clock even started because St. Michaels' Hunter Gottlieb drew a technical for dunking during pre-game warm-ups.\n\"No coach wants to start the game that way,\" Ricketts said. \"So I pulled Hunter in our huddle and I said, 'If you want to dunk the ball make sure you get one in the game.\"\nGottlieb did just that with under 2 minutes remaining in the first half, when he got a breakaway and hammered a one-handed slam that brought the crowd to its highest volume of the opening 16 minutes and gave the Saints a 33-15 lead.\n\"I guess he made me eat my words,\" Ricketts said laughing.\nUp 39-18 at halftime, Miller and Mautz continued driving to the basket in the second half. Mautz drilled one of his two 3-pointers less than 3 minutes into the third quarter, extending the Saints' cushion to 45-22. Mardela's Wyatt Livingstone answered with a trey to get the Warriors back within 20, But St. Michaels closed the third on a 16-6 run, with Miller accounting for 13 in the rally.\n\"It was just a team effort,\" Miller said.\nA theme the senior guard has undoubtedly picked up from his head coach this season.\n\"I tell them every time we win games it's a collective effort,\" Ricketts said. \"In the games we've won there's been no one man or two men that are showing up and doing it by themselves. The games we win is when they play together and everybody believes in it. Even our bench. That's what a basketball team is.\"\nThe Saints led 61-31 heading into the fourth quarter and watched Mautz reel off seven consecutive points, widening the lead to 68-33. The Warriors put together a 9-0 run to pull within 68-42, but wouldn't get any closer as Ricketts sat his starters late.\n\"I feel we can always be better,\" Mautz said. \"There's always levels to go up, but I'm decently happy about where we're at right now. Play hard it practice, start to knock down our free throws, there's really no reason why we shouldn't make a run in the playoffs this year.\"\nChristian Elsey led Mardela with 15 points.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 82, WICOMICO 64\nWYE MILLS — Amari Baynard scored 18 points and Collin Woolford added 16 as the Lions rolled in the second game of the Peake Challenge.\nNickai Gross finished with 15 points and Vince Gilberto netted 14.\nSSPP 66, CRISTO REY 53\nEASTON — Garett Hemingway's 34 points fueled the Sabres in Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association C Conference play Friday.\nCAMBRIDGE-SD 73, ARCADIA 44\nARCADIA, VA. — LaShaun Moody had 31 points to pace the Vikings. Koby Ennals had 16 for Cambridge-South Dorchester and Sterling Perkins had 10.\nJordan Cropper led Arcadia with 13 points.\nGIRLS BASKETBALL\nCAMBRIDGE-SD 56, ARCADIA 13\nARCADIA, VA. — LeAsia Todd finished with 15 points and 20 rebounds Friday, leading the Vikings.\nDamiya Henry scored 16 points to go with seven steals for Cambridge-SD. Tejanae Ballin had 14 points. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
