  {
    "title": "Bucs ride post play, down NC",
    "date": "2024-02-28",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bucs ride post play, down NC\n\n February 28, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 691\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — There have been seasons when Kent Island High's girls basketball team has beaten opponents from long distance.That hasn't been the Bucs' most common thread to victory this season.\nThe one-two inside scoring punch of Sydney Riska and Makayla Kropfelder combined for 27 points, and Kent Island went on a 13-0 first-half scoring run Wednesday night to gain separation in an eventual 46-37 victory over North Caroline in a Class 2A East Region II quarterfinal.\nThe defending region champion, fourth-seeded Kent Island (18-3) advances to Friday's semifinal at top-seeded Wicomico (21-0). Second-seeded Queen Anne's (19-2) hosts No. 3 Parkside (18-3) in Friday's other semifinal.\n\"We used to be a three-point team and now we're a post team,\" Bucs head coach David Plumer said. \"It's no secret. Everybody who watches film on us will know. But the post-play is getting better. Makayla and Sydney did great tonight. They can complement everybody else. We got the ball inside. We're a little bigger than them and we knew that.\"\nNorth Caroline (14-7), which came in having won nine of its last 11, took a 6-4 lead a little over 3 minutes into the game, when Chloe Escoe (team-high 14 points) hit the second of her four 3-pointers. But Riska (game-high 17 points) scored on a put-back with 3:09 left in the first quarter to start the Buccaneers on a decisive 13-0 streak.\n\"We started off moving the ball early,\" North Caroline head coach A.J. Jackson said. \"We had some good offensive possessions, but then we had a drought there where we got sort of undisciplined tonight, and that ended up hurting us later in the game.\"\nThe Bulldogs went 9 minutes without a field goal before Ashley Kercheval got behind the defense for a layup with 3:49 left in the first half, cutting Kent Island's lead to 20-8. Riska hit a pair of baskets during the run and Kropfelder (10 points) made a three-point play.\n\"We knew this was going to be a post-play game,\" said Riska, who finished with 13 rebounds for her 17th double-double of the season. \"So just getting the ball down to me and Makayla, and then just getting the shots up. Obviously we didn't do well from the three-point line, but we did work well in the paint. The outside shots weren't falling for me but I worked inside to get it done.\"\nAs did Kropfelder.\n\"Just trying to make the best plays for my team,\" the sophomore center said. \"If I'm not there, if there's too many defenders, pass it. I missed a couple opportunities but I just need to work on that personally.\"\nLacy Dauses hit a cutting Riska for a layup and a 23-10 halftime lead. Riska and Kropfelder then teamed to score all the points in a 6-0 burst to open the third quarter, expanding Kent Island's lead to 29-10 less than 3 minutes into the period.\nBut the Bulldogs outscored the Bucs, 14-4, over the remaining 4:52 of the third quarter to pull within 33-24 entering the fourth quarter.\nKent Island regained its double-digit lead with Lilli Dauses converting a pair of free throws and Kropfelder scoring off a Riska assist for a 37-24 cushion.\n\"Credit to their bigs,\" Jackson said. \"Second-chance shots, they really battled down there. And the effort on defense, especially when we made a little run there in the fourth quarter. Like hustling, not giving up. That team is well coached. Credit to Plumer.\"\nNorth Caroline again cut its deficit to under 10 with 5 minutes remaining when Escoe drilled another 3-pointer and Malayah Phillips scored on a layup to make it 37-29. But Kent Island twice worked the ball inside to Riska, who was fouled and hit four consecutive free throws to make it 41-29 midway through the final period.\nThe Bulldogs wouldn't get any closer than eight the remainder of the game, as their small runs were countered by the Bucs.\n\"It's always been a little bit of a dogfight,\" Kent Island guard Caroline Cavanaugh said. \"We go in there, we get a lead, they come back. We just need to be a little more consistent with our defense and our offense. They played really well. We just played better this game.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 28,
    "metadata_sport": "basketball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island High\", \"North Caroline\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach David Plumer\", \"Sydney Riska\", \"Makayla Kropfelder\", \"head coach A.J. Jackson\", \"Caroline Cavanaugh\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Kent Island 46, North Caroline 37",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Bailey's hot hand fuels Warriors",
    "date": "2024-02-29",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bailey's hot hand fuels Warriors\n\n February 29, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 705\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Wicomico High's defensive plan had held Easton's Marshall Bailey without a field goal for almost three quarters Thursday night.Then in a bid to generate some offense, Indians head coach Butch Waller dropped his triangle-and-two scheme and went man to man. Bailey took advantage of the switch, connecting for 25 of his game-high 30 points over the final 11-plus minutes while leading Easton to a 69-56 victory over Wicomico in a Class 2A East Region II semifinal.\nThe second-seeded Warriors (15-6) travel to top-seeded Queen Anne's (17-4) on Tuesday for the 2A East Region II championship at 5 p.m. The Lions defeated fourth-seeded Parkside, 62-55, in last night's other semifinal.\n\"Second half they went man and he was hard to stop,\" Easton head coach Dionte Hynson said of Bailey. \"He was hitting his jump shot, getting to the hole. He put us on his back in the second half.\"\nWith the Indians' triangle-and-two limiting Bailey and backcourt mate Ja'Kai Brooks, Easton worked the ball inside to Tymier Thomas (12 points) whose seven first-quarter points helped the Warriors take a 16-10 lead heading into the second quarter.\n\"They really couldn't get open,\" Hynson said of Bailey and Brooks in the first half. \"Thankfully, Tymier was hitting. He kept us in the game in the first half.\"\nDown 18-12, the Indians rallied to draw even at 18-all on Tyson Hayward's bucket with a little over 5 minutes remaining in the half. Brooks then hit a 3-pointer to break a 22-all tie with 2:06 left before intermission. Kyree Acree (12 points) sandwiched a 3-pointer and a layup around a Zymier Ramirez free throw to push the Warriors lead to 31-25 at halftime.\nWaller stayed with his triangle-and-two zone until midway through the third when he opted to go man in hopes of kick-starting his offense.\n\"We ran a little triangle-and-two and we did an OK job, but we couldn't balance it by scoring,\" Waller said. \"The defense was fine in the first half, even halfway through the third quarter. And then I just said, 'We've got to play them man to man, take our chances, pick the tempo up.' We couldn't do it.\n\"Same problem we've been plagued with all season long; can't score,\" Waller continued. \"Shooting percentage tonight was 32.1. We've had that same problem, which is understandable with some new players. At this level, or any level really, you've got to be able to put the brown thing in the round thing.\"\nBailey began doing just that.\nAfter consecutive baskets from Kahlil Batson, and a nice low-post bucket from Acree, Bailey made a three-point play with 3:13 left in the third quarter for a 40-29 Warrior lead. Wi-Hi's Tay'Shawn Swann (16 points) scored on a reverse layup. But Bailey answered with a 3-pointer and another three-point play, extending Easton's lead to 46-32 by the end of the third quarter.\n\"I just knew we had to lock in defensively because that first half we were only up by six and it was a sloppy game,\" Bailey said. \"So once we locked in defensively — I think we held them to like seven points — everything offensively started clicking for us.\"\nThomas scored on a drive to maintain the Warriors' 14-point cushion at 48-34 early in the fourth quarter. Bailey then hit back-to-back 3-pointers, drove for a layup and buried a pull-up jumper for a commanding 58-41 lead with 5:13 remaining in the game.\n\"Excellent shooter,\" Waller said of Easton's sophomore guard. \"One of the better shooters I've seen in years.\"\nBailey converted a Thomas dish for a layup, then assisted on Thomas and Acree baskets that gave Easton a 65-52 lead with just over two minutes remaining.\nTakai Hugee led Wi-Hi with 18 points.\n1A EAST REGION II PLAYOFFSCambridge-SD 84 Crisfield 32CAMBRIDGE — LaShaun Moody scored a game-high 30 points as the top-seeded Vikings rolled into the 1A East II final.\nKoby Ennals had 15 points for the Vikings, who got 11 from Sterling Perkins and 10 from Semaj Pinder.\n1A EAST REGION I PLAYOFFSCol. Richardson 75 Bohemia Manor 63AMERICAN CORNER — Dreden Lake led all scorers with 21 points and Jazir Dickerson and Makiah Batson each added 16 as the second-seeded Colonels advanced.\nManny Wallace finished with 15 points for Colonel Richardson.\nLandon Horsey and Ayden Holderer scored 18 points apiece for Bohemia Manor. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 29,
    "metadata_sport": "basketball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton Warriors\", \"Wicomico Indians\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Dionte Hynson\", \"head coach Butch Waller\", \"Marshall Bailey\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 69, Wicomico 56",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Lions survive Easton rally for title",
    "date": "2024-03-05",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions survive Easton rally for title\n\n March 5, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 615\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Jeff Hollis said he wasn't nervous, especially with seniors Vince Gilberto and Collin Woolford in the game.\"I always feel like with Vince and Collin on the court that somebody's going to make a winning play,\" said Hollis, Queen Anne's County High's boys head basketball coach.\nHollis was right. Somebody did make a winning play, and that somebody was freshman Jadereon Thompson.\nFouled with 1 second left, Thompson hit the first of two free throws Tuesday night to lift top-seeded Queen Anne's to a 66-65 victory over No. 2 seed Easton in the Class 2A East Region II championship.\nThe Lions (18-4), who have won six straight since a Jan. 30 loss at Easton, now wait for the Class 2A field to be reseeded, and will likely host a state quarterfinal either Friday or Saturday. Easton finishes the season 15-7.\n\"Really elated. It's been three years coming to this game and we've lost two of them,\" said Hollis, whose season was ended by Wicomico the past two years in the region final. \"So to finally win one feels really good.\"\nDown 32-31 at halftime, the Lions looked well on their way to victory as they outscored Easton, 22-7, in the third quarter to carry a 53-39 lead into the fourth.\nBut behind Marshall Bailey, who scored 17 of his game-high 27 points in the fourth quarter, Easton rallied as the sophomore guard scored on a drive with 4 seconds remaining to tie the game at 65-65.\nOn the ensuing inbounds pass, Gilberto threw a pass three-quarters of the court to Thompson, who drove straight to the basket and was fouled with 1 second left on the clock.\n\"I was either going to get the layup or get fouled,\" Thompson said.\nDuring a timeout prior to his free-throw attempts, Thompson said all his teammates came up to him offering him encouragement.\n\"It felt great,\" Thompson said. \"I had to knock 'em down to win the regional final.\"\nThompson hit the first, then watched the second attempt bounce and get rebounded by Gilberto to seal the win.\n\"He's really not a freshman,\" Hollis said of Thompson. \"He's a freshman in age, but he's not a freshman. He's been building to this point. He's just an exceptional kid and an exceptional athlete.\"\nEaston outscored the Lions 26-13 in the fourth quarter.\n\"I thought we were going to overtime,\" Warrior head coach Dionte Hynson said. \"I'm proud of the kids. Helluva comeback. We had to dig deep. We had to play solid D. I'm not taking anything away from Queen Anne's but our kids played their butts off tonight.\"\nDiontye Copper's 3-pointer with 6:27 left in the third quarter pulled Easton even with the Lions at 35-all. But Queen Anne's outscored the Warriors 18-4 over the final 5:39 of the quarter to build a 53-39 lead, with Woolford (12 points) scoring seven points in the run.\n\"We just couldn't score any points third quarter,\" Hynson said.\nBailey, who was 8 of 9 from the foul line, hit a pair of free throws to start Easton's fourth-quarter comeback, drained two 3-pointers and continuously attacked the rim, scoring or drawing contact.\n\"He's a great player,\" Hollis said of Bailey. \"Hat's off to Easton. I thought their players and their coaching staff did a great job. They weathered the storm when we got up on 'em. They came back.\n\"But our kids made the final winning play,\" Hollis added. \"We talk about it all the time. Things happen during a game. Who's going to be the man that makes the final winning play. We've had that a couple times this year.\"\nGilberto had 14 points for Queen Anne's, Nickai Gross and Hollace Branch each had 10, and Thompson finished with nine.\nRayan Haq had 10 for Easton and Kyree Acree eight. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 5,
    "metadata_sport": "basketball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Centreville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Easton High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Jeff Hollis\", \"Jadereon Thompson\", \"head coach Dionte Hynson\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Queen Anne's 66, Easton 65",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "QA's-Carver playoff cut short by altercation",
    "date": "2024-03-09",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "QA's-Carver playoff cut short by altercation\n\n March 9, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 516\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Friday night's Class 2A boys basketball state quarterfinal playoff between Carver Vo-Tech and host Queen Anne's County High was stopped with 6:57 remaining in the third quarter when an altercation broke out between the two teams that left one Queen Anne's player injured.In a press release late Friday night, the Queen Anne's County Office of the Sheriff said, \"it was actively investigating an assault that took place Friday on a Queen Anne's County High School basketball player at the game.\n\"We have obtained and are currently reviewing video surveillance footage as part of our ongoing efforts,\" the release stated. \"No arrests have been made at this time as we continue to review evidence and fully investigate this incident.\"\nCarver was winning 42-15 at halftime and extended that lead to 47-15, when Dorrian Sands hit a baseline jumper and a 3-pointer in the first minute.\nQueen Anne's was approaching mid-court on its ensuing possession when a cross-court pass was intercepted by a Carver player, who drove quickly to the basket and was fouled from behind by Thompson. A Carver player confronted Thompson.\nVideo footage on Twitter showed players from both teams exchanging words near the Carver basket. Players and coaches from both benches came onto the court. The video then shows the altercation escalating when a Queen Anne's player is punched in the face. It is not clear from the video who struck the Queen Anne's player.\nMembers of the Queen Anne's County sheriff's department, Queen Anne's school administrators and staff, and both coaching staffs quickly separated players. The game's three officials conferred and decided to officially call the game.\n\"Our players, teams, community and school staff deserve a safe environment, we have zero tolerance for this type of behavior at our public events, and we can assure our community that this investigation will be thoroughly completed, and appropriate charges will be done at the conclusion,\" Queen Anne's Sheriff Gary Hofmann said in the press release.\nQueen Anne's County athletic director Kwandrey Wilson and head basketball coach Jeff Hollis would not comment on the incident.\nCarver advances to the semifinals where it will meet Middletown.\nAs of Saturday afternoon, it was not known if the Maryland Public Secondary Schools Athletic Association would take any action on the incident or dole out any punishment.\nQueen Anne's (18-4) took a 2-0 lead on a pair of Vince Gilberto free throws 1:23 into the game. The Bears (16-4) rattled off the next nine points before Collin Woolford hit a 3-pointer for the Lions midway through the first quarter.\nCarver, which used a suffocating, in-your face pressure defense, stretched its lead to 16-5 when Sands (game-high 25 points) drained one of his five 3-pointers. Gilberto hit two free throws to get Queen Anne's within 16-7. But with 54.9 seconds left, Gilberto appeared to suffer an ankle injury while driving to the basket and was carried off the court. The senior guard did not return the rest of the game.\nThe Bears led 18-8 after one quarter, widened their lead to 32-14, then went on a 10-0 run to close the first half, taking a 42-15 lead into the locker room. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 9,
    "metadata_sport": "basketball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Centreville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Carver Vo-Tech\", \"Queen Anne's County High\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Gary Hofmann\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Carver wins 47-15 (game stopped due to altercation)",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "C-SD locked in for last dance",
    "date": "2024-03-14",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "C-SD locked in for last dance\n\n March 14, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 964\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n When Darnell Dantzler brought his Edmondson-Westside High boys basketball team together for its first practice Nov. 15, he established the same goal he's set the previous 17 seasons as head coach — winning a state title.That same day, Shawn Tucker, starting just his second season as Cambridge-South Dorchester's head coach, provided a string of goals for his team that included winning the North Bayside regular-season title, the Bayside Conference championship, the Class 1A East Region II crown and the state championship.\nTucker and the top-seeded Vikings (24-1) hope to complete that title string Saturday beginning at 1 p.m., when they face the No. 2 seed and defending state-champion Red Storm (22-2) for the Class 1A state championship at the University of Maryland's Xfinity Center.\n\"You had a lot of good pieces that needed to learn to play basketball together as a team really quickly,\" Tucker said, recalling his first impression of this season's team. \"But there wasn't quite a team.\"\nTucker has quite a team on his hands now, one that won its first 13 games this season, lost its one and only game to Queen Anne's, then reeled off 11 consecutive wins to earn a shot at the school's first state title in basketball since 1996.\n\"It's going to be a really, really good challenge,\" Tucker said of Edmondson. \"But I go back to us playing in the Bayside (Conference) championship versus a team like (Stephen) Decatur. It prepared us.\"\nCambridge-SD fell behind 8-0 to Decatur on Feb. 22, settled itself, then raced to its first outright conference championship since 1996.\nThe Vikings got off to blazing starts in their state quarterfinal and semifinals wins. The second of those came Tuesday against Fort Hill, when a one-time 17-point lead was whittled to just three with 5:38 remaining. But instead of calling timeout, Tucker let his team continuing playing, and watched as Anthony Hughes came up with two huge steals to spark a decisive 8-0 Vikings run.\n\"When that kid hit a three, I could have called a timeout,\" Tucker said of Jabril Daniels' trey that got the Sentinels within 47-44. \"And I looked at them and I said, 'Nope.' I'm going to let them play through that because this is the only way they're going to learn this scenario. They know that's me. In that situation, I call a timeout and I don't have any faith in them. So by not calling that timeout, it showed them that I faith in them and they responded.\"\nNow, the Vikings prepare for an Edmondson program that makes its sixth final appearance in Dantzler's 18 seasons at the helm, which include state championships in 2013, '14 and '23.\n\"First day of practice my goal always is to make it to the state championship, setting goals up as high as you can set 'em,\" Dantzler said during a phone interview Wednesday. \"But sometime being realistic, you might not be as good. But the number one goal is to always win a state championship.\"\nDanztler though wasn't sure the Red Storm would get the chance to defend their title this season.\n\"I'm really surprised,\" Dantzler said of reaching the state final. \"We had a couple of people that graduated and transferred out. So going into the season I didn't think we were going to make it there. But through playing hard, I'm just happy and fortunate to make it there.\"\nAdding even more meaning to this championship trip for Dantzler is the fact his son, Darnell Jr., is the Red Storm's starting point guard.\n\"It's been a dream come true,\" Coach Dantzler said. \"Sometimes it's tough trying to draw the line as father and coach, but I wouldn't trade it for the world, just to have the experience of him being a part of my team, and me being part of his success for his high school career.\"\nA 5-foot-9 junior, Dantzler Jr. has averaged 16.5 points and 11 assists a game this season. He is joined in the backcourt by senior Kyrie Sherrod (13.3 points per game). Then there is Edmondson's frontcourt that features 6-8 sophomore forward Chase Foster (19.8 ppg.; 11 rebounds per game) and 6-4 senior forward Josiah Brown (14.5 ppg., 11.5 rpg.).\n\"They've played hard all season,\" Coach Dantzler said. \"Hopefully we can bring it home when we play.\"\nThat is also the hopes of a Cambridge-SD team that has hounded the opposition on defense this season and attacked the boards on both ends of the floor.\nLeading the attack has been 5-9 senior point guard LaShaun Moody (25 ppg.) whose quickness has been a problem for opponents on both ends of the court. Moody may run the show, but has plenty of help. Koby Ennals, a 6-5 junior (19 ppg., 10 rpg), can slash to the rim or bury the 3-pointer. And there's the 6-2 Hughes (10 points), who has come up with huge rebounds, steals and buckets throughout the Vikings' run.\nComplementing that trio has been a rotation that includes junior Darren Belizare and sophomores Semaj Pinder, Sterling Perkins (12 ppg) and Thomas Wilson, who have more than made their share of big plays throughout this season.\n\"I hear they're really good,\" Coach Dantzler said of Cambridge-SD.\nReally good, and really locked in, a quality Tucker has praised during this postseason stretch.\n\"I wish people could be in the locker room for the pre-game speech,\" Tucker said. \"When I say they are zeroed in, they are locked in. And I've never experienced a group of young men that get locked in so fast. Like one minute they can be joking and playing and when we say, 'All right, let's go,' they just locked in immediately. I thought that wasn't something possible, 'cause you know you can get caught turning that switch on and off. When they lock in, they're in. And that's the way we're going to have to be against Edmondson.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 14,
    "metadata_sport": "basketball",
    "metadata_story_type": "preview",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Cambridge-South Dorchester Vikings\", \"Edmondson-Westside Red Storm\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Shawn Tucker\", \"Darnell Dantzler\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "state championship",
    "metadata_outcome": "",
    "metadata_importance": 9
  },
  {
    "title": "Vikings' title shot falls shy",
    "date": "2024-03-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Vikings' title shot falls shy\n\n March 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1051\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n COLLEGE PARK — Maybe the stage was too big. Or maybe the team from the big city was just too good.Either way, Cambridge-South Dorchester High wasn't going away.\nDown 10 in the first quarter, the Vikings rallied to pull even with Edmondson-Westside of Baltimore with under a minute to play in the first half.\nMaybe the defending state champions thought an early third-quarter flurry that pushed their lead to 11 was enough to wilt the team from the Eastern Shore. No such luck. Again Cambridge-SD battled back to tie with one quarter remaining.\nCertainly the back-to-back 3-pointers that gave the Red Storm a nine-point cushion with less than three minutes left was enough to make their opponent concede. Still no dice, as Cambridge-SD twice cut that deficit to four in the final two minutes.\n\"They wouldn't quit,\" Edmondson head coach Darnell Dantzler said of the Vikings.\nThey wouldn't. But they wouldn't win either.\nNo. 2 seed Edmondson converted 5 of 6 free throws in the closing 33 seconds Saturday to finally seal a 69-65 victory over top-seeded Cambridge-SD in the Class 1A state boys' basketball championship at the University of Maryland's Xfinity Center.\n\"Unfortunately, we didn't come out on the right side of the clock and the game,\" said Cambridge-SD head coach Shawn Parker, whose team finished the season 25-3. \"But man, you saw, they fought to the end. And that's what these guys have been doing all year; fighting 'til the end.\"\nBidding for its first state basketball title since 1996, Cambridge-SD fell behind 14-4 with less than a minute left in the first quarter, when Edmondson's Kyrie Sherrod hit a 3-pointer. Senior guard LaShaun Moody (team-high 17 points, 10 rebounds) scored a layup and a pair of free throws to get the Vikings within six before Xavier Davis (11 points) scored to beat the buzzer and give the Red Storm (26-2) a 17-8 lead after the first quarter.\nDown eight, Cambridge-SD pieced together the first of its comebacks. Semaj Pinder (11 points) and Sterling Perkins drained back-to-back 3-pointers before Kobe Ennals (12 points) scored a lay-in to forge a 23-23 tie with 51 seconds left in the first half.\nBut Chase Foster, Edmondson's talented 6-foot-10 sophomore center who finished with 18 points, 13 rebounds and five blocked shots, hit a pair of free throws before Sherrod drilled a 3-pointer just before the halftime buzzer for a 28-23 Red Storm lead.\nEdmondson looked on the verge of taking control in the opening 2:33 of the third quarter, as Ennals picked up his third foul while the Red Storm were in the midst of a 10-4 run that gave them a 38-27 lead with 5:27 to go in the third.\nBut trailing 39-31, Cambridge-SD again began whittling away its deficit. Moody made a three-point play, then scored on a put-back to make it a three-point game. Darnell Dantzler Jr.'s jumper with 2:07 to go in the third gave the Red Storm a 45-38 lead.\nBut Darren Belizaire (10 points) hit a trey. Moody made yet another three-point play. Belizaire converted one of two free throws, before Moody zipped one of his five assists to Anthony Hughes for a layup just before the buzzer, tying the game at 47-all entering the fourth.\n\"Unfortunately, you don't want to get down that big 'cause it takes a lot of energy to come back,\" Tucker said. \"But one word that sticks out to my head is resilient. We're resilient. No matter what, we're going to fight until the end and that's what we did.\"\nFoster's follow nudged the Red Storm back in front at 49-47. Ennals answered with a jumper to create the game's fourth and final tie.\nIt remained close. But Josiah Brown (13 points) hit two free throws and a 3-pointer before Sherrod nailed a 3 for a 60-51 Edmondson lead with 2:20 remaining.\nPinder's 3-pointer and Hughes' layup off a steal pulled Cambridge-SD within 60-56 with 1:57 left.\n\"They played hard,\" Coach Dantzler said. \"Even when we went up a couple points they came down and hit a couple big shots.\"\nDantzler Jr. scored on a drive, extending Edmondson's lead to 62-56. Ennals hit a free throw. Brown scored inside to make it 64-57 Red Storm. But Pinder drilled a 3-pointer with 52 seconds left, getting the Vikings within four again at 64-60.\nBrown made one of two free throws with 33.4 seconds left for a five-point edge. Pinder fouled out when he was called for a charge. Ennals tipped the ensuing inbounds pass, but the Vikings could not come up with the ball. A quick foul by Moody sent Foster to the foul line, where he made both free throws for a 67-60 lead with 19.9 left.\nBelizaire connected for a 3-pointer with 10.6 seconds left, drawing Cambridge-SD within 67-63. But Dantzler Jr. hit a pair of free throws with 9 seconds to go for a 69-63 advantage.\n\"It says that we wanted it,\" Moody said when asked about the Vikings' unwillingness to fold. \"We came up a little short but it definitely says that we wanted it.\"\nTucker agreed.\n\"When that buzzer went off we just fell a little short,\" Tucker said. \"We competed. We showed everybody in the state of Maryland that we can compete.\"\nIt was Edmondson's fourth state championship under Dantzler, and the second time the Red Storm have won consecutive titles, matching the 2013 and '14 teams. But it was the first time Edmondson has won the Baltimore city title and state championship in the same year.\n\"It just speaks volumes about these kids,\" said Dantzler, who has coached Edmondson for 18 seasons. \"They went undefeated in Baltimore city, they won the city championship, and then to come in and win the state championship. Who could ask for anything more when it comes to these kids? This is the best record I had overall to be 26-2. It's a remarkable season that these guys had.\"\nDantzler had similar praise for Cambridge-SD.\n\"I told all of them when we were shaking hands that they played well,\" Dantzler said. \"And I told the coach I know this is only his first year down here, and he's only a second-year coach, but they did a remarkable job.\"\nNotes: Cambridge-SD outrebounded Edmondson, 37-33. … The Red Storm had 17 turnovers to Cambridge-SD's 16. … Edmondson finished 12 of 17 from the foul line, while the Vikings were 9 of 16. … Edmondson made nine 3-pointers to Cambridge-SD's eight. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
