    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "North Caroline 12, Woodbridge 1",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "CLOSING STATEMENTS",
    "date": "2024-03-21",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "CLOSING STATEMENTS\n\n March 21, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1379\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n UPPER MARLBORO — Giuseppe Mellinger didn't storm past his coaches, chuck his headgear in a fury, or try to leave an impression on the nearest concrete wall with his fist.The opportunity to direct blame was certainly there, but the Kent Island High senior refused to point a finger.\n\"I'm not going to make excuses for myself,\" Mellinger said in the wake of a 3-2 ultimate-tiebreaker loss in the 175-pound final during the Class 2A/1A state wrestling championship tournament at the Show Place Arena on March 2. \"You try not to let refs dictate your match. Wrestling you know, you've got to be accountable. It's only you out there wrestling somebody else.\"\nOf the 16 wrestlers from North Bayside schools that qualified for states, Mellinger was the only one to reach the finals in the boys competition.\n\"Coming from Kent Island, we rarely have people that make it that far,\" said Mellinger, who became just the third Buccaneer to reach the finals during Tommy Sitar's eight seasons as head coach — joining Matt Miller (2020) and Justin Thomas (2016). \"So it was surreal to be there in that moment.\n\"Senior year. You've wrestled three years of high school,\" Mellinger added. \"Wrestled since you were like five or six years old, and then you make it to the state finals. Like wow. This is it. But I felt pretty confident. I felt relaxed. That was my last match ever so I'm going to go out there and let it fly. Like it's just one more match.\"\nOn the way to that last match, Mellinger had earned a first-round 15-13 win over Hammond's Jaden Timithy in the first sudden-victory period, took a forfeit in the quarterfinals, then defeated Middletown senior Kashton Dalhouse (47-4) via a 5-4 decision in the semifinals.\nThat set up his title shot with South Carroll sophomore Anthony Rodrigues (47-3). With 45 seconds remaining in the first period, the lead official held up two fingers, signaling a two-point takedown for Mellinger. But that initial call was waved off when the official ruled Mellinger did not to have enough control.\n\"I thought I did too,\" said Mellinger when asked if he thought he had the takedown. \"I didn't hear anybody argue against it. And the South Carroll guys, their body language was like, 'Yeah, wow. We got saved on that one.'\"\nThough he did not protest or go to the scorer's table for an explanation, Sitar also thought Mellinger had a takedown.\n\"I would have liked to see that call stand at two,\" Sitar said. \"It wasn't something where they came to the table an overturned it or anything like that, or even that the second ref came in and overturned it.\"\nIt remained scoreless through the remainder of the first. Starting on bottom, Rodrigues scored an escape just two seconds into the second period for a 1-0 lead. Sixteen seconds into the third, Mellinger escaped off bottom, to make it 1-1. Mellinger blocked a shot 14 seconds later and after two restarts, the score remained 1-1 heading into overtime.\nMellinger got two deep shots in during the 1-minute overtime, but Rodrigues wouldn't be taken down as the duel moved into the two 30-second ride-out periods. Rodrigues again escaped with only two seconds elapsed for a 2-1 lead. Mellinger answered with an escape with six seconds left in the second ride-out, forging a 2-2 deadlock heading into the ultimate tiebreaker.\nBecause he had scored the match's first point, Rodrigues got to choose top or bottom to start the final period. Rodrigues took bottom and six seconds into the final stage pulled his left leg from Mellinger's grasp for a 3-2 victory.\n\"That (takedown) would have won the championship,\" said Sitar (39-6), who was bidding to become Kent Island's first state champion since 2015 when Stevan Smith won his second straight state title. \"Whoever had a takedown was going to win because shots were so hard.\n\"I was hoping for a state title,\" Mellinger added. \"But at the end of the day I was the aggressive wrestler. As the match went on, offense got harder and harder, especially when we got sweaty. Nothing you can down now.\"\nKent Island senior Greg Couch (132 pounds) had his hopes for a state championship end in Friday's semifinal round, when he lost a 12-6 decision to defending — and eventual — state champion JoJo Gigliotti of South Carroll.\n\"I think he gave him his toughest match,\" Sitar said of Couch's battle with Gigliotti. \"I think he knew he had a match with Couch. I think had Couch been on the other side of the bracket I might have had a couple of finalists. Greg wrestled his butt off. He was peaking at the right time.\"\nCouch looked headed for the consolation bracket, when he fell behind 4-0 in the first period of his opening match to Southern Garrett's Hayden Harvey.\n\"He got taken down twice and then it was like the switch was flipped,\" Sitar said of Couch. \"He was ready to go. He was comfortable and he just let the rest of the weight class have it from there.\"\nStarting with Harvey, who would not score another point while Couch was rallying for a 13-4 major decision. Couch earned a 7-1 decision over Huntingtown senior Jett Cramer (24-5) in the quarterfinals but then lost 12-6 to Gigliotti (51-1), who pinned his other three opponents in the tournament en route to his second state title.\n\"When I got into the consi round I realized, 'This is all or nothing,'\" Couch said. \"'This is my senior year. This is all I got left.'\"\nAnd it took some doing.\nCouch and C. Milton Wright's Mitchell Nguyen (36-8) were tied 2-2 through regulation and overtime. Couch then rode Nguyen out through the first ride-out stage before working an escape in the second ride-out for a 3-2 win.\n\"That was definitely one of my hardest matches of the year, it not the hardest,\" Couch said of his bout with Nguyen.\nIn the consolation final, Couch crafted a 7-2 victory over Northern Garrett's Mason Glotfelty (39-5) for his 100th career victory and a third-place finish.\n\"I had a lot going through my mind before that last match,\" said Couch, who finished 33-9. \"I had the support of my family. My dad and my uncle were on the mat coaching for me in my corner. It definitely provided me with everything I needed for that last match.\n\"It was definitely a hard path back to try to get third,\" Couch said. \"They say second place usually looks the most upset and third is smiling a lot more, and I can definitely agree with that. Definitely makes a difference ending it on a win rather than a loss.\"\nQueen Anne's County senior Tremaine Jackson earned his 100th career win when he earned a 54-second fall in his first-round match on Thursday, but watched his state-title aspirations end Friday with a 4-2 loss to Northern Garrett's Devon Opel (38-7) in the 126-pound quarterfinals.\n\"I had the whole night (Friday) to think about what happened,\" Jackson said of his loss. \"I mean it hurt, but I knew I just had to bounce back, just start doing what I do best.\"\nJackson's road to the consolation final started Saturday morning with a 15-0 technical fall over Liberty's Alex Pearl in 3:15. He advanced to the consolation semifinals with a forfeit, then put together a 13-0 major decision over Eastern Vo Tech's Chase Ullman.\"\n\"The tech fall, the (Pearl) kid walked off the mat and he was like, 'Man, that kid is fricking good,' when he shook my hand,\" Queen Anne's head coach David Stricker said. \"We were like, 'Yeah, we know.'\n\"He could just stop putting the pressure on himself and just go be Tremaine, and pretty much show off his full talents; his full arsenal,\" Stricker said of Jackson after his quarterfinal loss. \"When he's there and he can just let it fly, there's not a whole lot of people that can stop him.\"\nThat included Rising Sun's Xzavier Brown, who faced off for a fourth time this season against Jackson in the consolation final and lost 5-2.\n\"It was close but I feel like I calculated that match,\" said Jackson, whose second consecutive third-place finish at states capped a 40-4 season. \"I wrestled him a bunch of times. And that was my last high school match, so I was coming out on top regardless.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 21,
    "metadata_sport": "wrestling",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Upper Marlboro, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Giuseppe Mellinger\", \"Tommy Sitar\", \"Greg Couch\", \"Tremaine Jackson\", \"head coach David Stricker\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "tournament",
    "metadata_outcome": "Anthony Rodrigues won 3-2 in ultimate tiebreaker",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Newcomb, Colonel continue hot, early start",
    "date": "2024-03-26",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Newcomb, Colonel continue hot, early start\n\n March 26, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 764\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n AMERICAN CORNER — Makayla Newcomb continued her early season mastery, crafting a six-inning, two-hit shutout Tuesday, as Colonel Richardson High's softball team remained unbeaten with a 10-0 blanking of Kent County.Newcomb (4-0) had a season-high 12 strikeouts and walked none en route to her second straight shutout. She has allowed just two runs — only one earned — and eights hits over 23 innings this season. Newcomb has struck out 39 and walked two.\nClarissa Peugh had three hits and four RBIs to lead the Colonels (4-0). Kylie Rhinehardt had two hits and an RBI, and Aubrey Taylor stroked two hits and stole three bases.\nOn Monday, Newcomb fanned 10 while pitching a one-hit shutout against Crisfield. Rhinehardt had two hits and three RBIs, and Alaina Hall delivered a pair of hits and two RBIs. Kayleigh Bradley (hit) drove in three runs and Tyla Dickerson doubled.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 13, NORTH CAROLINE 6RIDGELY — Autumn Huber went 4 for 4 with four RBIs, which included jacking a two-run, first-inning homer to center field Tuesday, as the Lions improved to 3-0.\nEmily Rendulic, Abby Denes, Caroline Taylor, Mikayla Livingston, Hannah Martin and Camaryn Jacobi each had multiple-hit games for Queen Anne's. Martin (3-0) earned the win, giving up three runs and three hits over 4 2/3 innings of relief. She struck out five and walked four.\nAnna Hutchison took the loss for the Bulldogs, who were led by Elizabeth Knott (2 for 3).\nOn Monday, Livingston hit a two-run triple, and Denes and Emilie Smith produced run-scoring singles in a four-run sixth inning, as the Lions rallied for a 5-3 victory over Stephen Decatur.\nMartin got the win, scattering four hits over seven innings, fanning nine and walking four. Huber and Jacobi each had two hits.\nNORTH DORCHESTER 10, POCOMOKE 0SHILOH — Maddie Nagel and Jewels Vroman combined for nine strikeouts and did not issue a walk Monday, as the Eagles improved to 2-0.\nMackenzie Lewis led North Dorchester (2-0) with three hits and two RBIs. Maggie Hubbard had two hits, and Kinsley Peters, Emilee Cohee, Lola Allen, Vroman and Nagel each had one.\nST. MICHAELS 15, SNOW HILL 1ST. MICHAELS — Madi White was 2 for 3 with a double and four RBIs, and Angel Bock (2 for 3) doubled twice and drove in three runs Monday, as the Saints evened their record at 1-1.\nBock picked up the win, surrendering one earned run over five innings. She struck out seven and walked two.\nKatie Jenkins (2 for 3) scored three runs and Mercy Haddaway (2 for 4) finished with five RBIs.\nBASEBALLCOLONEL 9, KENT COUNTY 0AMERICAN CORNER — Jordan Regulski (2-0), Jack Walls and Daniel Hesson combined for a no-hitter Tuesday, as the Colonels improved to 4-0.\nWalls, Hesson, Brayden Dadds and Caleb Rouser each had two hits for Colonel Richardson.\nOn Monday, Gavin Whitby went 2 for 2 with four RBIs, and Manny Wallace drove in three runs as the Colonels rolled to a 17-0 victory over Crisfield. Whitby, Kenton Robbins and John Beaven combined for a five-inning one-hitter.\nST. MICHAELS 10, SNOW HILL 0ST. MICHAELS — Junior Will Sherwood (1-0) struck out seven over six innings of one-hit ball Monday as the Saints improved to 2-0.\nSherwood, Anthony Massaro, Henry Mulliken, Bobby Fink and Jack Brennan each had one hit for the Saints (2-0).\nNORTH DORCHESTER 11, POCOMOKE 1SHILOH — John Boyle and Nate Swann each went 2 for 4 with two runs and an RBI Monday, as the Eagles raised their record to 2-0.\nTJ Leonard earned the win, yielding one run and one hit over four innings. Leonard struck out 10 and walked three. Wes Windsor worked two scoreless innings of relief, striking out six, walking one and giving up one hit.\nWindsor also knocked in two runs and had a hit. Brock Warrell had an RBI single and plated two other runs on a bases-loaded squeeze play.\nKENT ISLAND 10, WICOMICO 0STEVENSVILLE — Kerry O'Brien was 2 for 3 with three RBIs, and Hunter Austin (two RBIs) and Jack Grossi each doubled Monday to lead the Buccaneers (1-1).\nWyatt Offutt (1-0) earned the win, yielding one hit and one walk over five innings. Offutt struck out seven. Gavin Schulte and Grossi each had an RBI.\nGIRLS LACROSSESSPP 19, J.M. BENNETT 5SALISBURY — Sophomore Caroline Ewing netted seven goals and three assists, and freshman Emery Zimmerman had seven goals and a pair of assists Monday as the Sabres rolled to their second straight victory.\nFreshman Julia Spero (assist) and sophomore Avery McCall scored two goals apiece for Saints Peter and Paul (2-1). Kendall Kroniser had a goal and one assist, Eryn Callahan set up three goals, and goalie Kylie Kroniser totaled seven saves. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 26,
    "metadata_sport": "softball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Colonel Richardson High\", \"Kent County\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Colonel Richardson 10, Kent County 0",
    "metadata_importance": 6
  },
  {
    "title": "Sabres deal Seahawks first loss of year",
    "date": "2024-04-09",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Sabres deal Seahawks first loss of year\n\n April 9, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1358\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Alison Beyer knows her talented sophomore class will likely stretch the success of Saints Peter and Paul High's girls lacrosse team over the next few years.Beyer also knows senior captain Morgan Quade has been a huge reason why the Sabres have won three consecutive Eastern Shore Independent Athletic Conference championships, and will push for a fourth this season.\nQuade scooped ground balls that extended possessions and scored a season-high six goals, including a third-quarter strike that put the Sabres ahead to stay Monday on the way to a 13-10 victory over previously unbeaten Stephen Decatur.\n\"She's such a versatile player,\" Beyer, Sts. Peter & Paul's head coach, said of Quade, who is headed for Loyola University after graduation. \"She's just so smart. She makes it happen on the field, and sometimes I just don't know how she does it. It's just her energy. Her lacrosse IQ is on point. I'm a stickler for fundamentals and she's got it.\"\nIn a clash that featured five ties and four lead changes, Quade made a slew of plays that returned possession, or kept the ball in the Sabres' offensive end throughout the game.\nAfter four early saves by Decatur goalie Addison McDaniel (14 saves), sophomore standout Caroline Ewing (five goals, two assists) gave the Sabres (4-1) a 1-0 lead with 6:42 left in the first quarter. A little over a minute later, Quade scored her first goal before sophomore Avery McCall (goal, three assists) set up classmate Eryn Callahan's goal for a 3-0 lead.\nBut the Seahawks (5-1), who like the Sabres have a roster heavy with sophomores (11) and freshmen (three), and had never trailed this season entering Monday's game, netted four of the next six goals, as Sadie Kauffman (three goals) and Lily Carson scored 18 seconds apart near the end of the first quarter to knot the game at 4-4.\nKauffman nudged the Seahawks in front at 5-4 when she curved in from the right and snapped a shot into the left corner. About a minute later, Decatur's Jill Burton was injured after being struck inadvertently by a teammate's pass.\nWhile the play rattled some Decatur players, the Seahawks maintained their slim lead until Quade converted a Ewing feed for the equalizer with 3:02 left in the second quarter, then scored to put the Sabres in front at 6-5 just 1:18 later. The Seahawks again pulled even at 6-6 with 11 seconds left in the half on Laila Pacucci's goal.\n\"Lot of emotions going on on the sideline and the game,\" Stephen Decatur head coach Lindsay Owens said of Burton's injury. \"Trying to kind of come back from having one of your players down. I noticed a lot of girls were upset, but we were like, 'Hey, we're proud of you.' They were able to kind of put everything aside and also play, but also deal with all these emotions.\"\nThe Seahawks went back in front at 7-6 on Caitlin Shimko's goal 1:45 into the third quarter. But Ewing scored on a pair of free-position opportunities 1:14 apart, returning the lead to the Sabres at 8-7 with 7:15 left in the third. MB Morse slipped behind the defense and tucked a shot past Sts. Peter & Paul goalie Kylie Kroniser (eight saves) with 5:54 left to forge the game's fifth and final tie at 8-8.\nThe Sabres won the ensuing draw. Just 29 seconds later, Ewing got the ball on the left wing and flipped a pass to an open Quade, who quickly dumped a shot over McDaniel's left shoulder for her fifth goal and a 9-8 lead with 5:25 remaining in the third quarter.\n\"We really needed to get the draw controls,\" Owens said. \"They got those draw controls, they got the possession, they were able to come down, and they have some amazing players. They are able to shoot and go from everywhere.\n\"We made stops,\" Owens continued. \"Our goalie played amazing, had some great saves. But the second and third opportunities with a talented team like Saints Peter and Paul. ... If you keep giving them that, they're going to eventually score. And that's kind of the name of the game right there; possession and draw controls. And we just didn't get enough of them.\"\nAddison made a huge stop on Quade from close range near the end of the third. But the Sabres controlled possession and played settled in the fourth, outscoring the Seahawks 4-2 over the final 12 minutes for their fourth consecutive victory.\n\"That's what I wanted them to do the whole time,\" said Beyer, who called a timeout less than 5 minutes into the game to tell her team to slow down. \"That's what killed us in the beginning. Rushing. That was the negative aspect of what killed us in the beginning of the game. 'Slow it down,' I said. 'This is where the beginning starts now. We have possession. Here's where it begins.'\"\nGoals from Ewing and Quade fattened the Sabres lead to 11-8 before Kauffman completed her hat trick with 7 minutes remaining, drawing Decatur within 11-9. And though the Seahawks would get no closer, McCall hit Ewing in stride with a pinpoint pass and watched her teammate pop her fifth goal into the net for a touch of insurance with a little over 6 minutes remaining for a 12-9 lead.\n\"Just like a typical play we practice,\" Beyer said of the goal. \"Beautiful. They've been playing together for years. Just your average, everyday play. It was beautiful transition down the field. Just like we talked about.\"\nMorse also finished with a hat trick for Decatur.\n\"They have a ton of talent on that team,\" Owens said of the Sabres. \"Some of our girls that are starting, have never really been in a game like this before at this level. So it was really cool to have them see this game now in our season. I'm very happy with where we're at. We just need to fix a few things and kind of clean up the possessions and score on the other end.\"\nBASEBALLCOLONEL 9, MARDELA 4MARDELA SPRINGS — Jack Walls had three hits and earned the win Monday to lead unbeaten Colonel Richardson.\nManny Wallace also had three hits for the Colonels, and Jordan Regulski and Daniel Hesson added a pair of hits each. Kenton Robbins yielded one earned run in 4 1/3 innings for Colonel.\nST. MICHAELS 12 WASHINGTON 2PRINCESS ANNE — Jacob Seek allowed two hits, two earned runs and struck out 10 over five innings, as the Saints continued their unbeaten ways.\nConnor Wheeler was 1 for 3 with two runs scored and two RBIs for St. Michaels. Jack Brennan (1 for 1) drove in two runs and scored once, and Jacob Bealfeld (1 for 2) scored two runs.\nN. DORCHESTER 8 SNOW HILL 2SNOW HILL — John Boyle surrendered five hits and two earned runs over six plus innings Monday as North Dorchester rolled.\nBoyle struck out nine while earning the win. Nate Swann had a three-run double and a two-run single for North Dorchester. Wes Windsor, freshman Brayden Phippen and sophomore Manny Reyes each had two hits.\nSOFTBALLST. MICHAELS 18 WASHINGTON 1PRINCESS ANNE — Madi White was 3 for 4 with a double, and Katie Jenkins was 3 for 5 with three RBIs Monday to pace St. Michaels.\nMyla Ramey earned the win, allowing three hits and one earned run over five innings. Ramey fanned six and walked just one.\nAyla Gowe (2 for 3) doubled for the Saints and Ramey was 2 for 4.\nMARDELA 5, COLONEL 2MARDELA SPRINGS — Eileen Johnson and Kendall Sann had two hits apiece Monday, but it wasn't enough for the Colonels to upend undefeated Mardela.\nKylie Rhinehardt and Clarissa Peugh each had an RBI single for Colonel Richardson. Makayla Newcomb took the loss, yielding four earned runs and three walks to go with three strikeouts over six innings.\nKaylie Adkins got the win for the Warriors.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 10, WICOMICO 0SALISBURY — Emily Hirschbock pitched a two-hit, five-inning shutout Monday, striking out six and walking none to lead the Lions.\nCaroline Taylor hit a solo inside-the-park homer in the fourth inning, and Emilie Smith, Camaryn Jacobi and Autumn Huber each had multiple-hit games to lead Queen Anne's 11-hit attack. Abbie Denes added a three-run triple. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 9,
    "metadata_sport": "lacrosse",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
