  {
    "title": "Saints top Eagles, stay unbeaten",
    "date": "2024-04-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Saints top Eagles, stay unbeaten\n\n April 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 879\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n ST. MICHAELS — St. Michaels High head baseball coach Brian Femi has always been known for the small things.And the small things again helped produce a victory Wednesday afternoon.\nBut the Saints also got a big pitching performance from Will Sherwood and a huge throw from center fielder Connor Wheeler that cut down a run at plate while helping St. Michaels remain unbeaten with a 4-1 victory over North Dorchester.\n\"Anytime you don't walk anybody you're going to be in a ballgame,\" Femi said. \"Their pitcher (Wes Windsor) held us in check and we made a couple errors. And because Will pitched so well, it just kept us in a good position to win the game.\"\nA junior right-hander, Sherwood (2-0) was touched for seven hits, but did not walk a batter and struck out 12 over six innings, mixing fastballs and curves.\n\"Coach was calling a lot of curveballs,\" Sherwood said of assistant Zach Wallace. \"I was getting 'em over and they were swinging and missing at the ones that were low that he called. And locating the fastball was really nice today.\"\nNorth Dorchester (4-2 overall, 0-1 North Bayside) took a 1-0 lead in the second inning. Nate Swann reached on an error to lead off the frame. Sherwood got a called strike three on Vernon Price, but Manny Reyes singled Swann to third, and Chance Blake singled down the left-field line to plate Swann. Sherwood ended the rally there, striking out the next two batters.\n\"He's picked up a little more velocity, but I've noticed he's got a lot more poise to him now,\" Femi said of Sherwood. \"And I think it comes with experience. And he doesn't get rattled.\"\nSherwood could have gotten rattled in the fourth, when the Eagles' first two hitters got on. Instead, he struck out the next two batters before getting an inning-ending groundout.\nThe Saints (6-0, 1-0) took a 2-1 lead in the home fourth, but North Dorchester pieced together a two-out rally in the fifth, when Nate Fraccaroli reached on an error and Windsor dumped a single into center field. Swann followed by lacing a single up the middle that Wheeler got to quickly before unloading a strike to catcher Henry Mullikin, who tagged out Fraccaroli trying to score from second.\n\"I thought North Dorchester actually had the momentum in the early parts of the game until that throw,\" Femi said. \"I think it just changed the whole complexion of it; took the wind out of their sails. And I think it gave us a little bit of an offensive boost once that happened.\n\"That ball was a little bit in the gap,\" Femi continued. \"It wasn't like it was right to him. So he had to cut it off and then just made a throw right on the money. And that's a hard play for a catcher to make. People don't know. You got a guy barreling down on you. He still has to slide, but that's as bang bang as you can get.\"\nVernon Price hit a leadoff single in the sixth but Sherwood retired the next three batters to end his day. Anthony Massaro came on to pitch the seventh, allowing one hit.\n\"Pitchers did a great job today,\" North Dorchester head coach Dave Morrissette said. \"Both sides. Will did a great job. Kept us off balance with that curveball a little bit, early especially. We hit some balls hard, but we got to score some guys once we get 'em on too.\"\nSt. Michaels struggled for offense, totaling six hits against Windsor, but scratched two runs across in the fourth inning.\nJacob Bealefeld worked a leadoff walk. Mullikin singled to right field. Pearson Greenwood's groundout to second moved Bealefeld to third. Pinch-hitter Eli Wallace then dropped a bunt to score Bealefield with the tying run.\nWindsor hit Ethan Royer with a pitch. Royer stole second, broke for third and got caught in a rundown, allowing Mullikin to score from third for a 2-1 St. Michaels lead.\nThe Saints tacked on a run in the fifth. Hunter Gottleib reached on a bunt single and stole second. Wheeler hit a hard comebacker that Windsor made a nice play on before throwing to first for the out. Gottleib moved to third on the play and looked like he might get stranded, as Windsor retired Sherwood, then walked Bealefield. But Bealefeld broke for second, drawing a throw from Windsor. Gottleib raced home for a 3-1 lead.\n\"We bunt the ball and just try to put some balls in play,\" Femi said. \"We had a lot of at-bats where we were striking out and just didn't put balls in play. He (Windsor) competes. I can't say enough about him and his catcher (TJ Leonard). We just had to do what we had to do not so much because of us, but because of them. I thought they played a really good game.\"\nSt. Michaels added a run in the sixth. Greenwood singled off the glove of the leaping Fraccaroli at second. Bobby Fink moved Greenwood to second with a sacrifice bunt. Two batters later, Gottleib popped up a bunt that Windsor made a lunging attempt to catch, but couldn't come up with the ball, allowing Greenwood to score from third.\nNotes: Wheeler hit a two-out triple in the third but was stranded when Windsor got an inning-ending groundout. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 10,
    "metadata_sport": "baseball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "St. Michaels, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"St. Michaels Saints\", \"North Dorchester Eagles\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Brian Femi\", \"Will Sherwood\", \"head coach Dave Morrissette\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "St. Michaels 4, North Dorchester 1",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Easton gets in the zone, routs Rams",
    "date": "2024-04-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton gets in the zone, routs Rams\n\n April 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 825\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SALISBURY — This is how last season started for Easton High's boys lacrosse team.The Warriors lost their season-opener to Salesianum of Delaware then proceeded to piece together a school-record 14-game winning streak that ended in the Class 2A state semifinals.\nEaston head coach Dennis Keenan may have thought he was watching last season unfold all in one afternoon Friday, as the Warriors surrendered the game's first goal, started slow, then reeled off 12 consecutive goals en route to a convincing 14-2 win over Parkside for their third straight victory.\n\"We're starting slow so we got to work on that. It's my fault. I got to get them into a warmup or something. Or some kind of dance routine before the game,\" Keenan said with a smile. \"Get 'em a boombox. Get Saturday Night Fever going or something just to get 'em going.\"\nAfter Nate Pusey's goal staked the Rams (3-3) to a 1-0 lead with 1:41 elapsed, Easton (3-1) struggled against Parkside's zone defense. The Warriors finally broke through when Hudson Royer snapped a left-handed shot past Rams goalie Nicolas Pastusak with 3:50 remaining in the first quarter.\nEaston's struggles continued, but its defense allowed Parkside little in offense, killing off two extra-man opportunities early in the second quarter. The Warriors nudged in front when Drew Schmidt got free in front of the goal and tucked a shot over Pastusak's shoulder for a 2-1 lead with 4:52 left before halftime. Ty Finlay then won the ensuing faceoff, sprinted downfield and scored 12 seconds later for a 3-1 lead Easton carried into the half.\n\"First time seeing the zone this year and the guys just had to work through it,\" Keenan said. \"Once we saw the seams in the zone the guys were able to take advantage of it. They tried to dodge into the zone too much early. Once they were aware of it, they pulled it out and starting moving the ball more.\"\nThat was evident in the third quarter, as Easton, despite a brief, cold burst of rain and continuously strong gusting winds, began finding openings in Parkside's defense. Landon Chaney scored just 1:11 into the third quarter to make it 4-1. Only 37 seconds later, Tyler Rardin bulled through traffic and flicked the first of his game-high five goals into the net for a 5-1 lead.\n\"Defense played great all game,\" Rardin said. \"Offense, we finally just started shooting the ball more and they started falling second half. I think it was just trying to figure out their zone on offense, trying to find open shots. I think we got a lot in the first half, but really started making them in the second.\"\nCampbell Taliaferro set up Rardin's second strike with 4:21 left in the third. Less than a minute later, Blake Royer zipped a pass from behind the net to Charlie Kaminskas, who completed the nice give-and-score play for a 7-1 lead. Blake Royer capped the 5-0 third-quarter scoring spree with under 2 minutes remaining in the third, parking an overhand shot into the net for an 8-1 cushion.\n\"I think our offense is getting there,\" Warrior goalie Jack Kilbourne said. \"They're starting to click.\"\nThe offense continued clicking and widening the lead in the fourth. Rardin sandwiched a pair of goals around a Schmidt score, and Taliaferro notched a goal, all within the first 5 minutes of the fourth for a 12-1 bulge.\nParkside ended a 39-minute, 25-second scoring drought with just under 7 minutes left in the game, when Tavian Dashiell slipped one past Kilbourne, who along with defensive mates Kyle Dyott, BT Todd and Edward Whelan held the Rams to their lowest scoring output of the season.\n\"I think we just kind of played as a whole unit,\" said Kilbourne, who finished with 11 saves. \"And we really trusted our guys to push them in the right direction. I saw shots, I was able to make a save. They put me in a good position.\n\"I should have had it,\" Kilbourne said of Pusey's first-quarter goal. \"But you can't have 'em all. I think we're moving in the right direction. And I think we're only going to get better from here.\"\nEaston closed the scoring with goals from Jackson Wieland and Rardin in the final 6 minutes.\nGIRLS LACROSSEEASTON 18, PARKSIDE 7EASTON — Myka Motovidlak scored four goals and set up three others Friday as the Warriors routed the Rams.\nStella Kabler, Dakota Lhotsky, Addie Knox and Emily Currie each recorded hat tricks for Easton, and Miranda Stup and Anna Crist added one goal apiece. Zelia Proa made five saves and Haiden Hulliger stopped eight shots.\nBASEBALLQUEEN ANNE'S 13, ANNAPOLIS 12CENTREVILLE — Dalton Brown had three hits and drove in two runs, and Dulin Clark and Jacob Clough each had two hits and three RBIs Saturday as the Lions edged Annapolis.\nEvan Dickey earned the win for Queen Anne's County.\nColin Siwald and Dickey each had a hit and one RBI for the Lions, and Travis Sutch and Bobby McCarthy finished with a hit apiece.. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 13,
    "metadata_sport": "lacrosse",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Salisbury, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Parkside High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Dennis Keenan\", \"Tyler Rardin\", \"Jack Kilbourne\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 14, Parkside 2",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Easton continues new approach, tops Saints",
    "date": "2024-04-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton continues new approach, tops Saints\n\n April 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1079\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n ST. MICHAELS — The rough start to the season was one thing.Not making contact though may have troubled Ryan Brice even more. So Easton High's first-year head softball coach tried a different approach.\n\"I told them, 'We're not going to have any more double-digit strikeout games,'\" Brice said. \"I told them before each game we're going to line up in the on-deck circle and time the pitcher. I'm not sure, but I think today we only had one strikeout. So I'm going to keep on going with that approach.\"\nThat approach certainly worked Monday, as the Warriors churned out 17 hits, including four doubles, on the way to a 14-3 six-inning victory over St. Michaels that extended their win streak to three.\n\"It's very hard to beat a team like St. Michaels like this,\" Brice said of a Saints team that entered the game having won three of four, its lone loss coming to defending Class 1A state champion North Dorchester. \"They are very well coached. They're very aggressive. They're a good team from top to bottom. So for us to come here and do what we did is huge.\"\nEaston (4-3 overall, 3-0 North Bayside) pushed four runs across in the first inning. Mayah Wilkins (4 for 5) and Lyla Roe (3 for 5) reached on back-to-back singles. Olivia Orrell and Sammy Townsend followed with consecutive run-producing groundouts.\nSt. Michaels starting pitcher Angel Bock (2-3) looked like she might escape further damage when she worked the count to 1-2 on Laillah Janes. But Janes (2 for 5) singled to right and Gemma Vogel reached on an error before Brynlee Buchkoski (2 for 4) doubled down the left-field line to plate Janes and Vogel for a 4-0 lead.\nMyla Ramey relieved Bock to start the second, but Easton stretched its lead to 6-0, scoring two runs on a throwing error.\n\"Just an off-day all around,\" St. Michaels head coach David Fisher said after his team slipped to 4-4, 1-2). \"I really felt like we were focused and we were ready. We just didn't click. Couple mental errors. It wasn't even the Easton-St. Michaels hype. It was just lack of focus. And then all of a sudden pitching change and we got down early and just didn't pull ourself out.\"\nEaston expanded its lead in the fourth, cranking out five runs on seven hits. Roe reached on an error and scored on Orrell's double down the left-field line to make it 7-0. After Townsend singled, Janes and Vogel delivered consecutive RBI singles for a 9-0 cushion. Isabelle Ludwig added an RBI single before Roe hit a ball deep in the hole at short for an RBI infield hit and an 11-0 lead.\nRoe (2-1) had a shutout through four innings, but issued back-to-back walks to Bri Grow and Ramey to open the bottom of the fifth. Roe was relieved by Townsend, who got Katie Jenkins to bounce into a fielder's choice. But Madi White (2 for 3) singled to center, scoring Ramey. Bock followed with a single to right that plated Jenkins before White scored on Lola Browning's sacrifice fly to left, drawing the Saints within 11-3. Townsend stopped the threat there, inducing Brooke Reilly into an inning-ending groundout.\n\"She pitched a great game,\" Brice said of Roe, who allowed two runs, three hits, three walks and struck out three over four innings. \"I call her a dog for a reason. She's a tough one. Lyla Roe did an amazing job today.\"\nThe Warriors regained their 11-run edge in the sixth. Leigha Bosley lined a leadoff double to left. After a Wilkins single, Roe singled to right, scoring Bosley. Wilkins scored on a wild pitch before Townsend's groundout to short plated Roe for a 14-3 lead.\nMercy Haddaway reached on an infield hit to open the Saints' sixth, but Townsend recorded a strikeout before getting a pair of groundouts to second to end the game.\nCOLONEL 6 CAMBRIDGE-SD 1AMERICAN CORNER — Makayla Newcomb pitched a one-hitter, striking out 11 and walking just one, as the Colonels ended a four-game skid.\nNewcomb also had a two-run single for Colonel Richardson (6-4). Montana Davis hit a two-run triple, Sadie Prettyman belted an RBI triple, and Aubrey Taylor cracked a double.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 16 KENT COUNTY 0WORTON — Emily Hirschbock had three hits, including a two-run triple and a two-run double as the Lions remained unbeaten.\nHirschbock also earned the win, pitching five innings of three-hit shutout ball. She struck out seven and walked two.\nCamaryn Jacobi had three hits for Queen Anne's, and Caroline Taylor and Emily Rendulic had multiple hit games.\nBASEBALLST. MICHAELS 3 EASTON 2ST. MICHAELS — Senior left-hander Jacob Seek allowed no earned runs and five hits over 6 2/3 innings to help the Saints remain unbeaten on Monday.\nSeek struck out 12 and walked one. Will Sherwood earned the save.\nAnthony Massaro was 2 for 3 with a two-run double, and Jacob Bealefeld was 1 for 2 with an RBI and a run scored.\nNORTH DORCHESTER 8 KENT ISLAND 5SHILOH — Nate Swann was 2 for 3 with two RBIs and a run scored, and Wes Windsor reached base three times which included an RBI double to fuel the Eagles.\nVernon Price had an RBI double and TJ Leonard and Blake Bramble each scored twice. Boyle also got the save.\nCOLONEL 10 CAMBRIDGE-SD 0AMERICAN CORNER — Kenton Robbins yielded one hit over 3 2/3 innings, striking out four, as the Colonels blanked the Vikings.\nJack Walls had two hits for Colonel Richardson, and Jordan Regulski, Caleb Rouser, Manny Wallace, Derrick Mitchell and Preston Rickwood each had one hit.\nGIRLS LACROSSENORTH CAROLINE 20 EASTON 11RIDGELY — Riley Walstrum (two assists), Reece Bisesi and Claire Blue each had four goals Monday to lead the Bulldogs.\nAshley Kercheval netted a hat trick for North Caroline (3-1) and Ashlynn Bisesi had two goals and two assists. Gracie Faulkner, Calleigh Tribbett and Maddie McNinch had one goal each, and Maddie Nicholson had an assist.\nSSPP 21, PALLOTTI 4EASTON — Senior Morgan Quade dished nine assists and scored four goals, and sophomore Eden Zimmerman had four goals and two assists to pace the Sabres.\nFreshman Emery Zimmerman added a hat trick an two assists for Saints Peter an Paul, and Caroline Ewing and Kendall Kroniser had two goals and two assists apiece. Emma Hainey scored twice, and Eryn Callahan (assist), Avery McCall (assist), Kate Sweeney and Julia Spero had one goal each.\nGUNSTON 25 INDIAN RIVER 21CENTREVILLE — Sophomore Layla Kent had 12 goals and freshman goalie Sarah Porter made 12 saves to lead the Herons to their third straight win Monday. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 16,
    "metadata_sport": "softball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "St. Michaels, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"St. Michaels\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Ryan Brice\", \"head coach David Fisher\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 14, St. Michaels 3",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Mardela blanks ND in clash of unbeatens",
    "date": "2024-04-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Mardela blanks ND in clash of unbeatens\n\n April 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 691\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SHILOH — This was act one in a potential three-part series.If acts two and three do materialize, North Dorchester High head softball coach Carol Hubbard hopes they go a lot better than this one.\nMardela cranked out 14 hits and sophomore pitcher Hayden Adkins hurled a complete-game one-hitter for her sixth shutout of the season Thursday, as the Warriors rolled to a 10-0 victory over the Eagles in a battle of unbeatens.\n\"Definitely an off-day,\" Hubbard said after the Eagles committed an uncharacteristic six errors. \"We knew going in we're both tough teams. We both have strong pitching. We both typically put the bat on the ball. Tonight they outplayed us. No other way to say it. We had errors in the field. We didn't put the bat on the ball nearly as well as we're capable of. It just wasn't our day.\"\nMardela (12-0) took a 2-0 lead in the first inning against Eagles ace Emilee Cohee (5-1). Lexie Guy led off by blooping a single into center field. Julia Wisk advanced her with a sacrifice bunt. Camryn Dorr reached on an error that pushed Guy to third. Ava Twilley hit a comebacker to the mound that Cohee fielded. Knowing she didn't have time to get Guy at the plate, Cohee wisely threw for first for the second out. Karsyn Shockley then smacked a 2-2 pitch to the right-field fence for a double, plating Dorr. Cohee got Elana Donalds to ground out to second, ending the inning.\nCohee retired the first two batters of the second inning. Samantha Witte then hit a towering pop-up that landed between second baseman Emily Boyle and right fielder Journey Pearson. Witte raced to second on the play, stole third and scored on Guy's single to left field for a 3-0 lead.\n\"Eight of our nine in the lineup are batting .390 or above,\" said Mardela head coach Kory Shiles, whose team defeated North Dorchester in last year's Bayside Conference championship — the last team to defeat the defending Class 1A state champion Eagles. \"We have five\" — Twilley, Wisk, Dorr, Donalds, Abby Campbell — \"that are batting over .500. That to go with our phenomenal pitching ...\"\nPhenomenal indeed.\nComing into Thursday's game the Warriors had yielded just two runs in regular-season play — that to Colonel Richardson. Adkins (7-0) struck out eight and walked two. Then there's Twilley who has only given up one hit all season.\n\"Mardela played a great game,\" Hubbard said. \"They made the plays when we put the ball in play. And Hayden pitched a great game.\"\nNorth Dorchester got its only hit in the third, when Mackenzie Lewis beat out a spinning infield single up the third-base line. Cohee followed with a walk, but Adkins squelched the rally there, getting Kinsley Peters to ground out to short.\nThe closest the Eagles came to a second hit came in the fourth when Maggie Hubbard scorched a hard grounder went off first baseman Shockley that Shiles called an error. Adkins retired 11 of the final 12 batters she faced, surrendering a leadofff walk to Maddie Nagel in the seventh. Nagel, who was hit on the ankle with ball four, was lifted for a courtesy runner, who was thrown out trying to steal second.\nMardela extended its lead to 4-0 in the fourth. Guy and Wisk hit back-to-back two-out singles before Dorr singled to right, scoring Guy.\nThe Warriors put together another two-out rally in the fifth, but Cohee got a called third strike on Witte to end the inning.\n\"I thought Emilee pitched well,\" Hubbard said, whose starter gave up 11 hits, the last, an RBI double by Dorr that pushed Mardela's lead to 5-0 in the sixth and ended Cohee's day on the mound. \"She gave up one walk. Not a bad performance. Can't ask a whole lot more out of your pitcher than that.\"\nTwilley greeted reliever Jewels Vroman with an RBI single. The Warriors tacked on two more runs — both scoring on errors.\n\"I got a feeling we'll see them one more time, if not two more times,\" Shiles said of a potential rematch with the Eagles in the Bayside Conference championship and the Class 1A East Region I final. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
