  {
    "title": "Lions closer to finish line",
    "date": "2024-04-23",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions closer to finish line\n\n April 23, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 590\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — One word has been on the collective minds of Queen Anne's County High's softball team all season. Finish.\"Our motto has been finish all year long,\" Lions head coach Aaron Stewart said. \"We didn't finish last year. Coming into the season this year, one of my coaches just came up to me and said, 'You know the word of the year has to be finish.' And we're finishing right now.\"\nQueen Anne's took a big step in putting itself in a position to finish atop the North Bayside Tuesday afternoon, using a nine-hit attack to back the pitching of Emily Hirschbock in a 6-1 victory over defending division champion North Dorchester.\nThe Lions (12-1 overall) moved into first place in the North at 4-1 and now hold the tiebreaker over the Eagles (11-2, 4-1) after beating them in their only head-to-head meeting of the season. Colonel Richardson (10-4, 4-2), which defeated Kent County by forfeit Tuesday, North Dorchester and Easton (6-4, 4-1) remain in contention but need help to overtake the Lions.\n\"The bats definitely came back today,\" said Stewart, who was puzzled after his team was limited to just five hits in last Tuesday's 4-1 loss to Colonel Richardson. \"We hit the ball well. Opposite field, which is even better. I like to see opposite-field hitting. We practice that over and over and over again, to take that outside pitch and drive it to right field. And we executed it today.\"\nThe Lions did just that in the third inning, breaking a scoreless tie with three consecutive two-out, opposite-field hits for a 3-0 lead.\nMorgan Gottleib worked a one-out walk against North Dorchester ace Emilee Cohee (5-2) and was advanced to second on Emily Hirschbock's sacrifice bunt. Cam Jacobi lined a single into right field to score Gottleib. Caroline Taylor (3 for 4) drilled a 1-0 offering into the right field corner for a double. Autumn Huber then drove another Cohee delivery the other way for a two-run triple.\nNorth Dorchester scratched a run across in the top of the fourth. Maggie Hubbard (2 for 3) hit a leadoff single and scored four batters later, when freshman Journey Pearson lined a shot to center that Jacobi just missed making a spectacular leaping catch on for the third out of the inning.\nThat would be the lone run allowed by Hirschbock (7-0) who finished with a complete-game five-hitter, striking out four and walking four.\n\"I was just kind of thinking to attack every pitch,\" said Hirschbock, who retired nine of the last 11 batters she faced. \"It's been a big thing for me to attack specifically the first batter. I feel like I did that pretty well tonight. I'm proud of myself for that.\n\"It's always nice pitching with the lead,\" Hirschbock said with a big smile. \"My hitters hit and it was awesome.\"\nQueen Anne's extended its lead in the fifth. Jacobi reached on a one-out error. Taylor dropped a bunt that Cohee fielded, but had to hold onto with no one covering first. Huber walked to load the bases. Abbie Denes singled to left field to plate Jacobi for a 4-1 lead. Cohee notched her seventh and final strikeout before Emily Rendulic singled to score Taylor and Huber for a 6-1 cushion.\n\"Tough day,\" North Dorchester head coach Carol Hubbard said. \"They out-hit us. We had a few unfortunate things on the base paths. Not making excuses. They made the plays they had to make.\"\nMaddie Nagel relieved Cohee, got an inning-ending strikeout, then held the Lions scoreless in the sixth despite yielding singles to Jacobi and Taylor. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 23,
    "metadata_sport": "softball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Centreville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"North Dorchester\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Aaron Stewart\", \"Emily Hirschbock\", \"head coach Carol Hubbard\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Queen Anne's 6, North Dorchester 1",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Regulski, Hesson deliver Colonel to North championship",
    "date": "2024-04-30",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Regulski, Hesson deliver Colonel to North championship\n\n April 30, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 746\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n AMERICAN CORNER — Jordan Regulski never let the door get open. Daniel Hesson made sure it stayed shut.Colonel Richardson High's dynamic pitching duo combined to throw a two-hitter Tuesday as the Colonels defeated St. Michaels, 3-1, to clinch the North Bayside baseball championship.\n\"When you walk into a situation like this, I'm a lot more blessed than a lot of people that take over programs,\" Colonel Richardson first-year head coach Garrett Nepert said after the Colonels improved to 15-1 overall, 7-0 North Bayside with their 11th straight victory. \"I'm grateful. But this is all about the kids. I don't do too much. They do it all.\"\nEven if Colonels loses Thursday at North Dorchester, it would still win the division title based on defeating St. Michaels (14-2, 7-1). Colonel defeated the Saints, 6-5, last week, but Tuesday's second meeting between the two teams is the one that counts toward the division standings.\n\"We didn't deserve to win,\" St. Michaels head coach Brian Femi said. \"You can't give a good team like this all the opportunities that we gave them. And they played better defense and made smarter plays that we did. I told our guys their baseball IQ just wasn't there today. I told them that's not their fault. That's my fault because we've got to find away to teach it better.\"\nRegulski and St. Michaels ace Jacob Seek were locked in a scoreless duel, when Colonel scratched across two runs in the bottom of the fourth.\nBrayden Dadds worked a one-out walk. Derrick Mitchell was hit by a pitch. Both runners advanced on a wild pitch. Manny Wallace then popped out to St. Michaels first baseman Anthony Massaro in foul territory on the first-base side. With no one covering home, Dadds broke for home and dove across the plate in a cloud of dust for a 1-0 lead. Massaro got the ball to catcher Hunter Gottleib, whose tried to throw out Mitchell at third. But the throw to third was off the mark, bringing home Mitchell for a 2-0 lead.\n\"We did what we needed to do for sure,\" Nepert said. \"We still got a way to go. We've got a week before we've got to play our best baseball.\"\nSt. Michaels pulled within 2-1 in the top of the fifth. Regulski issued a leadoff walk to Ethan Royer. The junior right-hander struck out Brock Grow before getting Connor Wheeler to bounce into a fielder's choice. Wheeler stole second, moved to third on a passed ball, then raced home on a passed ball. Regulski struck out Jacob Bealefeld to end the inning.\nColonel regained its two-run lead in the home fifth. Seek walked Jack Walls and Regulski to open the inning. Hesson then dropped a bunt up the first-base line. Massaro fielded the ball, looked to third, then threw late to first, loading the bases. Seek fanned Dadds, but Walls scored on a passed ball to make it 3-1. Seek walked Mitchell before retiring the next two batters to end the inning.\nRegulski yielded a clean single to Pearson Greenwood (2 for 3) to leadoff the second. But that was the only hit St. Michaels mustered against the Colonel starter. Regulski allowed one run, walked four, hit Will Sherwood twice with pitches and struck out seven.\n\"He competes,\" Femi said of Regulski, who has verbally committed to Wake Forest University. \"He battles you. He is crazy athletic; makes plays that pitchers might not make, but makes them look easy.\"\nHesson came on at the start of the sixth inning. The senior left-hander who is headed to Salisbury University after graduation, gave up a one-out single to Greenwood in the sixth and nothing else, striking out four of the seven batters he faced.\n\"They're both fantastic kids,\" Femi said of Hesson and Regulski, who have worked at Femi's baseball camps the past two summers. \"Not only are they good baseball players but they're good people.\n\"He carries this team,\" Femi said of Hesson. \"Mentally, he's a leader for them.\"\nNepert kept his praise for his two pitchers brief and to the point.\n\"Excellent,\" Nepert said. \"That's about all I got. I thought they attacked the zone. They worked to their strengths.\"\nSeek, who struck out seven and walked seven, gave up a leadoff single to Walls (2 for 3) to start the game. It was Colonel's only hit off Seek. Grow came on in relief with a man on in the sixth. he gave up a one-out single to Walls in the sixth and finished with two strikeouts and a walk. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 30,
    "metadata_sport": "baseball",
