  {
    "title": "New-look Buccameers begin Davis era",
    "date": "2024-09-05",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "New-look Buccameers begin Davis era\n\n September 5, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 577\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n The head coach is new, and so are many of the faces in starting roles on both sides of the ball.But not everything has changed with Kent Island High's football program, which is set to begin the Zach Davis era Friday when the Bucs host Parkside at Andy Schipul Stadium at 6 p.m.\n\"We kept a lot of things very similar to how things have been done. I think that's helped,\" said Davis, who begins his 10th season as a head coach, but his first in Stevensville as the successor to Bryon Sofinowski, who retired after last year. \"Even though there's a whole bunch of new guys, it's not like it's brand new to the coaches and players. It's pretty similar.\"\nOne thing that has remained the same is senior quarterback Gavan Henry, who returns after helping the Buccaneers to a 9-3 season a year ago, which included upending North Caroline in the second round of the Class 2A East Region playoffs.\n\"He's throwing the ball really well,\" Davis said of Henry, who will be backed up by junior Ryan Brestensky. \"Good arm, good command of the offense. He's a good player for us.\"\nThough last year's starting backfield graduated, starters Nate Green (senior wingback) and Jaxson Hess (junior single back) did get playing time in reserve roles last season. Junior wingbacks Connor Byrne and Kerry O'Brien figure to also get plenty of touches.\n\"We're going to come downhill at you,\" Davis said. \"We're going to run zone, power schemes, counter schemes, some buck-sweep schemes. Our passing game comes off our run game. And then we have a spread package, where we can throw it some.\"\nWhen Henry does throw, his targets will include senior Richie Guthrie and juniors Luke Umstot and Isaiah Diaz at wideout, and senior tight end Jackson Towers.\nThe Bucs graduated their entire offensive line from last year, so Davis, who coaches the offensive line, has been busy rebuilding. Senior James Prince starts at center between junior guards Mike Luttrell and Gerson Chevez. Senior Joe Guida is at right tackle, while Jeremiah Bounds starts on the right side.\n\"They're a really good group and they're working hard,\" Davis said of the offensive front. \"But I'm their position coach so I'm partial. I think they're coachable, and I think if someone's coachable they can play pretty quickly, as long as they have the physical tools.\"\nPerhaps the biggest returner for the Bucs is longtime defensive coordinator Tim Goodrich, who watched his unit notch three shutouts last season, and limit two other opponents to just seven points.\n\"He tells me all the time if I can score 14 points we'll win a lot of games,\" Davis said of Goodrich, who looks to replace three all-Bayside Conference first-team selections in lineman Brady O'Neal, defensive back Tucker Claxton, and linebacker Giuseppe Mellinger, the conference defensive player of the year.\nJunior Greg Coffey, Bounds and Guida start up front, with senior Keegan Green and Towers at ends. Junior Roy Scott and O'Brien are at inside linebacker, with Green and Hess manning the outside backer spots. The secondary includes a combination between junior Taray Wright, Andy Bennett, Umstot, and Byrne.\nSpencer Chappell kicks for the Bucs and Keegan Green punts.\n\"I think you feel excitement before every game,\" said Davis when asked about heading into his 10th season opener, the first nine of which were as a head coach in West Virginia. \"That's good though. That means it means something. It's coming quickly. Then we'll move on to the next one.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 5,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "preview",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island High School Buccaneers\", \"Parkside\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Zach Davis\", \"Gavan Henry\", \"Tim Goodrich\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Ensminger, Monroy each score twice as Warriors romp in opener for new head coach",
    "date": "2024-09-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Ensminger, Monroy each score twice as Warriors romp in opener for new head coach\n\n September 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 600\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — He knows there will be tougher games and stiffer competition.But, man, did this first one feel good.\nKyle Ensminger scored a pair of goals in the opening 14 minutes, and Ale Monroy scored in each half Tuesday, as Easton High's boys soccer team rolled to an 8-0 victory over Wicomico in the head coaching debut of Avery Brice.\n\"Feels great,\" said Brice, who played on the Warriors' 2014 team that reached the Class 2A state championship game. \"I told the guys in the group, everything I asked of them they delivered. I think that means a lot for me. It means they're balled in. They got the same mindset as me. That means everything. First game, 8-0. But it doesn't stop today. And we don't be complacent.\"\nEnsminger collected a pass from Kai Ferraris, slipped past a defender, and deposited Easton's first goal of the season into the net just 4:14 into the game.\nWicomico keeper Matthew Bier stopped a point-blank bid by Ensminger a little over two minutes later. But in the 14th minute, Ensminger gathered a lead pass from Dominic Stanco, calmly pushed downfield with a pair of defenders closing on his right hip, drew Bier off his line, and neatly tucked a left-footed shot into the right side of the net for a 2-0 lead.\nThe Warriors continued to extend their advantage.\nMario Medina fed Monroy, who unloaded a low, hard drive off Bier's hands for a 3-0 cushion with 25:30 left in the first half. CJ Chaconas settled a ball from Ensminger and hammered a shot off the keeper's hands to make it 4-0. Landon Chaney capped the first-half scoring with a drive from near the left post for a 5-0 Warrior lead.\n\"We continue to go,\" said Brice of his team's not getting complacent despite its comfortable halftime lead. \"And I know when we get really tested, opportunities won't come like they did today. I told the guys that. So we do got to work on finishing, things of that nature, taking our chances and making them count when they are presented upon us.\"\nEaston junior keeper Sam Hutchison was never seriously challenged, as the Warriors' backline and midfield controlled play.\n\"Love our defense. Love our possession game,\" Brice said. \"The guys know that we got to play a little bit simpler. And I told the guys from the get (go), like we do the simple things correct, we'll be the best team.\"\nMedina broke in from left wing in the 8th minute of the second half and was taken down, setting up his penalty-kick goal that pushed the lead to 6-0. Monroy netted his second goal with an innocent-looking chip less than two minutes later. Elmer Pineda completed with scoring with 24:08 remaining when he put one past Indians backup keeper Sam Fleuriot, making it 8-0.\nA new rule instituted this season stops play in the 60th minute if a team has an eight-goal lead.\n\"It was the first thing I was thinking when I woke up this morning,\" Brice said of his first game as head coach. \"So proud of these guys. Talented team. I just want them to continue to know that. One game at a time. We move on. OK, it's not a celebration. We did what we were supposed to and we move on.\"\nQUEEN ANNE'S 2, PARKSIDE 2\nCENTREVILLE — Ryan Lee and James Rath each scored once, and Gage Jordan notched an assist as the Lions battled the Rams to a draw in double overtime.\nJ.M. BENNETT 10, N. CAROLINE 2\nRIDGELY — Heath Frantum and Ayden Russell had one goal apiece for the Bulldogs against the defending Bayside Conference-champion Clippers. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 10,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Wicomico\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Avery Brice\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 8, Wicomico 0",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Queen Anne's takes aim at fifth straight North Bayside title and more",
    "date": "2024-09-19",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Queen Anne's takes aim at fifth straight North Bayside title and more\n\n September 19, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1297\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Queen Anne's County High's girls soccer program has compiled an impressive resume during Mike Kern's 23 seasons as head coach.The Lions have won 14 North Bayside titles and two Bayside Conference championships. They've reached 11 regional finals, winning twice, and have advanced to the state finals once.\nBut as Queen Anne's has worked through preseason tryouts, scrimmages and into the regular season's early weeks, Kern sees a team that could be among his finest from front to back.\n\"This might be our most talented team we've ever had in my opinion,\" said Kern, who has never had a losing record as he enters his 24th season as head coach. \"Every single one of them has played travel for years. There's a lot of times we'd get good athletes that played lacrosse or something else, and just played soccer to stay in shape. But this year we have soccer players. And for most of the girls, their first sport is soccer. I think that's the difference from years past.\"\nDespite that talent, which includes all-state first-team selection and returning North Bayside Player of the Year Olivia Ryan, Kern knows there is one main obstacle in his team's path to a fifth consecutive North title — the 2020 COVID-shortened season excluded — and a potential regional title — Kent Island.\nThough Queen Anne's shut out the Bucs 3-0 during the regular season last year, it lost 3-2 in double overtime in the Class 2A East Region II title game.\nThe Lions blanked Kent Island, 1-0, already this season, but have a second date with their county rival Oct. 16 which will likely decided the division crown, and then could have a third encounter come the postseason.\n\"I'm excited. I really am,\" Kern said of the season. \"We should go far. But we've got to get through Kent Island.\"\nHere's a look at this year's teams.\nCAMBRIDGE-SDHead Coach: Sarah Alpaugh.\nWho's Where: Goalie: Akizhae Hammond (Jr.); Backs: Monti Jackson (So.); Skyla Ali (So.); Mimi Exume (So.); Sophia Diaz (Fr.); Midfield: Lainey Jones (Sr.); Olivia Fischer (Fr.); Kamryn Bourne (So.); Lilly Moxey (Fr.); Forwards: Laila Insley (Sr.); Madalyn Spicer (Jr.); Jade Bryant (Jr.); Kennedy Bourne (So.).\nOutlook: The Vikings graduated their entire defensive unit from a year ago, so one of Alpaugh's focuses will be hoping a young cast of replacements can stick together and gain experience to form a strong foundation for the future.\nJones is a four-year starter that anchors a midfield, which hopes to regain the services of returning starter Kamryn Bourne, who was injured in the season's second game. Alpaugh, who begins her third year, calls Insley a key component, and coupled with the speedy Bryant, could provide a nice one-two punch at the top.\nCOL. RICHARDSONHead Coach: Beth Carmen.\nWho's Where: Goalie: Cloee Welzel (So.); Backs: Olivia McGregor (So.); Kallie Peele (Sr.); Violet Cheezum (Jr.); Adan Alvi (Sr.); Agatha Divjak (Fr.); Lila Schriefer (Jr.); Vanessa Odidika (Sr.); Midfield: Brynne Harper (So.); Doranelli Tomas-Ortiz (So.); Forwards: Ryleigh Hall (So.); Sadie Prettyman (So.).\nOutlook: The Colonels got clobbered by Snow Hill a year ago, 8-0. They met the Eagles in this year's opener and scored the game's first goal in an eventual 4-2 loss.\n\"I have really seen a lot of improvement from last year,\" said Carmen, who is in her second year as head coach. \"I've actually had one of the refs come over to me and say this is one of the best looking Colonel girls soccer teams that he's seen in a long time.\"\nCarmen will rotate players between defense and midfield, and attack and midfield in hopes of finding a consistent combination. After falling to the Eagles in the opener, Colonel bounced back with wins over Cambridge-SD and North Dorchester.\n\"This is a really great group of kids,\" Carmen said. \"They work well together and they communicate well.\"\nEASTONHead Coach: Ellie Kilmon.\nWho's Where: Goalie Zelia Proa (So.); Backs: Dakota Lhotsky (Jr.); Sam Murphy (Sr.); Bella Swann (Sr.); Finley Golden (So.); Stella Petrichenko (So.); Midfield: Addie Schindler (Fr.); Laillah Janes (Sr.); Addie Chandler (Sr.); Anna Quidley (Sr.); Megan Wright (Fr.); Angie Martinez (Jr.); Forward: Miranda Stup (Jr.).\nOutlook: Kilmon isn't making any predictions whether the Warriors can battle Queen Anne's and Kent Island for division supremacy.\n\"I never know until we play,\" Kilmon said. \"It really depends on how the girls show up and play.\"\nCenter backs Lhotsky and Murphy look to provide a solid defensive core.\n\"I'm excited for the year,\" Kilmon said. \"I think we have a good amount of returners and we just have a nice team dynamics that's building so I'm excited to seem them grow together.\"\nKENT COUNTYHead Coach: Ben Joiner.\nWho's Where: Goalie: Sophia Cousineau (So.); Backs: Mary Jane Joiner (Jr.); Paige Miller (Sr.); Savanna Reynolds (Jr.); Reese Strong (Fr.); Midfield: Delilah Markovic (Jr.); Jane Cleary (So.); Emy Karlik (Fr.); Bella Hinefelt (So.); Forwards: Zuleika Nava (Jr.); Sabrina Tee (Fr.); Madison Garcia (Fr.); Morgan Kendall (Sr.); Kayla Aguilar (Fr.); Ashlynn Elborn (Fr.); Amelia Plummer (Fr.).\nOutlook: A 1995 Kent County graduate, Joiner takes over as head coach and has watched his young team get off to a 2-1 start.\n\"We really got a lot of positive momentum going with the number of girls that have joined the team this year,\" said Joiner, who praised the work of assistant Joel Tolbert in helping develop the team. \"We're super young. We're really relying on the leadership of our seniors and juniors to elevate the team.\"\nJoiner said an emphasis has been placed on defense in preparation for the 2A schools on the schedule, but has been encouraged by his team's play against 1A rivals.\nNORTH CAROLINEHead Coach: Jenna Riordan.\nWho's Where: Goalie: Rylee Gowen (Jr.); Amber Biars (Jr.); Backs: Sarah Brown (Sr.); Natalie Newman (Jr.); Izzy Knott (Sr.); Midfield: Emma Hansley (So.); Megan Rogers (Sr.); Madison McNinch (Jr.); Paisley Garland (Jr.); Linda Ridgell (Jr.); Forwards: Irma Sanchez (Jr.); Seanna Rupp (Sr.).\nOutlook: The Bulldogs opened the season by defeating Parkside — gaining a measure of revenge on the Rams, who knocked them out of the playoffs in last year's 2A East Region I title game — but then lost by a goal to Easton before getting humbled by Stephen Decatur.\n\"We have had a rough start to our season so far, but I'm hoping to get a couple wins under our belts the next two weeks,\" Riordan said. \"That ought to help our record.\"\nRiordan also hopes a few wins help her team's confidence as it transitions from a 4-4-2 scheme to a 3-5-2 alignment.\n\"We're still adjusting and learning our positions,\" said Riordan, who begins her sixth season. \"I've always said that we play up to the teams that are above us, so I do think we'll play (Queen Anne's and Kent Island) a really good game.\"\nQUEEN ANNE'SHead Coach: Mike Kern.\nWho's Where: Goalie: Bea Holthaus (So.); Backs: Shannon Rath (Jr.); Hayden Legg (Sr.); Finley Patchett (So.); Kathryn Baer (Sr.); Abby Robinson (So.); Midfield: Chloe Sharp (Sr.); Olivia Ryan (Sr.); Morgan Scott (Sr.); Kara Ringold (Jr.); Forwards: Madi Horney (So.); Riley Klepper (Sr.); Taylor Napier (So.).\nOutlook: The most notable graduation loss for the Lions was back Elizabeth Oldfield. But this could be one of the best defensive units Kern and longtime assistant Jeff Anthony have ever had in Centreville.\nKern also noted Scott and Ringold may be the two best outside midfielders Queen Anne's has had since the days of former standout Kesley Dean. Add to the mix Ryan, the all-state pick and the Lions' leading scorer from last year is back, along with a group of nine sophomores, several of which played together on a travel team coached by Kern.\nDespite all that, Kent Island remains the primary hurdle in the North, while James M. Bennett or Stephen Decatur — which defeated the Lions in overtime to open the season — are angling toward the South Bayside crown and a possible conference championship showdown.\nEditor's Note: Information for Kent Island and North Dorchester was unavailable. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 19,
