  {
    "title": "Warriors survive Bulldogs' rally",
    "date": "2024-09-20",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors survive Bulldogs' rally\n\n September 20, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 836\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — It didn't matter that North Caroline High's football team had lost its first two games this season.The Bulldogs just scared Easton head coach Matt Griffith. And in a furious fourth-quarter span of just over four minutes they showed why, shaving a 28-point Warrior lead to just seven.\nThat's when Mikey O'Connor took over. Easton's senior quarterback ran for a pair of first downs and threw for another in the final 2:52 Friday night to finally secure a 28-21 victory that pushed the Warriors' record to 3-0.\n\"They got great athletes all over the field,\" Griffith said of North Caroline. \"They've struggled early on, but you saw exactly how explosive they can be, and how quick they can be explosive. That's what I was worried about. Sometimes you can do all the right things, but sometimes really good athletes can make plays. And that's a lot of what happened in the last five minutes of the twilight zone that we're calling it.\"\nO'Connor hit Diontaye Copper for a 35-yard touchdown pass 1:41 into the third quarter, and Dominic Stanco tacked on the point-after kick for a 28-0 Easton lead.\nThe Warriors had not allowed a point all season until North Caroline's Tyson Weber scored on a 16-yard run with just under seven minutes left in the game, drawing the Bulldogs (0-3) within 28-7. Mason Shilling added the extra point.\nEaston went three-and-out on its next possession, with O'Connor booting a quick kick to North Caroline's 27. Bulldog senior quarterback Corey Bunce hit Derek Jones for 13 yards, then connected with Zymear Smith for a 33-yard pickup. Weber scored on a 27-yard touchdown run on the next play and Shilling again converted the PAT, narrowing Easton's lead to 28-14 with 3:57 left.\nNorth Caroline recovered the ensuing onsides kick at the Warrior 47. Bunce hit Jones for a 20-yard gain. Easton's Tymiere Thomas sacked Bunce for a 5-yard loss. But the Bulldogs' quarterback fired toward the end zone on the next play, and watched the ball deflect into the hands of Jones for a 33-yard touchdown. Shilling's point-after kick made it 28-21 with 2:52 remaining.\n\"Hell of a comeback,\" North Caroline head coach Nic McMorris said. \"I'm so proud of the resilience of our guys. It was the first time that we recognized this team.\n\"They're a great team,\" McMorris added of Easton. \"They have such a dynamic offense. But we were moving the ball and just kept shooting ourselves in the foot. And then we just started playing inspired football.\"\nNorth Caroline drove from its 38 to the Easton 12 when it fumbled the ball away late in the first quarter. But the Bulldogs regained possession four plays later when O'Connor's pass was deflected and intercepted by Smith. Again Caroline moved the ball, marching 32 yards to the Warrior 8. But the Bulldogs again fumbled, with Easton recovering at the 1.\nEaston's next drive ended with a fumble, giving North Caroline possession at the Warrior 25. But North Caroline went no where on three plays. Shilling came on to attempt a 40-yard field goal, but slipped on his approach, the kick falling well short.\nThe Warriors, who took a 7-0 lead on O'Connor's 3-yard touchdown run and Stanco's PAT with 3:54 left in the first quarter, stretched their lead to 14-0 with 2:11 left in the first half, when Jordan Horsey slammed in from five yards out. Stanco again made the extra point.\nEaston took advantage of a short Caroline punt, as O'Connor hit Carter Mooney for 18 yards, and Copper for 17 yards before scoring on a 2-yard run for a 21-0 halftime lead.\n\"We came out with a game plan and we executed very well,\" Griffith said. \"I think we did a lot of really good things early, even with two turnovers. Those are our first two turnovers of the year. We were able to bounce back.\"\nSo was North Caroline.\n\"No excuses,\" Griffith said. \"Caroline's a good football team. They're a very good football team for being 0-3.\"\nIt's one-time 28-point cushion reduced to just seven, Dustin Blue recovered the onsides kick for Easton after North Caroline had gotten within 28-21.\nO'Connor ran for three yards before Caroline called time. O'Connor then ran for eight yards and a first down. North Caroline stuffed Easton's quarterback for no gain on the next play and again burned a timeout. O'Connor then fired an 11-yard dart to Carter Mooney for a first down, then found room around left end for a 22-yard pickup and a first down that clinched the win. O'Connor took a knee on the next two plays to bleed the final minute.\n'We got up and then we just kind of let off,\" O'Connor said. \"And we just can't do that against a great team like this. Great first half. Then the second half we slipped off. But we need to play two halves of football.\"\nGriffith agreed but applauded his quarterback.\n\"He did a phenomenal job,\" Griffith said of O'Connor. \"And he took control at the end of the game to make sure we were able to seal the victory.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 20,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton Warriors\", \"North Caroline Bulldogs\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Matt Griffith\", \"Mikey O'Connor\", \"head coach Nic McMorris\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 28, North Caroline 21",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Yost's hat trick paces Bucs",
    "date": "2024-09-26",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Yost's hat trick paces Bucs\n\n September 26, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 700\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — It took Kent Island High's field hockey team some time to adjust to life without graduated standout Megan Carpenter last year.Without Carpenter — who was seemingly everywhere on the field — the Bucs dropped their first three games, and were outscored 8-7 through their opening five games.\nThat has not been the case this season.\nKent Island showed its strength on offense, defense and at the midfield Thursday, as Willow Yost notched her first hat trick of the season and Ellie Burnside totaled three assists to fuel a 4-1 victory over Crisfield at Andy Schipul Stadium.\n\"We don't really have a superstar,\" Bucs head coach Laura Harding said after her team improved to 4-1. \"We are a very, very balanced team. And I think last year we lost Megan and it took us a little while to figure it out because she did everything for us. She was on defense. She was on offense. She was distributing the ball all over the field. Everywhere.\n\"So it just took us a little while last year to figure it out, which is why I think we were way stronger at the end of the year,\" added Harding of a team that won eight of its last 11 games. \"And we have a lot of girls on this team that play year round.\"\nHarding may have felt she was watching last year's slow start during Thursday's first half, as the Buccaneers and Crabbers (3-2) were tied at 1-1.\n\"I just felt like we were a little flat in the first half and it just took us a while to get going,\" Harding said. \"Yes, we have a very strong mid(field) and a very strong D. So that's nice. But then we just have to work on getting it in the cage, which we did the rest of the game. We were just flat in the beginning.\"\nYost gave Kent Island a 1-0 lead with 55 seconds remaining in the first quarter, when she scored off Sadie Latchaw's assist. Crisfield struggled to get the ball past Kent Island's midfield through the first quarter and into the second. But with 3:43 left in the half, Emily Engle set up Mya Pendry's tying goal off a corner.\n\"That was a beautiful goal that Crisfield had on that corner,\" Harding said. \"The passing was gorgeous. So kudos to them.\"\nJust a 1:20 later though, Parker Tengwall scored off the first of Burnside's three assists. Tengwall just missed giving Kent Island a two-goal halftime lead when she clanged a shot off the left post with less than a minute left in the half.\nThe Bucs had three corner opportunities to the Crabbers' two in the third quarter, before Burnside set up Yost's second goal for a 3-1 lead with 5:08 to go in the third.\n\"We kept turning over the ball and we kept passing it to the other team,\" Yost said of Kent Island's early struggles. \"But then after the first half we definitely connected better.\"\nThe Burnside-to-Yost combination connected again with a little over 9 minutes remaining off a corner that widened Kent Island's cushion to 4-1.\n\"I definitely think her being in an offensive mid and me in the center, it works really well together,\" Yost said of her scoring connection with Burnside.\nYost thought there was a tendency for the Bucs to crowd one side of the field last season instead of spreading out. That appears to have been fixed this early season, as the Bucs have outscored their first five opponents, 13-3.\n\"I think we have a couple things to clean up,\" Harding said. \"We've been working on both circles. Like I don't think that goal should have happened. And I don't the one against Parkside should have happened. So we have to work.\n\"But the middle of our field is solid,\" Harding continued. \"We just have to work on finishing on the offensive end and not letting those goals in. But way better start than last year.\"\nQUEEN ANNE'S 4 J.M. BENNETT 1CENTREVILLE — Aly Edwards had one goal and an assist, and goalie Cassidy Alexander stopped two shots as the Lions won their fourth straight.\nHaley Cole, Jamie Baldwin and Jolie Leager had one goal apiece for Queen Anne's (4-1) and Ava Fields had an assist. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 26,
    "metadata_sport": "field hockey",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island High School\", \"Crisfield\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Laura Harding\", \"Willow Yost\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Kent Island 4, Crisfield 1",
    "metadata_importance": 6
  },
  {
    "title": "Lions show grit; Vikings off to fast start",
    "date": "2024-10-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions show grit; Vikings off to fast start\n\n October 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 623\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Queen Anne's County High isn't the fastest or strongest football team in the Bayside Conference.But four games into the season, the Lions are playing with a longtime program staple — grit.\n\"They're gritty,\" head coach Brian Mooney said after Queen Anne's — playing its first home game of the season — evened its record last Friday night with a 20-15 victory over James M. Bennett. \"There's no quit on this team. We're not going to quit at any point. Adversity struck and they smiled back and we ended up pulling it out.\"\nAdversity has been stalking the Lions since the beginning of the season.\nStarting right tackle Elijah Goldsborough went down during a Week 1 loss at Stephen Decatur. Starting quarterback Lance Arnold suffered a season-ending injury on the final drive of a Week 2 setback at Chesapeake-Anne Arundel.\nDespite those two injuries, Queen Anne's notched its first win of the season Sept. 19 with a 24-0 shutout of Wicomico.\nBut the Lions lost two more starting pieces on offense. Starting left tackle and team captain Nathan Mayoral got injured last week in practice. Then Drew Sutton, who starts at center and defensive tackle, got injured during Friday's win over the Clippers.\n\"We're shuffling some guys around for sure,\" Mooney said.\nJunior Gannon Brown, who was getting limited playing time, has taken over for Goldsborough at right tackle. Levi Frank has stepped into Mayoral's spot on the left side. And Ethan Wheaton is at center.\n\"We're getting back to playing Queen Anne's football,\" Mooney said. \"We're going to line up and punch you in the teeth. It's a next-man-up philosophy and hats off to (assistant) coach (Donny) Graef doing his job on the offensive line. We've had four different starting combinations on our line now in the last four weeks. We're just trying to get some consistency there.\"\nThen there is sophomore quarterback Carter Hercher, who on Friday carried nine times for 58 yards that included a 3-yard touchdown run and a QB sneak for the two-point conversion.\n\"He was a DB (defensive back) at the beginning of the season and we flipped him into the quarterback role,\" Mooney said of Hercher.\nThe Lions got a huge performance from running back John Connolly, who carried 27 times for 147 yards, including touchdowns runs of 18 and 8 yards. Hunter Kelly had 56 yards rushing on six touches, and Parker Whiteford logged eight tackles.\nUNBEATEN VIKINGSCambridge-South Dorchester on Friday pushed its record to 4-0 for the first time since 2014 with a 23-0 shutout of Colonel Richardson.\n\"To be fully honest I didn't expect us to shut 'em out,\" Vikings first-year head coach Rob Burnham said. \"I hoped to slow them down. I was very pleased with our defensive performance. I know the weather played a role in that, but our kids responded to the challenge that was laid before them, and I'm very pleased with the result.\"\nThe second half was played in a heavy rain, but Cambridge-SD had already built a big enough lead at that point.\nSterling Perkins racked up 175 yards rushing on 14 carries that included first-quarter 73-yard touchdown romp. The Vikings fattened their lead in the second quarter, as senior quarterback Anthony Hughes hit Antonio Blueford with a 44-yard touchdown pass. Hughes later scooped a fumble and returned it for a touchdown, then ran in the two-point conversion on a bobbled snap from center. Taybrion Watts had a safety for the Vikings' other points.\n\"Our kids are playing really hard,\" Burnham said of his team's 4-0 start. \"They bought into what I asked them to do. They're very coachable. They're doing the little things right for the most part, and they recognize where we have deficiencies and we've made improvements. We've tried to get better each and every time we touch the field.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 1,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Queen Anne's County, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County Lions\", \"James M. Bennett Clippers\", \"Cambridge-South Dorchester Vikings\", \"Colonel Richardson\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Brian Mooney\", \"head coach Rob Burnham\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Queen Anne's County 20, James M. Bennett 15; Cambridge-South Dorchester 23, Colonel Richardson 0",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Bennett blanks Queen Anne's",
    "date": "2024-10-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bennett blanks Queen Anne's\n\n October 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 751\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Mike Kern tried stopping them before they started.\"Don't hang your heads,\" Queen Anne's County High's girls head soccer coach instructed his players after Monday night's 3-0 loss to James M. Bennett.\nThough the defending Bayside Conference-champion Clippers had ended the Lions' five-game winning streak, Kern was encouraged by what he saw.\n\"It's great playing against good competition,\" said Kern, whose team had outscored its last five opponents 29-0. \"I thought we played well.\"\nQueen Anne's (5-2) had also come within inches of playing even with Bennett (7-0) until late in the second half.\nChloe Sharp sent a header wide right midway through the first half. Shannon Rath unloaded a rocket into the left side of the net less than a minute later, but it was waved off because it came on an indirect kick.\n\"We had our chances,\" Kern said.\nAnd the Lions would create more chances.\nBut it was the Clippers who were able to finish their opportunities.\nThe first of those came with 15 minutes, 41 seconds left in the first half, when a Bennett corner kick slipped behind Queen Anne's keeper Bea Holthaus. Queen Anne's Ava Bouch converged on the ball between Bennett's Gracie Core and Isabella Wieland. But Core, a freshman striker, arrived at the ball first, and poked the game's first score in with her left foot.\n\"We had two breakdowns,\" Kern said. \"One on the corner kick; we really didn't go to the ball well. The ball bounced around the middle and they snuck it in. It was unfortunate, but we've got to do a better job of winning the ball there.\"\nBennett widened its lead to 2-0 with 4:43 left in the first half, as Riley Matthews gained possession, came down left wing, and angled a hard shot that eluded Holthaus' lunge before settling into the right side of the goal.\n\"We shoot on sight,\" Bennett head coach Ed O'Leary said. \"When we get in front of the goal, when we get a chance, we get on target a lot. And that's what I think the difference was.\"\nThe Lions just missed trimming that two-goal deficit with 3:10 remaining in the first half, when Sharp unloaded a drive that banged off the crossbar, but fell just in front of the goal line.\nSharp rang yet another ball off the crossbar 50 seconds into the second half.\nHolthaus kept it a two-goal game, stopping a Matthews' shot from left wing 3 minutes into the second half, then tracking a 30-yard direct kick for another save less than 2 minutes later.\nMadi Horny had Queen Anne's two last scoring chances, angling a ball from right wing toward the left post that Valenzano smothered with 18:20 left. Valenzano stopped yet another Horney chance a little more than 3 minutes later.\n\"We got to score against good teams,\" Kern said. \"We've got to get the ball in the back of the net. We were creating some opportunities, but we got to finish.\"\nMatthews finished yet another chance with 6:28 remaining, when she got a thru pass from Core, broke down the left side alone against Holthaus and parked a shot into the left side for a 3-0 lead.\n\"That's what we can't have,\" Kern said of Matthews' breakaway. \"We've got to keep them in front, and that's kind of what we stress. We did a good job with that most of the game except for that one play.\n\"But at the end there we pushed Shannon (Rath) up,\" Kern said. \"We had some opportunities. We were attacking and had a lot of momentum going. And then they got that one little breakaway that sealed the game for them.\"\nThe three-goal victory was the Clippers' narrowest margin of victory this season, matching their 4-1 win over Kent Island on Sept. 16. Bennett had outscored its other five opponents, 40-1.\n\"We've kind of had an easy start,\" O'Leary said. \"Kent Island was a tough game for us, but this was our big test. We were looking forward to it. Teams coached by coach Kern, they're always physical and they're always skillful and they're motivated. So for us to come out here and win this. ... I think 3-0 is a bit flattering, but it was a good tough game and we needed it.\n\"It's going to do us a world of good going forward,\" O'Leary added. \"Just proud. They pinned us back into our half a few times and we stood tall and we played hard and we didn't concede a goal and got through. Some days it's your day, and some days it's not. Today was our day.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 1,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
