  {
    "title": "Vikings continue going back in time",
    "date": "2024-10-04",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Vikings continue going back in time\n\n October 4, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 404\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n It took until the middle of the third quarter Thursday night for Cambridge-South Dorchester High's football team to get going.It's taken the Vikings a great deal longer though to reach the midway point of a season with a 5-0 record.\nDown by a point with 9 minutes, 22 seconds remaining in the third quarter, Cambridge-SD closed with a flourish, scoring the game's last 39 points en route to a 46-8 victory over Kent County in Worton, giving the Vikings a 5-0 record for the first time since 2000.\n\"I guess our kids fell asleep on the bus and didn't wake up,\" Cambridge-SD head coach Rob Burnham said of his team's slow start. \"We tried to get there early enough to get them ready to go. It took us being down by a point before they decided, 'Oh, maybe we should go and actually play.' And that was really frustrating for me.\"\nCambridge-SD led 7-0 and was driving early in the third quarter, when Kent County's Jonah Somerville intercepted Viking quarterback Anthony Hughes' pass and returned it for a touchdown. Tyrell Fennell ran in the two-point conversion, nudging the Trojans (1-4) to an 8-7 lead.\nThat seemed to only arouse the Vikings, who got their running game cranked up and took control.\n\"It took awhile,\" Burnham said of his offense finding its groove. \"I'm not sure why, but they figured it out, and then we were able to move the ball, both passing and running. And it didn't really matter who was doing it.\"\nCambridge-SD amassed 379 yards rushing on 35 carries. Leading the way was Hughes, who carried 10 times for a game-high 152 yards, including touchdowns runs of 34 and 20 yards. Running back Sterling Perkins Jr. had touchdown runs of 16, 2 and 25 yards as part of his 13-carry, 132-yard effort.\nWilliam Jackson added a 36-yard touchdown run, and finished with 75 yards on eight carries. And freshman backup quarterback Nate Twilley had a 13-yard touchdown run.\n\"The strength of our team right now is running the football,\" Burnham said. \"So we want to make sure that we can establish the strength of our team and continue to move forward, and do the things that we do.\"\nHughes was 4 of 9 passing for 32 yards, and Twilley completed both his attempts for 33 yards.\nThe 2000 Vikings went 10-0 during the regular season under then head coach David Bromwell before losing to Southern Garrett in the Class 1A quarterfinals. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 4,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Worton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Cambridge-South Dorchester Vikings\", \"Kent County Trojans\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Rob Burnham\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Cambridge-SD 46, Kent County 8",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "RS Shooting Sports celebrates third anniversary with continued focus on youth sports",
    "date": "2024-10-04",
    "author": "ERIC SYLVIA Special to the Star Democrat",
    "content": "RS Shooting Sports celebrates third anniversary with continued focus on youth sports\n\n October 4, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: ERIC SYLVIA Special to the Star Democrat | Section: Business \n \n 437\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CHESTER — Owners of RS Shooting Sports Scott Gray and Robbie Frey invited the community to celebrate the business's three year anniversary and get a glimpse of the future.Joining the celebration at 110 D Country Day Rd. in Chester were coaches and student athletes from three area high schools that Gray and Frey are sponsoring as they compete in the Maryland State High School Clay Target League.\nGray and Frey set out to establish a different kind of gun shop, focused on establishing a community for the shooting sports enthusiasts in the area, and have succeeded in building a thriving business, growing steadily the past three years. Now entering its fourth year at this location, RSSS has expanded, adding a training facility for a myriad of courses.\nIn addition to the classes offered at the store, the shop also offers engraving services, full-service gunsmithing, on-site Cerakote services and Custom Kydex Holsters to fit any firearm. They are now running monthly classes for a number of firearms permits including the Maryland Handgun Wear and Carry Permit.\nThe two owners were happy to help the first Eastern Shore high school clay team — the Kent Island team is recognized as being the first public school in Maryland to join the USA High School Clay Target League and was followed soon after with the establishment of the club at Queen Anne's County High. They helped the team get established, and their commitment to the local shooting community has expanded with the introduction of a clay targets team at Easton High School.\nSpeaking with volunteer coach Joe Cap, he said, \"we have stressed to our team that this is not just about shooting. They are required to attend and participate in community functions.\" Cap continued, \"Just last week they attended the school board meeting, and the students provided them with an update.\"\nIn an email chat with QACHS coach Nathan Powell, he spelled out the underlying strategy, saying, \"The continual development and evolution of our program is building young adults who are active in our communities and developing critical leadership skills to continue to grow and contribute beyond the high school environment.\"\n\"Additionally, we have tapped into a population of student athletes who do not always fit the traditional sports model or mold,\" Powell continued. \"Our program has given them purpose, focus and direction that extends away from the sports environment, into the classroom, the home and the community.\"\nAlso joining in the festivities was Marine Corp recruiter Mark Winkles, who challenged guests with a chin-up challenge. Pupuseria Kent Island was on hand to feed the masses, along with tasty treats from AB Made Sweets and Treats. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 4,
    "metadata_sport": "shooting sports",
    "metadata_story_type": "feature",
    "metadata_location": "Chester, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island\", \"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Easton High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Joe Cap\", \"coach Nathan Powell\"]",
