  },
  {
    "title": "Warriors battle but can’t extend win streak against undefeated Bennett",
    "date": "2024-10-08",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors battle but can’t extend win streak against undefeated Bennett\n\n October 8, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 714\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Avery Brice was eager for Monday night.\"I was very excited about this game tonight just to see where we were at as far as playing against top competition,\" Easton High's first-year boys head soccer coach said of facing undefeated and defending Bayside Conference champion James M. Bennett.\nAfter 80 minutes, the Warriors were where everyone else has been this season after playing the Clippers — behind on the scoreboard.\nAnd yet despite watching Easton's four-game win streak come to a halt in a 3-0 loss, Brice was largely encouraged by what he saw from his team.\n\"Goals change momentum,\" said Brice, who watched the Clippers take a 1-0 lead, when star Noah Blankensop gained possession on left wing, and, with Easton keeper Sam Hutchison far from his goal, sent a ball drifting over everyone before settling into the right side of the net with 15 minutes, 27 seconds left in the first half. \"I loved how we responded. Going down a goal sucks. Guys responded great. Better than we have previously. We get scored on and it's like, 'Wow.' You just see everyone drop. But tonight you saw guys, 'Hey come on. Next play. Let's go again.' I was really proud of them for that.\"\nBrice was also proud of Hutchison's play in goal. The junior keeper stopped a hard shot by Blankensop in the 9th minute and made a save on Sebastien Dorvelus just a moment later to keep the game scoreless. Hutchison then made perhaps his stop of the game, when he punched away a shot that banged off the left post with 7 minutes left in the first half, keeping it a one-goal game.\n\"Goalie had a huge night,\" Brice said of Hutchison, who finished with 11 saves. \"Crazy saves. One of the best games I've seen him play.\"\nBennett (8-0), which has beaten six of its eight opponents this season by six or more goals — the exceptions, Queen Anne's (3-0 on Sept. 19) and now Easton — looked ready to take a 1-0 lead halftime. But with just over 10 seconds remaining, Grant Ziervogel served a ball that Easton's Felix Valentin attempted to head away, only to watch it go into the net for a 2-0 Clippers lead.\n\"The own goal, miscommunication,\" Brice said. \"We learn from it. Felix, he is an amazing defender and I told him, he's saved games prior to this. 'Don't even worry about it. It happens. It's part of the game.'\"\nThe Warriors (6-2) thought they cut their deficit in half with 20:20 left in the game, when Clay Nagel, with Bennett keeper Jake Ziervogel away from the goal and two defenders on his back, caught up to a ball and touched it into the net. But the goal was waved off, when Nagel was ruled offsides.\n\"First competitive (game),\" Bennett head coach Gabe Kane said. \"First one that wasn't decided by halftime.\n\"We don't want 12 strolls,\" said Kane in reference to the Clippers outscoring their opponents, 53-2 this season. \"We want to be battle tested, and Easton came at us for 80 minutes today. There was no quit in them. They seemed to play harder after the third goal. I think they had more chances at the end of the game when I know they were tired. But they kept battling.\"\nBennett bumped its lead to 3-0 as Grason Adkins scored from the right side of the box with 7:26 left.\nJake Ziervogel, who according to Kane has not had more than one save in a game this season prior to Monday, made a leaping stop on Ale Monroy with 6:45 left. Blankensop, who was recruited by a number of Division I schools but is headed to Salisbury University, just missed pushing the lead to 4-0 in the waning moments when he blasted a shot off the crossbar.\n\"I knew they were going to be our best opponent we'd play to date,\" Brice said. \"I think we made some silly mistakes and we paid for them. But they are just minor ones. We switched off for a moment, not marking in the box, not getting and winning the second ball in the box.\n\"The score does not reflect how we played,\" Brice continued. \"I think going forward, we work on finishing our chances and then just being a little more creative up top.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 8,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"James M. Bennett High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Avery Brice\", \"head coach Gabe Kane\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Bennett 3, Easton 0",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "SSPP defeats Herons to strengthen hold on top seed for ESIAC playoffs",
    "date": "2024-10-09",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "SSPP defeats Herons to strengthen hold on top seed for ESIAC playoffs\n\n October 9, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 587\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — With no seniors on her team, Saints Peter and Paul High girls head soccer coach Nicole Edsall called last season a bit of a growing year.Edsall called the beginning of this season, \"a bit of a rough start\" after the Sabres went 2-2 over their first four games.\nNow, with just one regular-season game remaining, Edsall has seen her team grow into the No. 1 seed for the approaching Eastern Shore Independent Athletic Conference playoffs. Buoyed by a dominating midfield, the Sabres tightened their grip on that top spot Tuesday afternoon with a 3-1 victory over Gunston for their fourth straight win, and fifth victory in six games.\n\"Saints Peter and Paul has a great team,\" said Gunston head coach Tony D'Antonio after losing to the Sabres for the second time in five days. \"They're best in the conference for sure. Saints Peter and Paul's midfield was just dominant.\"\nEdsall has literally watched that midfield grow, especially center mids Avery McCall and Annie Albright, who she has coached since fifth grade.\n\"Avery is just extremely athletic, can control the field,\" Edsall said. \"And Annie's got a ton of grit and never stops running. So Annie's usually on an offensive side and Avery really controls the back and the defensive side.\"\nCompleting that midfield are seniors Gracie Galdieri and Keely Morgan.\n\"For the past three years they've been kind of bouncing around,\" Edsall said of Galdieri and Morgan. \"They found homes in the outside, running the flanks. They've done a really good job doing that. They've all grown into the positions.\"\nThat group helped keep the Sabres (7-3 overall, 7-0 ESIAC) control possession, keep play largely in the Herons' end, and limited Gunston's chances on the offensive end. Adding to Sts. Peter & Paul's midfield dominance was the fact D'Antonio had his best midfielder, senior Vee McCluskey, playing goalie.\n\"We need her in goal,\" said D'Antonio, whose team lost to the Sabres 4-0 last Friday in Centreville. \"Our game plan was to kind of pack things in a bit and try to attack on the transition.\"\nThat transition game materialized only a few times compared to the Sabres' attack, which struck in the 18th minute, when Kendall Kroniser crossed a ball that Albright put away in the left side of the net. Sts. Peter & Paul scored again not quite 2 minutes later, as Eden Zimmerman sent a pass across the goalmouth to Kroniser, who tapped it in for a 2-0 lead.\nGunston (5-4-1, 5-3) came close to scoring in the fourth minute of the second half on a direct kick. But Sabres keeper Kaylin Palmer tracked the ball coming in from her right side, watched it bounce through the crease, before smothering it.\nSts. Peter & Paul, which has outscored its ESIAC competition 29-4 this season, was pushing for its fourth shutout in conference play. But with 1:27 left, Gunston's Abby Philippon unloaded a 20-yard rocket from just outside the box that found the upper right corner to make it 2-1.\n\"That's her thing,\" D'Antonio said of Philippon. \"She did not get a whole lot of chances today. They did a nice job. They defended very well.\"\nThe Sabres answered quickly as Eden Zimmerman converted a pass from her sister Emery Zimmerman for a goal and a 3-1 lead just 45 seconds later.\n\"We had a rough start to the season, but we had a tough schedule,\" Edsall said. \"It seemed like we were losing game after game but as soon as we started the conference it clicked. So I'm thankful for the tough start to the season.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 9,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Saints Peter and Paul\", \"Gunston\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Nicole Edsall\", \"head coach Tony D'Antonio\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "SSPP 3, Herons 1",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Queen Anne's extends North shutout streak",
    "date": "2024-10-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Queen Anne's extends North shutout streak\n\n October 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 748\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Easton High's Dafny Vasquez is one of the North Bayside's more dynamic female soccer players.And Queen Anne's County junior center back Shannon Rath is well aware of those talents.\n\"I've played with Dafny most of my childhood, so I'm really close with her,\" said Rath, who used to play on the same club team with Vasquez. \"I think because of that, I know just to contain and keep her in front of me, and don't let her get behind. Because if she gets behind you she's very dangerous.\"\nRath and her teammates kept a close eye on Vasquez Wednesday night, limiting the junior striker to just two shots, as the Lions, paced by Madi Horney's second hat trick of the season, shut out the Warriors, 6-0, for their eighth win in nine games.\n\"We just got done playing Colonel (Richardson) and they have Sadie Prettyman, who in my opinion is a very, very good soccer player, that we knew we had to be aware of her at all times,\" Queen Anne's head coach Mike Kern said. \"So that's kind of what we did tonight.\n\"We didn't mark her up or anything,\" continued Kern, who had no specially designed defensive package just for Vasquez. \"We just made sure we were aware of her when she did get the ball. We looked to double her, just contain her to keep her in front of us. And I think we did a good job doing that tonight because she is a very good player.\"\nTwo days after netting a hat trick against Cambridge-South Dorchester, Vazquez was limited to just two shots on goal. The first of those came in the 15th minute when she fired a strong shot from left wing that Queen Anne's keeper Bea Holthaus stopped. The Warrior standout wouldn't get another shot off until 15:21 remained in the game, when she booted a ball well over the crossbar.\n\"I think they always expect Dafny so they look for that double team on her,\" Easton head coach Ellie Kilmon said. \"I think they did contain her pretty well. But we always expect it. It's just how they (Queen Anne's) work together as a team.\"\nWhile the Lions (8-2 overall, 6-0 North Bayside) stifled Vasquez and Easton (6-4, 4-2), they also maintained their season-long shutout streak against division competition, blanking their seventh North opponent. Though Queen Anne's shut out Kent Island early in the season, it's next Wednesday's home game against the Buccaneers that counts toward in the division, and will determine the regular-season champion.\n\"Our defense has been great all year,\" said Kern, whose team has outscored its division opponents 49-0. \"Bea in the goal has been great. Our defense is very, very good. We probably could score more goals if we didn't put so much effort in the defense.\"\nThe Lions put plenty of effort trying to score in the first half, but were either foiled by Warrior goalie Zelia Proa (10 saves), or launched shots over the crossbar.\n\"The first half we just couldn't get it in the net,\" Kern said. \"So in the second half we did a better job of finishing.\"\nQueen Anne's managed to get two in the net in the first half, the first coming with 6:48 remaining, when Horney set up Olivia Ryan's goal. The Lions made it 2-0 with 3:40 left, after Kara Ringold was taken down inside the box, setting up a penalty kick by Horney, whose shot glanced off Proa's fingertips and into the left side of the net.\nQueen Anne's began widening its lead with 23:50 left in the game, when Rath's corner kick landed right at the feet of Horney, who tapped in her second goal.\nAddison Chandler had a chance to put Easton on the board less than 3 minutes later, but her shot went over the crossbar.\nProa made two more saves midway through the second half before Rath struck a ball that was headed into the net by Horney, completing her hat trick for a 4-0 lead.\n\"I let the play come to me,\" Horney said. \"I don't come into it cocky. I just do it.\"\nThe Lions tacked on two more goals, Ryan setting up Lexi Kern, who hit a beautiful chip from about 20 yards out that sailed over Proa and into the net with 10:31 to go, and Mia Bennett scoring with 6:30 left.\n\"I think we definitely have our ups and downs,\" Horney said. \"But as of right now we're having our ups, and hopefully it keeps going coming into Kent Island next week.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 10,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's Lions\", \"Easton Warriors\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Shannon Rath\", \"head coach Mike Kern\", \"head coach Ellie Kilmon\", \"Madi Horney\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Queen Anne's 6, Easton 0",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Bratten, Rams race past Warriors",
    "date": "2024-10-11",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bratten, Rams race past Warriors\n\n October 11, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 662\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Easton High's football team still has a chance to earn the No. 2 seed for the Class 2A East Region playoffs.But head coach Matt Griffith knows his team has to do better than this.\nParkside running back Karontez Bratten returned the opening kickoff 80 yards for a touchdown, then ran for four more TDs Friday night, leading the Rams to a 37-30 upset of the turnover-plagued Warriors on homecoming.\n\"Not good,\" Griffith said of his team's tackling. \"They've got good athletes, good running backs. We knew that coming in. You can only rep it so much in practice. It's hard when you don't have someone that's like number one (Bratten), number two (Tavian Dashiell), number zero (Canaan Mapp). They're very athletic.\n\"But we hurt ourself tonight,\" Griffith continued. \"Four turnovers; three interceptions, one fumble. Missed field goal. So there's seven to eight extra possessions that we're giving a really good athletic football team. And we can't do that.\"\nBratten took the opening kickoff, veered right and bolted down the visiting sideline to give Parkside (3-3) a 6-0 lead with only 18 seconds off the clock. The point-after kick was blocked.\nParkside's Nazere Sturgis intercepted Easton quarterback Mikey O'Connor (13 of 27, 211 yards, two touchdowns, three interceptions) less than 2 minutes into the second quarter. Three plays later, Bratten found room around right end and was off on a 58-yard touchdown romp to make it 12-0 with 8:44 to go in the second quarter. Easton stopped the two-point conversion attempt.\nThe Warriors (4-2) got on the scoreboard with 4:34 left in the half, when O'Connor hit Zach Barnhart with an 18-yard touchdown pass. Dom Stanco's extra-point made it 12-7.\nBratten returned the ensuing kickoff to Easton's 27. Six plays later, Matt Kramer booted a 25-yard field goal for a 15-7 Rams lead.\nStanco missed a 25-yard attempt with 43.9 seconds left in the half. But on the next play, Easton's Jordan Horsey intercepted Parkside quarterback Chris Morandi and returned the ball 30 yards for a touchdown. O'Connor hit Colin Mooney in the left corner of the end zone for the two-point conversion and a 15-15 tie with 15.6 seconds left before the break.\nBut Bratten (23 carries, 248 yards) again stung the Warriors, finding a seam on the right side and blazing 74 yards for a TD and a 21-15 Rams lead at halftime.\n\"He's getting better every week,\" Parkside head coach Brendan Riley said of Bratten. \"He doesn't need much room, man, and he can frickin find it and he rolls.\"\nParkside recovered an Easton fumble on the first play of the second half, setting up Bratten's 1-yard touchdown run. Harmon ran in the two-point conversion for a 29-15 lead with just under 10 minutes remaining in the third quarter.\nThe Warriors narrowed their deficit to 29-21 with 5 seconds left in the third quarter, when O'Connor swept right and slammed into the end zone with a 2-yard touchdown run. Stanco's PAT pulled Easton within 29-22.\nBratten was sprung for his fifth touchdown of the game, a 37-yard jaunt around the right side with 10:13 left in the game. Harmon handled a bad snap from center and hit Austin Townsend with a two-point conversion pass to make it 37-22.\nEaston closed its deficit again, as O'Connor hit Diontaye Copper on a slant down the middle for a 38-yard touchdown play. O'Connor then connected with Carter Mooney on the two-point conversion pass, drawing Easton with 37-30 with 9:19 to go.\nParkside's running game chewed up almost 6 minutes of clock before a bad snap from center got past Morandi. Barnhart recovered for Easton at the Rams 40. But two plays later, Harmon intercepted O'Connor. The Rams picked up three first downs on the ground to bleed the game's final 3:19.\n\"We've got a really solid core of backs and Karontez ran his tail off. James (Harmon) ran his tail off tonight. Offensive line had a great night up front.\"\nNotes: Parkside finished with 492 yards rushing. ... Parkside's James Harmon carried 11 times for 124 yards. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 11,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Parkside High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Matt Griffith\", \"head coach Brendan Riley\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Parkside 37, Easton 30",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Orrell's overtime goal lifts Warriors over Kent Island in thriller",
    "date": "2024-10-15",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Orrell's overtime goal lifts Warriors over Kent Island in thriller\n\n October 15, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 730\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — Addie Knox paused.Easton High's sophomore midfielder stood on the end line 4 minutes deep into overtime preparing to deliver an entry pass on a penalty corner, when she asked her teammates aligned inside the circle's fringe, \"What are we doing?\"\nLess than a moment later, Knox had her answer: scoring.\nOlivia Orrell scored from just inside the circle Monday night, lifting Easton High's field hockey team to a 1-0 victory over Kent Island at Andy Schipul Stadium — the Warriors' second overtime win in four days.\nAfter Tuesday's games, Queen Anne's (9-1 overall, 4-0 North Bayside) sits atop the division alone in first with Easton (7-3, 3-1) in second and Kent Island (8-3, 3-1) in third. Kent Island could forge a three-way tie atop the North when it hosts Queen Anne's Saturday. But the Lions would likely win the tiebreaker based on a better overall winning percentage.\nFour days after defeating Pocomoke in overtime, Easton and the Bucs needed extra time to settle things, courtesy of some outstanding defense and fine goalie play. Warrior goalie Taylor Bowen (seven saves) faced her heaviest pressure in the first half but let nothing get by her. Easton dominated play in the second half, but couldn't beat Bucs goalie Brynja Petursson (11 saves), who stopped shots sitting and laying down, sending the scoreless stalemate into overtime.\n\"She's a great goalie,\" Kent Island head coach Laura Harding said of Petursson. \"I thought the defense played well. Emma Fleshman stepped up; she was the one playing over Sydney Riska (who was out of town) and did a good job.\n\"We had many opportunities to put it in in the first half,\" Harding continued. \"That's when we had the momentum and couldn't finish. You've got to finish to win.\"\nEaston controlled the second half, and was inches from taking the lead on a corner with 7 minutes left in the third period. But Petursson, with the Warriors poking and trying to jam the ball past her, first made stops while seated, then stretched her body across the goalmouth to keep the game scoreless.\n\"First quarter when they came out I told them we're playing as individuals,\" Easton head coach Kim Neff said of her team. \"And then they started communicating better and playing as a unit. That's when we kind of took over and dominated. But I have to give it to their defense and their goalie because they played a heckuva game. They are hard to get through.\"\nEaston also got some big defensive plays. Stella Kabler poke checked the ball away from Kent Island's Rita Yost on a breakaway, and Myka Motovidlak twice spoiled threats.\nKent Island earned two late corners in the final 2 minutes of regulation, but fired wide of the cage, then watched Easton clear the second attempt, sending the game into the 7-on-7 overtime.\n\"I think we were really determined to get the corner, or at least get the shot off,\" Orrell said. \"We just kept pounding the goal and I got the corner.\"\nKnox set up on the end line to the left of Petursson and directed a pass to Orrell, who took possession, stepped out of the circle, then back in, and smacked a low, hard shot that slid past the right leg of the Bucs' goalie for the game-winner.\n\"I'm like, 'I can do this,'\" Orrell said of the corner. \"I knew it was a good shot. It was a hard hit. And then it went in and made the best sound ever. And then when the ref called it I looked at Addie and the whole team and I was so excited.\"\nWithin seconds, Orrell swarmed on by her jubilant teammates.\n\"It was a great game,\" Harding said. \"Great reffed game. Great played game. Easton ended up wanting it a little more than us in the end. It was clear.\"\nKENT ISLAND 3 KENT COUNTY 1\nWORTON — Rita Yost, Caroline Levin and Audrey Fleshman each scored one goal Tuesday, as the Bucs topped the Trojans.\nEllie Burnside had an assist for Kent Island and Brynja Petursson had three saves.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 7 ST. MICHAELS 0\nCENTREVILLE — Ella Rebstock and Jamie Baldwin netted two goals apiece as the Lions won their ninth straight.\nJordan Bilbo and Haley Cole each had a goal and an assist for Queen Anne's. Aly Edwards added a goal and Ellee McLean an assist. The Lions didn't allow a shot on goal in posting their sixth shutout of the season. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 15,
    "metadata_sport": "field hockey",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Kent Island\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Laura Harding\", \"head coach Kim Neff\", \"Olivia Orrell\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 1, Kent Island 0 (OT)",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Corcoran wins playoff, girls",
    "date": "2024-10-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Corcoran wins playoff, girls\n\n October 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 764\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n GRASONVILLE — She had not placed in the top three in any regular-season event.She didn't have the leader board up on her phone because she didn't want to stress herself out.\nThen, after 18 holes Tuesday, Queen Anne's County High's Keira Corcoran walked to the Prospect Bay Country Club scoreboard and was greeted by a group of team family members.\n\"They told me I was about to go into a playoff because I was tied for first,\" Corcoran said. \"I did not believe them. I had to go up to the scoreboard myself.\"\nCorcoran was surprised when she saw she was indeed tied for first and headed for a playoff with North Caroline's Taryn Brandt. She was even more surprised two playoff holes later, when she made par on No. 18 to win the girls individual championship at the District 8 tournament.\n\"It was absolutely unbelievable,\" Queen Anne's County head coach Brad Niesz said of Corcoran's victory that gave the Lions a sweep of all three titles — Nate Smith winning his third straight boys individual crown while helping Queen Anne's win the team championship. \"I would say that it was something that we knew was within her, but also surprising in that we do have a very talented field of female golfers. And when you combine the North females with the South females, what she accomplished was pretty incredible.\"\nPlaying in separate groups, Brandt fired a 42 over her first nine holes while Corcoran recorded a 43. The Queen Anne's senior then birdied her 17th hole, drawing her even the North Caroline senior. Both then bogeyed their final hole, finishing tied at 85 and creating a playoff.\n\"Through the first nine holes that I played I felt I was playing really good,\" Corcoran said. \"And then I started to get a little bit tired I guess. I felt like I played well, but I didn't feel like I play outstanding.\"\nThen came the discovery there was still more golf to play.\n\"I kind of started getting really nervous,\" Corcoran said. \"I had to go by myself a little bit and I started just chipping and putting to kind of keep myself warm I guess.\"\nCorcoran was still wrestling with her nerves the first playoff hole, the par-4 17.\n\"I was extremely nervous,\" she said. \"I was shaking the entire time. Putting. Everything. When I would put my ball down I was shaking. I just had to contain myself and try to make a decent shot.\"\nBoth players made decent shots to earn par and remain deadlock, forcing the playoff to a second hole, the par-5 18.\nAdmittedly calmer on 18, Corcoran hit a beautiful drive down the middle of the fairway, then drove her second shot 130 yards, putting her 10 yards in front of the water hazard.\n\"I knew that I just didn't want to get into the water,\" Corcoran said. \"I was focusing on the green. The flag was in the back of the green and there's a bunker behind (the flag) so I don't want to go long. I was just focusing on getting it up there. I didn't care if I had to chip. I just wanted it to be there.\"\nCorcoran's third shot traveled about 100 yards, leaving her with a putt in the neighborhood of 20 feet.\nBrandt's third shot caught a piece of a tree and fell into the water's marshy bank, virtually knocking her out of title contention.\nCorcoran's first put stopped three feet shy of the cup.\n\"I knew I could miss this putt, but I knew I could definitely make it,\" Corcoran said. \"Not quite a tap-in, but it was pretty close. I just knew I had to get it into the center of the cup. There was no break. I just needed to aim for the center of the cup.\"\nCorcoran found the center to complete her title run.\n\"I tried to contain myself because I knew that it was a big deal for her as well,\" Corcoran said of Brandt. \"I was extremely excited and I went up and shook her hand and told her she did a great job. And then I wanted to go to coach (Niesz) right away because I knew how excited and nervous he was for me.\"\nCorcoran and Brandt each qualified for the state tournament at the University of Maryland in College Park. The state qualifying cut was 94 for the girls. Also qualifying were Parkside's Addison Apolenis (3rd, 86); Stephen Decatur's Samantha Boger (4th, 87); St. Michaels' Lexi Rockwell (5th, 89); defending champion Brianna Kurtz (6th, 91) from Kent Island; St. Michaels' Lola Browning (6th, 91); and Kent Island's Noelle Agee (8th, 92). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 16,
    "metadata_sport": "golf",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Grasonville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"North Caroline\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Keira Corcoran\", \"head coach Brad Niesz\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Keira Corcoran wins individual championship",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Lions win North despite tie with Bucs",
    "date": "2024-10-17",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions win North despite tie with Bucs\n\n October 17, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 694\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Queen Anne's County High girls head soccer coach Mike Kern didn't pop out of gear or launch into a tirade.But with his team trailing and doing everything but sticking the ball in the net Wednesday night against Kent Island, Kern was a bit more animated than usual as he pleaded for more.\n\"I just told them we had to play harder,\" Kern said. \"We were knocking on the doorstep the entire first half. I thought we dominated the game. We had more opportunities to score and we just couldn't get it in the net. I just told them they had to play with a little more heart.\"\nIn another physical battle that has become commonplace in this rivalry, Queen Anne's got the equalizing goal it needed, when Shannon Rath buried a penalty kick into the right side of the net in the third minute of the second half in an eventual 1-1 double-overtime tie.\nWhile the Lions and Buccaneers each finished the regular season with identical records — 9-2-1 overall, 6-0-1 North Bayside — Queen Anne's wins the tiebreaker between the two schools based on its 1-0 victory over Kent Island on Sept. 11. Though that first meeting did not count toward the North division, it was the deciding factor since head-to-head competition is the first tiebreaker.\nQueen Anne's faces two-time defending conference champion James M. Bennett (12-0) for the Bayside championship on Monday at Snow Hill High School at 6:45 p.m. It will be the fourth consecutive year the Lions and Clippers decide the conference championship. Bennett beat Queen Anne's, 3-0, on Sept. 30 in Centreville.\n\"I thought Olivia (Ryan) played her best game of the season,\" Kern said of Wednesday's clash with Kent Island. \"So I think we're starting to peak a little bit. We're pretty confident. I think going against (head coach) Ed O'Leary's (Bennett) team, they know us well, we know them well. I think it's going to be a good battle.\n\"I think this is one of the more talented teams we've ever had,\" Kern continued. \"I mean we can play with anybody as far from 18 to 18. We've struggled to get the ball in the back of the net against these tougher teams. But we can defend. Bennett when we played them last time, we lost 3-0, but the score didn't depict the game. We lost on the scoreboard. But we got to get the ball in the back of the net.\"\nQueen Anne's had three direct kicks and three corner kicks in the first 17 minutes against the Bucs, but could not beat Kent Island keeper Taylor Ritz. Then with 15 minutes left in the half, Buccaneers speedster Abbie Mahaffey — with Queen Anne's Rath trying to shield her from the ball and Lions keeper Bea Holthaus moving toward the ball from the opposite direction — managed to chip a shot that floated over Holthaus for a 1-0 Kent Island lead.\n\"I think it was just a miscommunication,\" Kern said of the play. \"Shannon running toward her shielding the ball, and Bea coming out. And I think Abbie just made a good play to poke a foot in there and poke it around her. It was just communication.\"\nKent Island, which stretched its unbeaten streak to nine games after a 1-2 start to the season, held that lead into halftime, despite the Lions swarming near the goal with less than 2 minutes left in the first half in a bid for the tying goal and Ritz away from the net. But the Bucs defense denied Queen Anne's, carrying the 1-0 lead into the break.\nThe Lions put immediate pressure on Kent Island early in the second half, sending a header over the crossbar. Then with almost 3 minutes elapsed, Kara Ringold was fouled just inside the box, setting up Rath, who roped a shot into the right side past Ritz's dive.\nTwo yellow cards against the Bucs gave Queen Anne's two more direct-kick chances, but the first sailed wide right and the second was stopped by Ritz.\nThe Lions came within inches of taking the lead when a shot hit under the crossbar and fell in front of the goal with 9:20 remaining in regulation. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 17,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Centreville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Kent Island\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Mike Kern\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "1-1 tie",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "QA's tops Holly Grove",
    "date": "2024-10-17",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "QA's tops Holly Grove\n\n October 17, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: News \n \n 29\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n WESTOVER — Jordan Bilbo, Jamie Baldwin and Ella Rebstock each scored a goal Thursday, as Queen Anne's County High's field hockey team earned a 3-0 win over Holly Grove. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 17,
    "metadata_sport": "field hockey",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Westover, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Holly Grove\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Queen Anne's County High 3, Holly Grove 0",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  },
  {
    "title": "Sabres finish strong on way to crown",
    "date": "2024-10-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Sabres finish strong on way to crown\n\n October 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 767\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Keely Morgan's goal just 2 seconds before halftime put the home team in front.But it was Eden Zimmerman's goal off Avery McCall's beautiful corner kick that put Saints Peter and Paul High's girls soccer team finally in control.\nThat score gave the top-seeded Sabres an infusion of confidence Friday en route to a 4-1 victory over Gunston in the Eastern Shore Independent Athletic Conference championship.\n\"It feels amazing,\" said McCall, a junior midfielder, who has now been a part of conference title teams in soccer, basketball and lacrosse. \"This is a very special team.\"\nA team that had been bounced in the first round of the ESIAC postseason the past two years. A team that went undefeated in conference play this season and came in as the No. 1 seed. And a team that won its first ESIAC championship since 2019.\nBut the Sabres (10-3) were consistently beaten to the ball by the Herons (6-5-1) in the first half, and halfway through the second half.\n\"We were definitely dominant in the first half and the first 20 minutes of the second half,\" Gunston head coach Tony D'Antonio said. \"And then it was pretty even after that. They found the net and we didn't.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul found the net in the 8th minute, when Emery Zimmerman parked a direct kick into the right side of the net for a 1-0 lead.\nGunston got a pair of corner kicks in the 18th minute, the first skidding through the crease but finding no one on the other end. Sabres keeper Kaylin Palmer made a save on a direct kick with 13:30 left in the first half.\nThe Herons then put together a flurry off a corner with 5 minutes remaining before the break. A corner kick sailed over the Palmer's outstretched fingers and through the crease. Gunston kept possession, striking a ball that hit one of its own players before sending a shot over the crossbar.\nThen with 2:04 left before halftime, Ellie Roberts sent a drive from about 25 yards out that nestled into the far right corner for a 1-1 tie.\n\"It's hard to beat a team three times,\" McCall said of the Sabres' two regular-season wins over Gunston. \"But I think we were mostly in panic mode. They (Gunston) always come out really fast and we're normally a second-half team. So we were very defensive and I think that's what saved us. And then eventually we got the offense together and it all came together.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul got a huge boost just 2 seconds before halftime when Morgan got a ball and tucked it inside the left post for a 2-1 halftime lead.\n\"It was absolutely huge,\" Sabres head coach Nicole Edsall said of Morgan's goal.\nSophomore Emery Zimmerman agreed.\n\"I think that was essential because it really just got us going,\" Emery Zimmerman said. \"It (made us) realize, 'OK, we need to win this. We want to win this. This is ours. We came all this way undefeated (in conference play). This is the time that we need to come out and score.\n\"When they caught up to us and we got back up one more like right off the bat, it really got us going,\" Emery Zimmerman continued. \"Gave us a lot of enthusiasm and momentum.\"\nBut not right away.\n\"They definitely had jitters in the first half,\" Edsall said of her team. \"And when we talked at halftime I said, 'I know this isn't the team that we are. So just kind of brush it off. Let it go. Start the half over and let's play our game.'\"\nPlay evened out midway through the second half. Then with 8:42 left, McCall bent a corner that plopped right in front of Eden Zimmerman, who tapped it in for a 3-1 lead.\n\"I've been struggling on my corners all season,\" McCall said. \"I usually just boot it and hope. Eden is always everywhere and she just found it. It got the curve that it needed and she was just there for it. We've always been a team that struggles to like get it in (on corners). It's always gone right through. But she was just right there where she needed to be.\"\nLess than 2 minutes later, the Sabres spilled into the box before Kendall Kroniser gained possession, spun away from a defender and drilled in a fourth goal.\n\"I'm sure it's great for the seniors,\" Emery Zimmerman said. \"I'm happy for them. I feel over the last year nobody leaving (through graduation) and through this year, we really built up on our skills and built great team chemistry. And this is what happens when you work hard at practice.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 18,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Sabres\", \"Herons\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Avery McCall\", \"head coach Tony D'Antonio\", \"Eden Zimmerman\", \"Keely Morgan\", \"head coach Nicole Edsall\", \"Emery Zimmerman\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Sabres 4, Gunston 1",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Core's late goal lifts Bennett to third straight title",
    "date": "2024-10-22",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Core's late goal lifts Bennett to third straight title\n\n October 22, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 682\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SNOW HILL — James M. Bennett High girls head soccer coach Ed O'Leary knew the final of the regular-season match was deceiving.\"When we beat them three-nil in Queen Anne's, we knew it wasn't a three-nil game,\" O'Leary said of the Clippers' Sept. 30 game with Queen Anne's County.\nMonday's rematch was much different, but still the Clippers came out on the winning end, as freshman Gracie Core's goal with 3 minutes, 8 seconds remaining lifted Bennett to its third consecutive Bayside Conference championship via a 3-2 victory over the Lions at Snow Hill High's Kelly Shumate Stadium.\n\"We expected that from Mike (Kern) and Jeff (Anthony),\" O'Leary said of Queen Anne's head coach and assistant. \"First minute they just were on top, and they were so aggressive. And we like that. And they made us fight for everything. You know what? That's the way it should be.\"\nThe first half ended with Bennett (13-0) in a place it had never been all year — trailing.\nWith not quite 2 minutes remaining, Queen Anne's Shannon Rath drove a direct kick that Clippers keeper Sophia Valenzano (six saves) dove to her left to make an initial save on. But the ball trickled to the right of Valenzano who was still on the ground when the Lions' Kara Ringold rushed in to knock in the game's first goal.\n\"We came out and we ended up getting that first goal in the first half,\" Kern said. \"It's what we needed. It was very important because we've struggled scoring against the good teams this year. We've scored a lot of goals for the season, but (in) these big games we're having a tough time putting the ball in the back of the net. So getting that first goal was huge.\"\nQueen Anne's goalie Bea Holthaus (seven saves) kept the Lions (9-3-1) in front early in the second half when she came out to intercept a corner kick headed near her goal line. But less than 3 minutes later, Holthaus made a sliding stop on Core, who quickly possessed the rebound and dished a short pass to Rilynn East for the tying goal.\nHolthaus and Valenzano each made saves on corner kick 3 minutes apart.\nBennett transitioned downfield. Core came down the right side, was bumped by Queen Anne's Hayden Legg, managed to stay on her feet, and steered a shot past Holthaus into the left side for a 2-1 Bennett lead with 20:41 remaining in the game.\n\"Bennett's a strong team and we defended them well for most of the game,\" said Kern of a Clippers team that had outscored the opposition 82-3 this season.\nQueen Anne's wasn't quite through though.\nWith 11:10 left, Madison Horney carried into the box, stumbled slightly through a tackle by Bennett's Taylor Matthews, then while falling, managed to get enough of her right foot on the ball to get it past Valenzano for a 2-2 tie. It was the first time Bennett had allowed two goals in game all season.\n\"We came back, hung tough,\" Kern said.\nQueen Anne's came within inches of taking a lead when it banged shot off a corner went off the crossbar with 6:22 remaining.\nThe game appeared headed for overtime. But with the clock approaching the 3-minute mark, Core collected a ball that glanced off a defender, and from right wing, drove a left-footed shot into the left side of the net for the winning goal.\n\"I hope the crowd enjoyed it because it was a wonderful game,\" O'Leary said. \"We got a little bit lucky in the end. They could have scored but the crossbar. But our super freshman (Core) went and scored a cracking goal at the end.\"\nIt was the Lions third straight loss to the Clippers in the championship game, yet Kern — who told his team afterwards \"This was better than any practice we could have had,\" came away encouraged.\n\"Hit the crossbar on a corner kick and we had bad clear in the back and they capitalized on it,\" Kern said. \"Proud of the girls. Proud how they played. The effort was there. And I think we can build from it moving into the playoffs.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 22,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Snow Hill, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"James M. Bennett Clippers\", \"Queen Anne's County Lions\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Ed O'Leary\", \"Mike Kern\", \"Gracie Core\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Bennett 3, Queen Anne's 2",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Bulldogs survive early strike, oust Easton in quarterfinals",
    "date": "2024-10-24",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bulldogs survive early strike, oust Easton in quarterfinals\n\n October 24, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 448\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n North Caroline High boys head soccer coach John Austin knew things could have gone sideways for his team this season after an 0-4 start.The Bulldogs recovered, winning five of their final eight regular-season games.\nThings could have gone sideways again for North Caroline during Wednesday night's Class 2A East II quarterfinal at Easton.\n\"We really thought whoever scored the first goal was going to win,\" Austin said. \"And they proved me dead wrong on that.\"\nThe fourth-seeded Warriors grabbed an early lead, only to watch North Caroline reel off the next three goals on the way to a 4-2 victory.\nThe No. 5 seeded Bulldogs (6-7) advance to the 2A East II semifinals, where they will play at top-seeded Parkside on Friday at 3:30 p.m.\n\"I'm just proud of the resiliency of the team,\" Austin said. \"They really have battled back to where they are now. To actually watch the joy on their faces playing soccer, I couldn't be more happy for them. They're really understanding what it is now to play high school soccer at a high level. Our play right now is really good. It's the best we've played all year.\"\nEaston (7-6) took a 1-0 lead past the midway point of the first half on Luke Stanco's goal.\nJust 4 minutes later, Lewis Higgs sent a ball across the box that was headed to an unmarked Omar Marroquin, who buried the tying goal.\n\"For them to answer back after giving up one immediately and then score four minutes later was just really big in the game,\" Austin said.\nAyden Russell scored back-to-back goals to push North Caroline to a 3-1 lead. The Warriors cut that deficit to 3-2, when the Bulldogs were called for a handball in the box, setting up Ale Monroy's penalty kick.\nBut North Caroline restored its two-goal cushion, as Marroquin steered a corner kick that Jimmy Cohoon headed into the side of the net.\n\"They're really a resilient group,\" Austin said. \"They are one of the more resilient groups I've had.\"\nMason Shilling had nine saves for North Caroline.\nK.Island 5 WICOMICO 1\nSTEVENSVILLE — Simon Vujinovic scored a pair of goals and assisted on another Wednesday, pacing the Buccaneers in a 2A East II quarterfinal.\nThe third-seeded Buccaneers play at No. 2 seed Queen Anne's on Friday at 5:30 in a semifinal.\nSpencer Chappell, AJ Miller and Robert Simkins each scored for Kent Island, and Eric Hoffman and Brendan Thomas had one assist apiece.\nGIRLS SOCCER EASTON 4 PARKSIDE 3\nEASTON — Addison Chandler scored with 1:30 remaining in the second overtime period Wednesday, as the Warriors rallied from a three-goal deficit to oust the Rams.\nDafny Vasquez scored a hat trick to pull Easton even in regulation. The Warriors advance to play top-seeded Queen Anne's on Friday. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 24,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"North Caroline High School\", \"Easton High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach John Austin\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "North Caroline 4, Easton 2",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Easton's fast start too much for Bennett",
    "date": "2024-10-25",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton's fast start too much for Bennett\n\n October 25, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 418\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — It may have been the most efficient offensive performance produced by Easton High's football team this season.The Warriors scored on all six of their first-half possessions, then scored the first time they had the ball in the second half Friday night en route to a 49-31 victory over James M. Bennett.\nEaston improved to 6-2, strengthening its hold on the No. 2 seed in the approaching Class 2A East playoffs behind undefeated and defending Class 2A state champion Stephen Decatur (8-0).\n\"We did very well in the first half,\" Warrior head coach Matt Griffith said. \"We came out and I thought we did well in the second half, too. I think we went down and scored pretty quickly. Senior night, try to get a lot of players in and do some different things. But no matter who's in the game, we've got to learn to finish.\"\nSenior quarterback Mikey O'Connor's 6-yard touchdown run around left end and Dom Stanco's point-after kick moved Easton to a 49-10 lead with 9 minutes, 20 seconds remaining in the third quarter, setting the running clock in motion.\nThe Warriors carried that 39-point cushion into the fourth quarter before the Clippers (3-5) got a pair of touchdown passes from Liam Nelson as part of a 21-point closing surge. But Easton's opening onslaught proved too much for Bennett to overcome.\nDustin Blue carried 17 times for 115 yards and scored on touchdown runs of 1, 1 and 11 yards in the first quarter to help Easton build a 21-3 lead.\n\"Dustin ran great tonight,\" Griffith said. \"Awesome. Dustin dictated the tempo and how we were going to play physical up front tonight.\"\nEaston's passing game took over from there, as O'Connor connected with Colin Mooney down the center of the field for a 37-yard touchdown strike that extended the Warriors lead to 28-3 just 33 seconds into the second quarter.\nO'Connor, who had 14 carries for a game-high 129 yards and a touchdown, completed 9 of 13 for 120 yards, three touchdowns and no interceptions.\n\"Just kind of taking what they gave us in how they tried to defend us,\" Griffith said.\nChase Raab hauled in a 17-yard touchdown pass with 8:44 still to go in the second quarter, widening Easton's lead to 35-3.\nBennett stopped Easton's scoring spree, when Zakai Smullen hit Jamareon Stevens for a 1-yard touchdown pass on fourth-and-goal with 4:25 left in the first half.\nBut Easton answered, as O'Connor hit Mooney for an 18-yard scoring play, pushing Easton's lead to 42-10 with 1:28 left in the first half. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 25,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"James M. Bennett High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Matt Griffith\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 49, Bennett 31",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Lions serve up 3rd straight title",
    "date": "2024-10-29",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions serve up 3rd straight title\n\n October 29, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 630\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SALISBURY — Queen Anne's County High's volleyball team spent time during the first week of practice in August getting to know new head coach Jamie Leventry.But it was on the first day of practice that Leventry defined the importance of serving.\n\"We talked about it starting Day 1,\" Leventry said. \"'We're going to serve tough. It's what we're going to do.'\"\nThe Lions proved extremely tough from the serving line Monday night, as Emerson Stewart reeled off the team's longest service run of the season during a dominating 25-10, 25-9, 25-5 victory over Parkside that gave Queen Anne's its third consecutive Bayside Conference championship.\n\"There's a reason why their record is what it is,\" Parkside head coach Roy Geiser said after the Lions improved to 15-0. \"Great serving team. Great passing team. And frankly we didn't play our best game. But they put a lot of pressure on us and we weren't prepared.\"\nThe Rams (14-4) got an idea of things to come in Game 1, when Sydney Pinder (eight kills) served four straight points, turning a 5-4 Lions lead into a 9-4 edge.\n\"I feel it gives us such an advantage and I think it helps build up our team's momentum,\" Pinder said of her team's service game. \"It just keeps us up as a team.\"\nQueen Anne's maintained that five-point edge before Kaisa Hickson started a five-point serving spree with one of the Lions' 22 aces, extending the lead to 19-9.\nParkside put down a winner to stop that run before Bianca Baker (team-high 13 kills) closed the first game with a six-point roll from the line to cap Game 1.\n\"It's just a matter of putting a lot of pressure on the other team, and kind of getting them out of system,\" Leventry said. \"Whenever we're back there putting good, tough serves on them and they're struggling to pass those, then that puts us in a really good spot.\"\nJensen Nutley put the Lions in a really good spot, when she strung together eight service points for an 8-0 Queen Anne's lead to start Game 2.\n\"I think that was really important because when you're up by so much, I feel like you don't think about making mistakes so much,\" Nutley said. \"When you're in a neck-to-neck game, like when we play a team like Kent Island, where it's serve and serve back and forth, that takes a lot of effort from us not to make mistakes and think about it a lot. When we're up by that much I feel like it lets us loose and play our game.\"\nA Baker kill and three consecutive service points from Stewart pushed the Lions' lead to 11 at 12-1. Queen Anne's lead remained around 11 points until 20-9, when Stewart again stepped to the line and wedged two more aces into a game-sealing, five-point run.\n\"Climbing back on a team like that you just can't do,\" Geiser said. \"We got to play at our very best and that's not what we did tonight.\"\nParkside's Brylie Geiser served to open Game 3, but Baker unloaded another kill for a 1-0 Lions lead. Stewart moved back to the service line and wouldn't leave until Queen Anne's lead was at 19-0.\n\"My main goal is to keep it in; keep a nice, clean flow at serve,\" said Stewart, whose previous best service run was 13. \"I think it's definitely intimidating when there's a long serving streak, especially with a big game on the line.\"\nQueen Anne's completed its 12th sweep of the season with a kill from Nutley and a Marylee Kline serve that was not returned.\n\"Feels great,\" Leventry said. \"It's been a fun season so far. And like I said, we're not done yet. A state championship is our goal.\"\nIt was the first time Queen Anne's has won three straight conference titles in volleyball. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 29,
    "metadata_sport": "volleyball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Salisbury, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Parkside\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Jamie Leventry\", \"head coach Roy Geiser\", \"Sydney Pinder\", \"Jensen Nutley\", \"Emerson Stewart\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Queen Anne's 25-10, 25-9, 25-5",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Warriors beat clock, Seahawks",
    "date": "2024-10-30",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors beat clock, Seahawks\n\n October 30, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 675\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n It's won on a penalty stroke in overtime.It's also won on penalty corners — once in overtime, once in double OT.\nTuesday evening, Easton High's field hockey team found yet another way to win — and fray their coaches nerves — scoring just before the clock ran dry.\nAddie Knox scored on a corner with 11 seconds remaining in regulation, boosting the Warriors to a 2-1 victory over top-seeded Stephen Decatur in the Class 2A East Region II championship in Berlin.\nEaston (11-3), which has won seven straight, is seeded fourth in the Class 2A state tournament and will host fifth-seeded Hereford (11-3) on Thursday at 3 p.m. in a quarterfinal match.\n\"They never give up,\" Easton head coach Kim Neff said of her team during a phone interview Wednesday. \"They never come off the field thinking like, 'We're done.'\"\nDecatur (11-4) took a 1-0 lead in the second quarter on Jill Burton's goal, and carried that lead into halftime. But the Warriors didn't panic.\n\"I feel like this is our MO really,\" Neff said. \"We're used to coming from behind. We don't stress too much about it. We knew we had plenty of time to get back in that game. And I told them, 'Just go put a goal in right now and score and tie it up.' And sure enough they went and tied it up.\"\nLess than 2 minutes into the second half, Easton's Myka Motovidlak smacked a drive toward the goal that was knocked into the cage by Georgia Spies for a 1-1 tie.\n\"We have faced a lot of challenges this season,\" Neff said. \"Not a lot of teams have. Some teams it comes a little easier to win. I feel running into the playoffs that's an advantage for us. We know we can come from behind. We know we can go to double overtime and win. We've experienced that. I feel like that's an advantage for us.\"\nPlay evened out through the rest of the third quarter. The Seahawks then got a string of corners in the fourth quarter, but could not get anything else past Easton's defense of Bis Johnson, Kadence McGuckin, Lea Boutte and freshman goalie Taylor Bowen (12 saves).\n\"Our goalie saved the day,\" Neff said of Bowen, whose only experience in goal prior to this season was in rec league play. \"Taylor just did a phenomenal job. She's so coachable and she sees the field. And she's so athletic. She just impressed all of us.\"\nEaston survived Decatur's flurry of corners and worked the ball back into the Seahawks end with about 2 minutes remaining. It was then that Neff turned to assistant Katie Spies and said they needed to start thinking about who they were going to put on the field for the first overtime period.\nBut with a little under a minute to play, Easton's Ryan Quicke drew a foul, setting up a corner.\n\"We got in a little huddle right before we got the corner and Stella (Kabler) was like, 'This is it guys,'\" Knox said. \"Then I looked up at the scoreboard and it said 50 seconds left. And when (Neff) called that Liv (Olivia Orrell) was taking the direct shot, I was like, 'This is it.' Because Liv always gets it in there.\"\nKnox inserted the ball off the end line, then moved to the left post. Orrell sent a drive that was stopped by Decatur's goalie. But the ball bounced upward toward Knox, who batted the ball into the cage for a 2-1 Warriors lead.\n\"It was great,\" Knox said. \"Everybody just jumped up. I couldn't even breath.\"\nBut while Easton's players were celebrating, Neff was pleading.\n\"Everybody was losing their mind except me who was saying, 'We still have 11 seconds left,'\" Neff said. \"'Do not let them go down and get in the circle.'\"\nDecatur rushed downfield and got close to the circle before Johnson swept the ball out of bounds, leaving the Seahawks not enough time to retrieve the ball and mount one last threat.\n\"They believe in themselves and they believe in their teammates,\" Neff said. \"It's really an awesome group of girls.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 30,
    "metadata_sport": "field hockey",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Berlin, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Stephen Decatur\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Kim Neff\", \"Addie Knox\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 2, Stephen Decatur 1",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Hughes, Vikings rally past Warriors",
    "date": "2024-11-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Hughes, Vikings rally past Warriors\n\n November 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 885\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — His first touchdown run evened the score in the second quarter.His second TD trimmed the home team's deficit in the third.\nBut it was Anthony Hughes' final touchdown run — a 11-yard sweep around right end with 4 minutes, 23 seconds remaining — that put Cambridge-South Dorchester High's football team ahead to stay Friday night, as the Vikings rallied for a 27-24 victory over longtime rival Easton to cap an undefeated regular season.\nCambridge-SD (9-0) will be the No. 1 seed in the Class 1A East Region playoffs and have a first-round bye. Easton (6-3) slipped to the No. 3 seed in the Class 2A East postseason, and will host No. 6 Parkside on Thursday night in the first round.\n\"Anthony is an absolute dog,\" Viking first-year head coach Rob Burnham said in praise of his senior quarterback. \"He is so physical. He plays outside linebacker and quarterback for crying out loud. I think he tapped out on me for two snaps because he was tired.\n\"I'm so proud of him,\" Burnham continued of Hughes. \"His growth as a player from a ninth grader when he came up to play for us as as young kid to now is absolutely tremendous.\"\nBurnham's stress level may have been reaching the tremendous range in the first half, when the Vikings committed three fumbles — two leading to Easton touchdowns.\nThe Warriors recovered a third fumble when Jordan Horsey stripped the ball from Sterling Perkins at Easton's 2-yard line late in the second quarter. Easton could not convert that miscue into points.\nThe Warriors led 21-14 at halftime, and had a chance to widen that lead when it recovered a fourth Vikings' fumble on the second-half kickoff. Easton drove to the Cambridge-SD 7. But on third-and-goal, Cambridge-SD's Majestic Jones stepped in front of Warrior receiver Chase Raab and intercepted Easton quarterback Mikey O'Connor's pass in the end zone, keeping it a seven-point game.\nOn the very next play, the Vikings lost their fifth fumble on their 20-yard line. The Warriors pushed to the Cambridge-SD 9, where they settled for Dominic Stanco's 26-yard field goal and a 24-14 lead with 8:55 left in the third quarter.\nBut Easton wouldn't score the rest of the game.\n\"We had opportunities to put them away, to increase the gap,\" Easton head coach Matt Griffith said. \"We failed to do that. When you do that against a good team this is what happens. It comes back and bites you.\"\nEaston got the ball back after a Vikings' three-and-out. But on second down, Jayden Hollowell intercepted O'Connor, giving the Vikings possession on their 44.\nBack-to-back false starts pushed Cambridge-SD back to its 34, where on first-and-20, Hughes found a seam and trucked 66 yards for a touchdown, drawing the Vikings within 24-20 with 6:28 left in the third quarter. Cambridge-SD bobbled the snap on the point-after attempt, and tried a pass, but was called for intentional grounding.\nKimir Lake picked off O'Connor to end Easton's next possession, but the Warriors forced a punt and carried their four-point lead into the fourth quarter.\nEaston looked ready to potentially add to its lead driving from its 25 to the Vikings 26. But on second-and-10 Lake intercepted O'Connor again and returned the ball to the Warrior 40. Hughes had runs of 8, 7 and 17 yards before turning the corner for an 11-yard touchdown run with 4:23 left. Collin Bair's point-after kick made it 27-24.\n\"I got to get it,\" said Hughes when asked what he was thinking on his final scoring run. \"I've got to do it for my coach, my line. My teammates trust me so I got to go do it for them.\"\nEaston punted on its next drive. Cambridge-SD kept the ball in Hughes' hands for the better part of the final minutes before punting the ball back to Easton with 34.7 seconds left. But the Warriors couldn't mount a last threat, as Brady Walls' fourth-down heave was intercepted by Lake. Hughes took a knee to end the game.\nEaston took a 7-0 lead when O'Connor hit Raab with a 27-yard touchdown pass and Stanco hit the PAT with 9:56 left in the first quarter.\nCambridge-SD answered, as Perkins was sprung for a 55-yard touchdown run. Bair's extra point tied the game 7-all with 8:53 to go in the first.\nThe Warriors capitalized on a Vikings fumble, as O'Connor scored on a 2-yard run for a 14-7 lead with 38.2 second left in the first quarter.\nHughes responded with a 58-yard touchdown run just 52 seconds into the second quarter to tie the game at 14-14.\n\"He's good,\" Griffith said of Hughes. \"We knew coming in he's good. (Number) One and two (Perkins), very, very good. They're a load to tackle. Two is very shifty. One is just a man.\"\nEaston moved back in front with 7:17 left in the second quarter on O'Connor's 8-yard touchdown run.\n\"Our defense is pretty stout,\" Burnham said. \"I think that's the most offensive points we've given up on the season. The O'Connor kid, he's a tremendous football player. Coach Griffith had a great game plan. They did a lot of really good things in the box, attacking downfield with switch routes and different combinations. And I would not want to play them again. I'm glad they're in the 2A and we're in the 1A because I don't want any part of the Easton Warriors right now.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 1,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Cambridge, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Cambridge-South Dorchester High\", \"Easton High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Rob Burnham\", \"head coach Matt Griffith\", \"Anthony Hughes\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Cambridge-SD 27, Easton 24",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Lions prepare for first state semifinal since 2004",
    "date": "2024-11-05",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions prepare for first state semifinal since 2004\n\n November 5, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 618\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Lexi Kern was going to get a turn regardless.\"The whole team knows Lexi does well with PKs,\" Queen Anne's County High girls head soccer coach Mike Kern said of his daughter.\nCoach Kern sent his 11 players onto the field for a shootout to decide last Friday's Class 2A state quarterfinal against Wilde Lake, but noticed his third and fourth shooters had bumped themselves further back in the lineup. After Shannon Rath and Morgan Scott each buried their PKs, Kern watched his daughter move into the third spot and step behind the ball.\n\"She said, 'OK, I'll go,'\" the coach said.\nLike Rath and Scott, Lexi Kern parked her PK in the right corner, then watched Wilde Lake's third attempt miss the mark, sealing a 1-0 victory that gave the Lions their first state semifinal berth since 2004.\nSecond-seeded Queen Anne's (12-3-1) advances to Saturday's 2A state semifinals, where it will meet No. 3 seed Century (12-2-1) at 2:30 p.m. at Kent Island High School in Stevensville.\n\"That was kind of a cool moment for me,\" Mike Kern said of Lexi's PK. \"Nobody else wanted it, which is the crazy part. Lexi is usually four or five or six (in the PK lineup). They just did not want to take it.\"\nThe Lions followed a familiar pattern against the seventh-seeded Wildecats (7-6-3), dominating play without benefit of a goal — though one score was negated by a questionable offsides call.\nAfter two overtime periods, the game moved into penalty kicks.\n\"I was surprised how calm I was,\" said Mike Kern, who noted an eerie quiet fell over Lions Stadium during the shootout. \"It was like golf. You could hear a pin drop. Both teams. After they made them they cheered. But it was quiet as could be. I had never experienced anything like that.\"\nQueen Anne's went first, with Rath driving a shot into the right side of the net. Wilde Lake's first PK sailed over the crossbar.\nCelebrating her 18th birthday, Scott deposited her PK in nearly the exact same spot at Rath's shot. The Wildecats' second attempt went wide. Lexi Kern promptly booted her shot into the right corner for a 3-0 lead. Wilde Lake's third — and final chance — again went well over the head of Lions keeper Bea Holthaus and the crossbar, clinching the victory.\nThis is the Lions' third trip to the state semifinals under their 24-year head coach. Queen Anne's last reached the state semis as a 1A school in 2004, when it lost in the closing minute of double overtime to Tuscarora. Queen Anne's also reached the state semifinals in 2003, outlasting Oakland Mills in an 11-man shootout before falling in the state final.\nKern and longtime assistant Jeff Anthony have built solid teams in the past, but often were eliminated by 2A powers Fallston and Bel Air.\nBut this season, Kern thought he had the goods to make a return trip to the final four.\n\"I really thought this team had the chance to get there,\" Kern said. \"I think they're competitors. They love to compete. Even at practice with each other, they don't like to lose. And I think they accept challenges. They've been together for so long and played together for a long time. And I think they feed off each other. I think that they're competitors is the biggest thing.\"\nQueen Anne's has posted 10 shutouts this season, and now faces a Century program that won the state title just two years ago. Since losing their first two games of the season by a combined score of 7-0, the Knights have gone 12-0-1 and logged 11 shutouts, including 10 straight.\n\"They very defensive,\" Kern said of the Knights. \"They are very similar to us. I think we've got a legitimate shot.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 5,
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "preview",
    "metadata_location": "Queen Anne's County, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Century\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Mike Kern\", \"Lexi Kern\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
