  },
  {
    "title": "Top-sdded Mavs oust Easton",
    "date": "2024-11-07",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Top-sdded Mavs oust Easton\n\n November 7, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 650\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n GLEN BURNIE — It had found multiple ways to win since early October.Easton High's field hockey team had won in overtime and double overtime. It had won with 11 seconds remaining in regulation. It had won on a penalty stroke and in a 1v1. And it continued to find paths to victory no matter how big the game.\nBut Wednesday night against top-seeded Manchester Valley, the Warrior offense rarely found its way into the circle and had its storybook season ended in a 3-0 loss to the Mavericks in a Class 2A state semifinal at Glen Burnie High School's Pop Whayland Stadium.\nManchester Valley (16-0) advances to Saturday's 11 a.m. Class 2A state championship game where it will meet three-time defending state champion Glenelg (16-0) at Stevenson University's Mustang Stadium.\n\"I'm so proud of them,\" said Easton head coach Kim Neff, whose team had its eight-game win streak ended, finishing the year at 12-4. \"Taylor Bowen again had so many shots on her that she was able to save. They battled many corners clear. It was a defensive-heavy game and they handled it very well.\"\nIndeed, the Mavericks, who had blanked their previous three playoff opponents by a combined 27-0, were held to their lowest goal total of the postseason.\n\"Their whole team, they played hard,\" Manchester Valley head coach Denean Koontz said. \"They did a very nice job of doubling us. They took us out of our game plan a little bit. They stepped to the ball very well. Defensively, they were on us everywhere. They did a very nice job of smothering the ball and smothering our players.\"\nThe Mavericks finished with 11 corners before Easton earned its first with only 1 minute, 26 seconds remaining in the game.\nBy then the No. 1 seed had already built a three-goal cushion that was started off a pair of first-quarter corners.\nBowen, Easton's freshman goalie, and her defense weathered Manchester Valley's first two corners in the opening 5 minutes. The Mavericks were awarded their third corner less than 3 minutes later and converted, as Lily Brookhart scored off Shelby Wurzburger's assist for a 1-0 lead.\nManchester Valley earned its fifth corner with 13 seconds remaining in the first quarter, and again capitalized, with Allie Largent setting up Brookhart's second goal with no time showing on the clock.\n\"They were aggressive,\" Neff said of Manchester Valley, which held Easton scoreless for the first time since a 1v1 loss to Queen Anne's on Oct. 1. \"And their stick skills were incredible.\"\nBowen made a save on the Mavs' first corner of the second period, and watched her teammates foil a second corner near the end of the period.\nBut Easton generated little offense, the long rushes downfield by Olivia Orrell and Addie Knox were often and quickly stopped well short of the circle.\n\"I do feel like my girls held their own,\" Neff said. \"And I think this is where it really comes into play not having a turf field, and not being able to practice on a turf field, and play on a turf field. It's hard to keep up with the competition. Their whole team was just fast.\"\nManchester Valley picked up three more corner chances in the third, but could not add to its lead as Bowen registered two more saves on corners.\n\"Our defense, our middies, they all stepped up,\" Neff said. \"I will say I feel like each quarter we came out a little more aggressive cutting to the ball better. I feel if we had one more quarter you might have seen maybe a goal.\"\nManchester Valley put away one more goal with only 4 seconds left on the third-quarter clock, when Carroll County scoring leader Amanda Herrold scored on a breakaway.\nThough they continued battling, the Warriors mounted few threats and got their first corners in succession with 1:26 left, when Mavericks' goalie Makayla Shoaf registered her lone save.\n\"They played hard,\" Koontz said of Easton. \"They did a great job.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 7,
    "metadata_sport": "field hockey",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Glen Burnie, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Manchester Valley\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Kim Neff\", \"head coach Denean Koontz\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Manchester Valley 3, Easton 0",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "K. Island topples Q. Anne's for title",
    "date": "2024-11-08",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "K. Island topples Q. Anne's for title\n\n November 8, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 149\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Gianna King had 16 kills, 11 digs and five aces Wednesday, and sophomore Madison Parks had the game-winning block in the fifth game, as Kent Island High's volleyball team defeated top-seeded and unbeaten Queen Anne's County, 25-18, 19-25, 25-17, 23-25, 15-10 to win the Class 2A East Region II championship.Kent Island (14-2) will be seeded second in the Class 2A state tournament and host No. 7 Williamsport in a quarterfinal on Saturday at 11 a.m.\nMacayla Kirk finished with 11 kills, 10 digs and one ace for the Buccaneers, who lost both regular-season matches to the defending region-champion Lions. Alaina Dlabich totaled 16 assists, nine digs and two aces, and Payton Goldfein netted eight kills and a block.\nKent Island also got contributions from Abbigail Fisher (six digs, three aces, assist), Parks (four blocks, kill), Lauren Wigglesworth (three kills, two blocks) and Madison Saville (five digs, four aces). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 8,
    "metadata_sport": "volleyball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Centreville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island High\", \"Queen Anne's County\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Kent Island 3, Queen Anne's 2",
    "metadata_importance": 9
  },
  {
    "title": "Bucs can't sustain momentum against Wildcats",
    "date": "2024-11-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bucs can't sustain momentum against Wildcats\n\n November 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 693\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — It had cut its deficit in half, rallying and grinding to a Game 3 victory.Playing on its home floor, Kent Island High's volleyball team had momentum, and may have even felt it had gained a bit of traction. But that momentum was quickly extinguished Saturday, as Williamsport built an early Game 4 lead it steadily expanded on the way to sealing a 25-16, 25-15, 23-25, 25-9 victory over the Buccaneers in a Class 2A state quarterfinal.\nNo. 7 Williamsport (12-5) advances to Tuesday's 2A state semifinals at Watkins Mill High School in Gaithersburg, where it will face third-seeded Calvert (15-2) at 5 p.m.\n\"That third set, just getting that little bit of momentum, and placing the ball and playing a little bit smarter, that's what won us the victory there,\" Kent Island first-year head coach Brittney Guy said.\nDespite pulling within 2-1, Guy reminded her team, \"That set's over with. You've got to move on. We've got to play composed and play our game.\"\nBut the Wildcats recaptured momentum early in the fourth set, getting a side out before Kennedi Lawhorne reeled off three consecutive service points for a 4-0 lead.\n\"We kind of got stump on our serve-receive,\" Williamsport head coach Karen Hampton said of the Game 3 loss. \"They were serving us really tough. So once we were able to kind of fix that we kind of got back on track.\n\"We definitely wanted to start out with a lead,\" Hampton said of Game 4. \"We were receiving serve, so our goal was to side out on that very first ball so we could get it and try to get the momentum early. And we were able to do that.\"\nThe second-seeded Bucs (15-3) stopped that early run via Macayla Kirk's kill to make it 4-1. An errant Kent Island return made it 8-3, bringing Skylar Norris to the service line, where she remained until delivering an ace that punctuated a 10-point service streak for an 18-3 Williamsport lead.\n\"Coming into the fourth set, energy was low,\" Guy said. \"Communication was low on the court. We came back a little bit. They dug deep, but it was just a little too late in that fourth set.\"\nKent Island never had back-to-back service points in Game 4, as Norris' kill on the final point sealed the 25-9 victory and the Wildcats' 26th trip to the state semifinals.\n\"We had a bus ride; a long ride,\" Hampton said of her team's trip from Washington County. \"So we had to kind of get loose a little bit. But once we found our groove and we found our rhythm, I felt like we were able to execute what we needed to.\"\nThat was apparent in Game 1, as a game-opening side out, coupled with Alaina Dlabich's four-point service run, staked Kent Island to 5-0 lead. The Bucs clung to their lead until a net return on a serve by Williamsport's Elizabeth Warren forged the game's first tie at 14-all. It remained tight until the Bucs struggled returning Norris' serve, helping the Wildcats score the final nine points of Game 1 for a 1-0 lead.\nGame 2 was also tight early, but again Williamsport closed strong, netting the last 10 points for a 25-15 win.\n\"We missed a few more serves than we normally do,\" Guy said. \"Just the technique-wise, when you come into games like this with teams like this, everything technically has to be right. You got to be ready in the right defensive spot. I don't think we completely gave it to them, but we could have played a little bit more diligent ball. The opposing team, they played very smart.\"\nWarren's five-point service run helped the Wildcats to a 7-2 lead in Game 3. But in a game that included seven ties — the last at 20-20 — Kent Island took a 21-20 lead on Dlabich's block. Williamsport hit an errant return on a Gianna King serve to make it 22-20. Kirk's block and a returning error moved the Bucs to a 24-21 lead. Williamsport pulled within 24-23 before Kirk drove a winner into a vacant left corner for a 25-23 Bucs win — giving Williamsport its first loss in three postseason matches. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 10,
    "metadata_sport": "volleyball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Kent Island Buccaneers\", \"Williamsport Wildcats\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Brittney Guy\", \"head coach Karen Hampton\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Williamsport 25-16, 25-15, 23-25, 25-9",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "North Dorchester's deepest run ends",
    "date": "2024-11-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "North Dorchester's deepest run ends\n\n November 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 557\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n GAMBRILLS — Carol Hubbard knows it takes time to build programs.In the spring of 2023, Hubbard guided North Dorchester High's softball team to its first Class 1A state championship.\nHubbard guided the Eagles to yet another first this year, leading the volleyball team to a Class 1A state semifinal berth for the first time in the program's 11-year history.\nThat bid for a state crown ended though Tuesday night at Arundel High School, as second-seeded Boonsboro swept No. 6 seed North Dorchester, 25-12, 25-6, 25-13 in that 1A state semifinal.\nThe Warriors (16-3) will play fifth-seeded Clear Spring (14-5) at noon on Saturday for the 1A state championship at Harford Community College's APG Federal Credit Union Arena.\n\"It was a huge accomplishment for them to get to this point,\" said Hubbard, whose team had a five-match win streak ended, finishing the year 12-7. \"This is the farthest that any team has gone in our program.\"\nNorth Dorchester scored the first point of the night, and stayed within two to three points of the Warriors early. But a four-point service run from Larissa Blair, that included a pair of kills from Rosslyn Briggs, helped give Boonsboro separation in Game 1 at 15-6. Another Briggs kill and an ace from Aly Phillips extended the Warriors lead to 19-8. Back-to-back errant returns by the Eagles closed the 25-12 set.\n\"Our game plan coming in is to always serve tough,\" Boonsboro seven-year head coach Shelby Wallace said. \"But we had a few select styles of serve that we wanted to execute today and I think we did that. Mostly the deep float and some short serves where appropriate.\"\nThose service combinations fueled Boonsboro's most dominant set of the match, as Devin Glines served 10 straight points before Blair reeled off a five-point run in Game 2.\n\"Serve-receive struggled,\" Hubbard said of her team. \"And then when we did get a decent pass we weren't able to capitalize on it on the front row. So it was just kind of a snowball. I think the front row was getting frustrated with the back row some, and then the back row would finally get (the ball) up and they were getting frustrated with the front row.\"\nA service error and back-to-back points off Katelyn Hopkins' serve gave North Dorchester a 4-3 edge in Game 3. Boonsboro rallied to a 9-5 lead. Brooke Outten answered, coupling a kill with consecutive aces to draw the Eagles within 9-8.\nBut Boonsboro steadily began widening its lead. A Warrior kill and two return errors bumped the gap to 13-8. Brooke Iager then delivered back-to-back aces to open a five-point service spree that gave Boonsboro a 19-9 lead. North Dorchester kept its deficit under 10 before Anna Jackson's kill and two errant returns capped the 25-13 Game 3 win.\n\"The third set early on we had a little run there and evened it up,\" Hubbard said. \"I tip my hat to them. They are a strong team.\n\"As far as an all-around balanced team, I would say they're the best that we've seen,\" Hubbard added of Boonsboro, which advanced to its first state final since 2007. \"They didn't have a weak spot. They were strong all the way around. You play some teams and they have a really strong outside hitter, or a really strong middle hitter. I would say they were as well-rounded a team as we've faced.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 13,
    "metadata_sport": "volleyball",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Dorchester, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"North Dorchester High School\", \"Boonsboro High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Carol Hubbard\", \"head coach Shelby Wallace\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Boonsboro 3, North Dorchester 0",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "O'Connor, Easton top Kent Island",
    "date": "2024-11-15",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "O'Connor, Easton top Kent Island\n\n November 15, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 669\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — Kent Island High head football coach Zach Davis has seen enough of Mikey O'Connor.\"I'm glad he graduates,\" Davis said of Easton High's senior quarterback. \"He basically beat us by himself. I told him before the game, I thought he was a great player. And he showed it tonight.\"\nO'Connor ran for two touchdowns and threw for two, including a 43-yard connection to Diontaye Copper with 2 minutes, 15 seconds remaining Friday night, as the third-seeded Warriors defeated the No. 2 seed Buccaneers, 28-21, in a Class 2A East Region second-round playoff at Andy Schipul Stadium.\nAfter going 1-9 a year ago, Easton is 8-3 and now awaits for the state tournament to be re-seeded for next weekend's state quarterfinals.\n\"We didn't score the whole second half,\" said O'Connor, whose 3-yard touchdown run with 1:17 left in the first half helped Easton to a 21-14 halftime lead. \"They were really stopping us and we were just thinking, 'First down. Next first down.' So we were taking it one play at a time. And when you do that, you put a bunch of good plays together, and you bust one, that really turned the tide.\"\nAfter a scoreless third quarter, Kent Island (7-4) tied the game 4 seconds into the fourth quarter on quarterback Gavan Henry's 1-yard sneak. Spencer Chappell's point-after kick tied the game at 21-all.\nEaston's ensuing possession ended with a fourth-down incompletion. Kent Island punted the next time it had the ball.\nThe Warriors took possession at their 20-yard line with 6:17 remaining. On third-and-11, O'Connor hit Colin Mooney for an 11-yard pick-up and a first down. Five consecutive O'Connor runs set up a third-and-five at the Bucs' 43. O'Connor hit Copper on an out route, then watched his receiver cut toward the middle of the field and outrun everyone into the end zone for a 27-21 lead. Dominic Stanco drilled his fourth straight point after for a 28-21 Easton lead.\n\"You try to stop him running the football and he throws on you,\" Davis said of O'Connor.\nKent Island got the ball back on its 37 with 2:15 left. As the Bucs lined up on first down, Easton called time to make a defensive adjustment. On the next play, Copper intercepted Henry, giving the Warriors the ball at the Kent Island 49 with 2:09 left.\n\"Luckily for us we were able to make one more big play than Kent Island tonight, on both sides of the ball,\" Easton head coach Matt Griffith said. \"Big drive. Drove down and scored with like 2:15 left. And then get a huge pick. Call a timeout to see the formation, made a quick adjustment on our coverage, and, man, played it perfect.\"\nO'Connor picked up two first downs to kill the remaining 2:09, cementing the victory.\n\"They came through when they needed to and that was at the most important time,\" O'Connor said of his offensive line. \"When we got those first downs, it was all them. Like I didn't get touched literally until I had the first down.\"\nEaston took a 7-0 lead, when O'Connor busted loose around right end for a 41-yard touchdown just over 2 minutes into the game. Stanco added the PAT.\nKent Island fumbled on its first play of the game, giving Easton the ball on the Bucs 45. The Warriors drove to the Bucs' 13, setting up Stanco's 30-yard field goal attempt that drifted wide right.\nHenry's 1-yard sneak and Chappell's extra-point tied the game at 7-7 with 32 seconds left in the first quarter.\nThe Warriors moved back ahead with 8:50 to go in the second quarter, when O'Connor hit Mooney with a 23-yard touchdown pass in the left, front corner of end zone for a 14-7 lead.\nThe Bucs again pulled even, as Nate Green was sprung for an 11-yard touchdown run with 2:26 to go before halftime.\nEaston responded. Dustin Blue broke free on a 50-yard run on his first carry. O'Connor carried for 12 yards and then raced around left end and squeezed down the sideline for the go-ahead touchdown and a 21-14 halftime lead. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 15,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Stevensville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton Warriors\", \"Kent Island Buccaneers\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Zach Davis\", \"Mikey O'Connor\", \"head coach Matt Griffith\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Easton 28, Kent Island 21",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Blueford, Vikings rise up to get past stubborn Colonels",
    "date": "2024-11-20",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Blueford, Vikings rise up to get past stubborn Colonels\n\n November 20, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 533\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Colonel Richardson High head football coach James Jackson didn't think Cambridge-South Dorchester was 23 points better than his team in the fourth week of the season.The Colonels certainly proved that last Friday night, as they battled the top-seeded and undefeated Vikings down to the wire in a Class 1A East Region second-round playoff. But with 1 minute, 15 seconds remaining, senior wide receiver Antonio Blueford made a leaping grab in the back of the end zone on Anthony Hughes' pass for the winning touchdown, lifting Cambridge-SD to a 14-7 victory.\nThe top-seeded Vikings (10-0) will host three-time defending Class 1A state champion Fort Hill (4-7) on Friday at 6:30 p.m. in a 1A state quarterfinal. The Sentinels, who forfeited five victories this season for using an ineligible player, outscored their two regional opponents by a combined 103-20.\n\"Even though our season ended, the way we played, and Cambridge was the number one undefeated team (in the 1A East Region), I couldn't be more proud of our guys the way they stepped up,\" said Jackson, whose team lost 23-0 to the Vikings during the regular season. \"We showed we shouldn't have lost that game by 23 points. We should have been in that game, but we didn't play to our potential. So when we play to our potential I feel like we can play with anybody.\"\nCambridge-SD took a 7-0 lead in the first quarter when Hughes' broke up the middle for a touchdown run and Collin Bair tacked on the extra point.\nColonel (6-5) answered in the second quarter, when Ja'Zir Dickerson (18 carries, 82 yards) ran off right tackle for a touchdown run inside run 10-yard line. Morgan Demir added the extra point.\nAn interception by Colonel's Jay Wilkins kept the game tied at 7-7.\nIt remained that way until late in the fourth quarter, when Cambridge-SD drove inside Colonel's 5-yard line. The Vikings were backed up after getting tagged with an unsportsmanlike conduct penalty. But Hughes then dropped back and arced a pass that Blueford jumped and came down with despite good coverage.\n\"It was just a great ball,\" Blueford said. \"I just went up and got it and the game was on the line. I knew it was going to be a good pass. I just had to come down with it.\"\nIt was the lowest point total of the season for the Vikings.\n\"We had opportunities,\" Cambridge-SD head coach Rob Burnham said. \"Right before the half we got a short field and got inside the 5 and then ended up kicking a field goal off the left upright and missing it. Then early in fourth quarter we got inside the 20 and threw an interception.\n\"They played well and tackled well and took some things away that they gave us last game,\" Burnham added of Colonel. \"We took some things away that we gave them last game. They played better offensively than they did the first time. A win's a win and we'll take it.\"\nT.J. Wenger had 14 tackles for Colonel, Chatydrick Pickrom had 13, Collin Brewer nine, and Chris Rockwell eight.\n\"I thought our defense held up,\" Jackson said. \"Even on the last play we were in perfect position. On that play Blueford just made a great catch.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 20,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Cambridge-South Dorchester Vikings\", \"Colonel Richardson Colonels\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach James Jackson\", \"Antonio Blueford\", \"head coach Rob Burnham\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "playoffs",
    "metadata_outcome": "Cambridge-SD 14, Colonel Richardson 7",
    "metadata_importance": 8
  },
  {
    "title": "Vikings face Fort Hill in quarterfinals instead of title bout",
    "date": "2024-11-21",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Vikings face Fort Hill in quarterfinals instead of title bout\n\n November 21, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 798\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n With the exception of a few injuries, and the assorted turnovers and penalties that leave coaches muttering, the season has gone pretty much as planned for Cambridge-South Dorchester High's football team.\"We had talked all season,\" first-year Vikings' head coach Rob Burnham said, \"about doing the things we're supposed to do, and being successful, and work hard, and improve day after day. Get through the season undefeated. Get the number one overall seed in the (Class) 1A, and then we won't play the Sentinels until we go to Annapolis. Obviously, things changed on that.\"\nCambridge-SD did go undefeated during the regular season and earned the No. 1 overall seed when the state playoffs were reseeded last week.\nAs for meeting three-time defending 1A state champion Fort Hill in a potential state-title showdown in Annapolis? Well, that changed when the Sentinels forfeited five regular-season games for using an ineligible player. Fort Hill remained eligible for the postseason though, romped to a pair of regional victories, and come into Friday's 6:30 p.m. state quarterfinal at Cambridge-SD (10-0) as the No. 8 seed at 4-7.\n\"I met with our guys and said, 'Obviously the circumstances have changed and what we worked hard for isn't necessarily going to be given to us,'\" Burnham said. \"'We still have to go out and take it. So in order to get what we want we have to beat them. We just have to do it two weeks earlier than we had planned on.'\"\nA Cambridge-SD assistant last year, Burnham and Fort Hill head coach Zack Alkire agreed this is not the same Sentinels team that routed the Vikings, 38-20, in last year's state semifinals in Cumberland.\n\"We have a much better shot to run the ball on them this year than last year,\" Burnham said. \"I've coached for 26 years and that was the best high school team I've seen regardless of classification in three different states. They were really good.\"\nBut that team that went on to win a third straight state title while extending its winning streak to 21 games, graduated 20 players, and is not as senior heavy this year.\n\"We play 10 sophomores throughout the course of a game and they've done really well for us,\" Alkire said. \"There's been some growing pains. But the seniors have shown good leadership and gotten those guys to get us to where we are today.\"\nOne thing hasn't changed though with the Sentinels.\n\"Still the same ground-and-pound style of offense,\" Alkire said. \"We try to spread you out when we can, but it's mostly going to be under center, ground and pound.\"\nLeading that rushing attack is senior running back Jabril Daniels, who has toted the ball 156 times for 1,664 yards and 26 touchdowns this season. Braelyn Younger (43-485-9) is also a threat. And though senior quarterback Nash Cassell, who has completed 13 of 27 passes for 276 yards and six touchdowns, isn't a huge running threat, Alkire said he \"does a great job of getting the ball where it needs to be.\"\nThat offense faces one of the Bayside Conference's quickest defenses.\n\"They're very aggressive defensively; try to cause problems in your backfield,\" Alkire said of Cambridge-SD.\nBut Burnham knows the unit he's called the backbone of his team since August, faces perhaps its biggest test of the season.\n\"Fort Hill football is Fort Hill football,\" Burnham said. \"They play physical. They play fast. They do multiple things offensively. They run the ball consistently with multiple backs. We have to contain their run, limit their explosive plays on defense.\"\nCambridge-SD also possesses big-play talent, with senior quarterback Anthony Hughes and senior running back Sterling Perkins Jr., who has gained over a 1,000 yards this season.\n\"Offensively, we need to protect the football and then be opportunistic and make plays when we can,\" Burnham said.\nThough the Sentinels have usually been at home during the past three postseasons, Alkire said his team has adapted to playing on the road after the ineligibility ruling.\n\"Just a matter of getting the kids in the mindset to be able to go on the road and endure some of the longer road trips, and experience some of the things that opposing teams have had to experience against us,\" Alkire said. \"It's been a transition but the kids have accepted it and they moved on.\"\nAnd though its date with Fort Hill has come two weeks earlier than expected, Burnham thinks the Vikings are ready.\n\"I think our kids are excited about an opportunity to prove ourself on a big stage against a quality opponent,\" Burnham said. \"In order to get where we want to be we have to beat them. To coin the phrase, 'To be the man you've got to beat the man.' They've been the man the last three years and we want to take it. So we have to win. That's how it is.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 21,
    "metadata_sport": "football",
