  {
    "title": "Wicomico defeats Easton",
    "date": "2024-12-11",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Wicomico defeats Easton\n\n December 11, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 577\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Jason Biringer has no illusion of flipping Easton High's wrestling program around overnight.\"It's growing pains,\" said Biringer, who has returned as the Warriors head coach after three seasons away from the program. \"We set small goals for each of these guys. Hey, sometimes it might be just, get through the first period for those super raw new guys. After that we just keep building a little more. Make it through your first match. Get your first win. Get another win. Small steps.\"\nBiringer saw some of his green roster take some small steps Wednesday night during a 58-24 loss to Wicomico, which won four of the five varsity matches wrestled to go along with six forfeit wins.\n\"We know we have to work,\" said Biringer, whose roster of 30 has only six wrestlers with more experience than just this season. \"And we know there's a lot to do, and a lot of ways to get better. But I'm happy with the effort they are putting in. Like I said, it's a lot to ask.\"\nWicomico had an 18-6 lead via three forfeits through the first four weight classes, then extended that cushion to 24-6, when Deion Finney pinned AJ Matulay in 1 minute, 4 seconds at 132 pounds.\nEaston (0-2) picked up its lone win not by way of forfeit at 138, when Shawn Arnold fell into a 3-0 first-period hole, scored a late escape, then pinned Ethan Brewington at 2:39, pulling the Warriors within 24-12.\n\"Shawn is a scrappy kid,\" Biringer said. \"We've been working with him. He's got a high octane motor. Getting him to be settle down and be more controlled out there, that's our focus. But he has a ton of potential. He's a second-year kid. Bright future for him if he keeps working hard.\"\nThe Indians won the other three matches that were wrestled, the tightest being at 144, where Corey Richardson decisioned Easton's Adonis Schmitt, 7-2. Wi-Hi's Adelson Theajeon pinned Anthony Melendez in 2:45 at 175, and Keven Mondestin got a 21-second fall over Matthew Brunner at 285.\n\"Those guys who wrestled, we're asking a lot of those kids,\" Biringer said. \"Wi-Hi also has experience. They have a good program. (Head coach) Nate Stone's been there for many years now and always has a solid group.\"\nWicomico's Terry Scott (106), Tavenon Outlaw (120), Davidson Fortunel (126), Rowan Okerblom (157), Cameron Wawrzenak (165) and Jamier Johnson (215) each won by forfeit.\n\"We've got a core of guys that have several years of experience, but they are all kind of lumped down in like in the lower weights,\" Stone said. \"But we have a bunch of guys we think are going to do really well. Overall I thought our conditioning was pretty good for it being this early (in the season). And we didn't look super sloppy. So fundamentally we did OK.\"\nEaston's other wins came via forfeit from Estaben Velasquez (113), Matthew Bravo (150) and Wahaab Popal (190).\n\"We're focused on each individual's production,\" said Biringer, who was also encouraged by his girls winning three of five matches, and his boys winning three of the seven junior-varsity bouts. \"Lot of youth. Almost exclusively sophomores and freshmen, and then a few juniors and two seniors.\n\"And the kids have had a good attitude,\" Biringer added. \"They're trying. They want to learn the moves. They want to get better. It takes showing up and putting in that work every day. It's a sport of experience and you have to have the long view.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 12,
    "day": 11,
    "metadata_sport": "wrestling",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Easton High School\", \"Wicomico High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"Jason Biringer\", \"Nate Stone\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Wicomico 58, Easton 24",
    "metadata_importance": 6
  },
  {
    "title": "Colonel tops Easton, evens slate",
    "date": "2024-12-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Colonel tops Easton, evens slate\n\n December 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 590\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n AMERICAN CORNER — Easton High's wrestling team is rebuilding this season.Colonel Richardson?\nColonels head coach Bryan Hall winces at the idea of a rebuild, preferring to say, \"We're filling in.\"\nColonel filled in quite nicely Wednesday night, scoring the first takedown in eight of the 10 matches wrestled en route to a 58-24 victory over Easton.\n\"It hurts 'cause we thought last year we had eight sophomore starters, three freshmen starters, and only one senior,\" Hall said. \"But we've lost four of our starters from last year. One transferred to North Caroline. One is injured. Two decided not to come out. We lost some guys we were going to be counting on as part of our season, so we're filling in.\"\nOne of those filling in for Colonel, 120-pounder Elisee Pierre-Louis, won by injury default. Pierre-Louis was taking Otto Perez to the mat, when the Easton freshman injured his arm while trying to brace his fall. Perez was taken by ambulance to have the injury treated.\n\"That was a momentum punch in the gut for the guys,\" Easton head coach Jason Biringer said of losing Perez.\nEaston (0-3) had built an early 12-0 lead on Skye Matthews' forfeit win at 106, and Esteban Velasquez' 1:59 pinfall over Brody Omohundro at 113.\nThe injury default cut Easton's lead in half, but Colonel (2-2) won the next three matches. Jayquan Wilkins crafted a 17-2 technical fall over John Seymour at 126, pulling Colonel within 12-11. Zy Nichols (132) and Evan Alexander (138) each won by forfeit, pushing the Colonels' lead to 23-12.\nThe Warriors stopped that streak when Adonis Schmidt pinned Trynton Parks in 59 seconds of the 144-pound match, drawing the visitors within 23-18.\nBut Colonel Richardson won six of the final seven matches, scoring bonus points in five of the bouts.\nAnthony Alexander (150) started Colonel's roll, pinning Matthew Bravo just as the first-period buzzer sounded. Gary Koeneman (157) followed, working a 19-4 technical fall over Vlad Oslam. Colonel's Ethan Thomas then got turned to his back, yet rolled through on the way to a 2:47 pinfall over Darren Foxwell at 165, stretching the Colonel lead to 40-18.\n\"They wrestled amazing,\" Hall said of his team. \"We've been shocked by Ethan Thomas. He's a freshman, just started wrestling last year in a middle school club. He's wrestling up a weight class and has been huge.\n\"Other guys we expected to win, won,\" Hall continued. \"Other guys we're expecting to go out there and wrestle hard, are. It's been a good season so far.\"\nTony Melendez got Easton back in the win column with a 1:07 fall over freshman Brox Meissner at 175. But the Colonels closed the match, with Richard Taylor (190) pinning Wahaab Popal in 2:15, Wilson Clough (215) winning by forfeit, and Chatydrick Pickrom (285) pinning Matthew Brunner in 2:45.\nStill, Biringer was encouraged by what he saw.\n\"We are seeing some better habits,\" Biringer said. \"We're also seeing some self-destructive moves, where it's, 'Don't make up your own moves. Do the moves you're taught.' Usually when something bad was happening they were trying something that wasn't a fundamental move. So it's beginner's mistakes, but at the same time a lot more effort, and progress is being made.\"\nColonel's girls earned 39-22 victory in what was Easton's first girls dual meet. Each team won two of the four matches wrestled.\nColonel's Luna Infante-Reyes (115) pinned Leah Gonzalez-Reyes in 3:38, and Estelle Lima (190) earned a 7-0 decision over Khadija Popal.\nEaston's wins came from Shiza Gondal (135), who pinned Lizzy Hesson, and Thanh Mai Pham (140), who earned a 10-2 major decision over Aiyanna Groce. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 12,
    "day": 18,
    "metadata_sport": "wrestling",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Easton, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Colonel Richardson\", \"Easton High School\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"head coach Bryan Hall\", \"head coach Jason Biringer\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "Colonel Richardson 58, Easton 24",
    "metadata_importance": 7
  }
]